var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#Text-entry","page":"Text entry","text":"Two widgets: TextInput for one line, TextArea for many. They share a discipline rather than a supertype — the same cursor units, the same clamps, the same grapheme rules — and they differ exactly where a line break makes them differ.","title":"Text entry"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#TextInput","page":"Text entry","text":"A single-line field with a placeholder, a submit handler and a caret:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nsubmitted = String[]\nfield = TextInput(\"\", i -> push!(submitted, i.text[]);\n                  placeholder = \"name?\", id = :name)\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, field)\nlayout!(field, Region(1, 1, 10, 1))\n\nbuf = Buffer(10, 1)\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, field)\nbuf\n\nThe placeholder shows while the text is empty, and it is painted dimmed rather than as content:\n\nhas(buf[1, 1].style, Attr.DIM)\n\nType into it and press enter:\n\nfor c in \"ada\"\n    dispatch_event!(field, key(c), field)\nend\ndispatch_event!(field, parse(KeyEvent, \"enter\"), field)\n\n(text = field.text[], cursor = field.cursor[], submitted = submitted)\n\ntext and cursor are Reactive, but with Dirty.PAINT rather than the conservative Dirty.LAYOUT default — and that is a design commitment, not an optimisation. measure is text-independent:\n\nmeasure(field, Size(30, 9))\n\nA TextInput takes the width it is offered and is always one row tall, so it never resizes to its content — that is what its horizontal scroll is for. A keystroke provably cannot move a single box, so a keystroke costs zero layout. Do not copy the PAINT choice to a widget whose size depends on its state; there it would be a correctness bug.\n\nGive it a narrow box with width: 20 if you want one.","title":"TextInput"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#TextArea","page":"Text entry","text":"A multi-line editor over a Vector{String}:\n\nnotes = TextArea(\"alpha\\nbeta\")\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, notes)\nlayout!(notes, Region(1, 1, 10, 3))\n\ninsert_text!(notes, \"X\")\ninsert_newline!(notes)\n(text = text(notes), line = notes.line, col = notes.col)\n\nmeasure returns everything it is offered:\n\nmeasure(notes, Size(30, 9))\n\nAn auto-height TextArea would be as tall as its document and would never scroll at all, so give it a height: 10 or a grow: 1 parent.\n\nIt scrolls by indexing its lines rather than by shifting an origin: render! touches scroll.y + 1 : scroll.y + height and nothing else, so painting is O(window) and a 100 000-line document costs the same frame as a ten-line one.\n\nThe document is a plain field, deliberately not a Reactive: an == guard over a Vector{String} would be an O(n) elementwise compare on every keystroke, and an in-place edit would never trip it anyway. version is the reactive cell instead — an Int compare, O(1), for the O(1) edit that actually happened. The price is worth stating plainly: mutate lines behind version's back and it will not repaint, so every edit goes through the operations below.\n\ncontent_extent is overridden to report the document rather than the (nonexistent) children, and that override is the whole of its integration with the scrolling machinery. A Scrollbar reports on a TextArea with no new code at all:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\narea = TextArea(join([\"line $i\" for i in 1:20], \"\\n\"); id = :area)\nbar = Scrollbar(area, ScrollAxis.VERTICAL; id = :bar)\nrow = Container(area, bar; id = :row)\n\napply_stylesheet!(css\"\"\"\n                  #row  { display: flex; layout: row; }\n                  #area { grow: 1; }\n                  #bar  { width: 1; shrink: 0; }\n                  \"\"\", row)\nlayout!(row, Region(1, 1, 10, 4))\n\n(content = content_extent(area), window = layout_of(area).content)\n\nscroll_to!(area, Offset(0, 8))\nbuf = Buffer(10, 4)\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, row)\nbuf\n\nScrollpane(TextArea(...)) is also legal and scrolls the area's whole box — the two mechanisms compose rather than compete. But a TextArea contains no Scrollpane, and never should: a pane scrolls children, so a 10 000-line document would become 10 000 widgets.","title":"TextArea"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#The-cursor","page":"Text entry","text":"The caret is reverse video on the cell it rests on, not a hardware cursor: the tree paints into a Buffer, and the Driver seam has no cursor-placement method at all. It appears while the widget is focused.\n\nCursor indices are 0-based and count grapheme clusters: 0 is before the first cluster and n is after the last. This is not a violation of the package's 1-based rule — that rule governs Region, Buffer and MouseEvent, and a cursor is none of those. It is a count of what lies behind the caret, which makes 0:n the natural range and both ends legal split points.\n\nWidget Cursor\nTextInput cursor[], a cluster index in 0:n\nTextArea line (1-based into lines) and col (a cluster index)\n\nTextArea also keeps a goal column, in cells, which up and down aim for and which every horizontal move re-pins. Cells are the unit the user can see; a goal measured in graphemes would land in a visually different column across wide clusters. Its absence is the single most-noticed bug in a text editor — run down through a short line and back up, and the caret comes home:\n\ng = TextArea(\"aaaaaaaa\\nbb\\ncccccccc\")\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, g)\nlayout!(g, Region(1, 1, 10, 3))\n\nmove_by!(g, 6)                              # line 1, cell column 6\ndown1 = (move_line!(g, 1), g.col)           # line 2 is only 2 wide\ndown2 = (move_line!(g, 1), g.col)           # line 3 is wide again\n(goal = g.goal, down1 = down1, down2 = down2)\n\nmove_line! lands on the cluster whose prefix width is the largest not exceeding goal, so up/down can never come to rest inside a wide cluster.","title":"The cursor"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#Editing-keys","page":"Text entry","text":"Both widgets act on the way up, on unmodified keys only, and consume what they act on.\n\nKey TextInput TextArea\na character, space insert at the caret insert at the caret\nenter calls on_submit splits the line\nbackspace delete the cluster before at column 0: join the line above\ndelete delete the cluster at at end of line: join the line below\nleft / right one cluster one cluster, wrapping across lines\nup / down — one line, aiming at goal\npageup / pagedown — one window less one row\nhome / end start / end of the text start / end of the line\ntab, escape fall through fall through\nctrl+…, alt+… fall through fall through\n\ntab and escape falling through is what keeps the tab order alive, and modified keys falling through is what stops a focused field silently shadowing your application bindings:\n\nbox = TextInput(\"x\")\n(tab = dispatch_event!(box, parse(KeyEvent, \"tab\"), box),\n letter = dispatch_event!(box, parse(KeyEvent, \"a\"), box),\n ctrl_a = dispatch_event!(box, parse(KeyEvent, \"ctrl+a\"), box),\n text = box.text[])\n\nOnly the plain letter was consumed, and only it was inserted.\n\nEvery key has a function behind it, so you never need to synthesise events to drive a field: insert_text!, insert_newline!, backspace!, delete_forward!, move_by!, move_to! (TextInput), move_line! (TextArea), plus set_text!, text and refresh_extent! on TextArea.\n\nhome and end are move_to! with the extremes of Int and let the clamp decide — \"go as far as you can\" is the implementation. In a TextArea they are per line, because that is what a caret in an editor means; a Scrollpane's document-wide home/end still reaches an ancestor pane whenever the area is not focused.\n\nTextArea keeps widest as a monotone high-water mark, raised in O(1) by every edit and lowered by nothing. The tradeoff, stated so nobody has to discover it: after you delete the longest line the horizontal range stays too wide — the thumb is slightly too small — until refresh_extent! rescans. Paying O(lines) per keystroke to save a scrollbar thumb one cell is not a trade worth making. set_text! calls it for you.","title":"Editing keys"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#Paste","page":"Text entry","text":"A PasteEvent arrives whole, from bracketed paste, and the two widgets treat it differently because their content differs.\n\nTextInput strips newlines and every other control character: a single-line field has nowhere to put a line break, and silently accepting one would make its text unrenderable.\n\none = TextInput()\ndispatch_event!(one, PasteEvent(\"one\\ntwo\"), one)\none.text[]\n\nTextArea splits it into real lines — it is exactly the widget a multi-line paste belongs in:\n\nmany = TextArea()\ndispatch_event!(many, PasteEvent(\"one\\ntwo\"), many)\n(text = text(many), lines = length(many.lines))\n\nA paste that strips to nothing still consumes: it was a paste into this field, and letting the husk bubble would be a second delivery of the same event.","title":"Paste"},{"category":"section","location":"textentry/#Grapheme-correctness","page":"Text entry","text":"This is the section that matters, because it is the thing everyone else gets wrong.\n\nA terminal grid is not a string, and a user's text is not a sequence of codepoints. Take a ZWJ family emoji — man, zero-width joiner, woman, zero-width joiner, girl:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nfam = \"👨‍👩‍👧\"\n(codepoints = length(collect(fam)),\n bytes = ncodeunits(fam),\n cells = grapheme_width(fam),\n base_textwidth = textwidth(fam))\n\nFive codepoints, eighteen bytes, two cells — and Base.textwidth says six, because it sums codepoints and knows nothing about clustering. It is forbidden throughout ManyUI's text handling for exactly that reason. (textwidth(\"❤️\") is 1, for a cluster that renders in two: the error goes both ways.)\n\nThe rule is: a cursor steps by grapheme cluster. One step, however many codepoints or cells that cluster is.\n\nw = TextInput(\"a$(fam)b\")\nmove_to!(w, 0)                       # Home\n[move_by!(w, 1) for _ in 1:4]        # four presses of `right`\n\nThree clusters — a, the whole family, b — and the fourth press saturates instead of running off the end. The family is one step, not five, and not eighteen.\n\nNow watch the same cluster occupy two cells. Put the caret on it, paint, and find the cell the caret reversed:\n\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, w)\nlayout!(w, Region(1, 1, 8, 1))\nw.focused[] = true\nmove_to!(w, 1)                       # the caret is ON the family\n\nbuf = Buffer(8, 1)\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, w)\nbuf\n\ncaret = findfirst(x -> has(buf[x, 1].style, Attr.REVERSE), 1:8)\ncell = buf[caret, 1]\n\n(column = caret,\n glyph = String(cell.content),\n cells = Int(cell.width),\n next_is_continuation = is_continuation(buf[caret + 1, 1]))\n\nOne step of the cursor, one cell of caret, two cells of grid — and the continuation cell after it is intact. The caret reverses the head of a wide cluster and leaves its continuation a continuation, so the grid never desynchronises. A wide cluster that would straddle either edge of the window is dropped whole, never halved, at the left edge as well as the right.\n\nDeleting is the same rule. One backspace over the family takes all eighteen bytes of it, not the last codepoint:\n\nmove_to!(w, 2)                       # just past the family\nbackspace!(w)\n(text = w.text[], cursor = w.cursor[])\n\nThe subtlest case is insertion, and it is why the caret is always recomputed from the new prefix and never advanced by the number of clusters inserted. Type a combining acute onto an e:\n\nv = TextInput(\"e\")\nmove_to!(v, typemax(Int))            # End -- one cluster behind the caret\ninsert_text!(v, \"́\")            # COMBINING ACUTE ACCENT\n\n(text = v.text[],\n bytes = ncodeunits(v.text[]),       # was 1\n cursor = v.cursor[],                # was 1\n cells = grapheme_width(v.text[]))\n\nThe string grew by two bytes and the cluster count did not change: the mark merged into the e. A caret advanced by \"one inserted cluster\" would now sit past the end of a string that never grew. Recomputing is the only rule that is right for every input, which is why it is the only rule used.\n\nThe same discipline runs all the way down: byte indices and cluster indices meet in exactly one place, a small set of shared helpers that TextInput defines and TextArea uses, so the two cannot drift apart — and no widget code indexes a user's string by byte.","title":"Grapheme correctness"},{"category":"section","location":"api/tables/#Table-and-DataTable","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"","title":"Table and DataTable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.Table","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"mutable struct Table{R, F, A} <: RowsWidget\n\nColumns with headers over a Vector of rows.\n\nTable OWNS its rows. A view that owns nothing cannot answer _tc_sync!, cannot reindex_* its selection, and forces a SECOND data model across Table/DataTable – which is a second paint loop or a messier seam. One data model, one paint loop, and view_source is the only delta between the two widgets. A caller with a lazy source materialises a window into a Vector themselves.\n\nTHE CELL CALLBACK IS ON THE WIDGET, NOT ON THE COLUMN, and this is the decisive type decision of the tier. A Vector{Column{T,G}} parametric on the projection is IMPOSSIBLE – each column's closure has its own type, so one G cannot serve them all – which forces Column to hold an ABSTRACT projection field and pay ONE DYNAMIC DISPATCH PER VISIBLE CELL, FOREVER. cell::F on the widget is one CONCRETE type parameter, a STATIC dispatch, the same expressive power (cell(row, j) can branch on j however it likes), and it is the Button{F} pattern this package already uses three times.\n\nrows is a PLAIN field, ALIASED, not a Reactive – List's reason, List's price, List's render!-time self-heal via _tc_sync!.\n\nversion is Dirty.PAINT-reactive – List's reason, verbatim.\n\ncell(row, j) MUST return an AbstractString, MUST be PURE and CHEAP, and MUST NOT contain a newline. It is called ONCE PER VISIBLE CELL PER FRAME – never for a culled column, never for a row outside the window, and never during _tc_auto! for a non-AUTO column.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ngrid::TableGrid\nrows::Vector\ncell::Any\nversion::Reactive{Int64}: Bumped by every data OR selection change. Dirty.PAINT.\nsel::Selection\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_activate::Any: Called as on_activate(table) on ENTER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.Table-Union{Tuple{A}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{Vector{R}, Vector{Column}}, Tuple{Vector{R}, Vector{Column}, A}} where {R, F, A}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"A table of rows under cols, calling on_activate(table) on ENTER.\n\nrows and cols are both ALIASED, never copied. Focusable by construction. Seeds every AUTO column from the headers and SOURCE rows 1 : min(sample, length(rows)), once – see _tc_auto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tb_reseed!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tb_reseed!(w::Table)\n\n\nReset every AUTO mark to its header seed and re-measure SOURCE rows 1 : min(sample, n) – the seeding pass, exactly as at construction.\n\nTHE ONE PLACE the sample is re-scanned, and it is never a data change: push_row!/insert_row! raise the marks from the NEW ROW ALONE, which is what keeps a push O(1). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tb_reseed!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tc_cell_default-Tuple{Any, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tc_cell_default(row, j::Int64) -> AbstractString\n\n\nThe default cell function: _tc_show(row[j]). Works for a Vector of Tuples, of NamedTuples and of Vectors – the three shapes a table's rows actually arrive in – and for nothing else, which is exactly what the cell keyword is for. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_cell_default"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tc_header_rows-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tc_header_rows(w::Table) -> Int64\n\n\n(show_header ? 1 : 0) + (show_header && rule ? 1 : 0). The rows of the content box that are pinned CHROME. 0, 1 or 2. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"content_extent(w::Table) -> Size\n\n\nSize(cache_total, row_count + header_rows). OVERRIDES the container default: a Table's content is DATA, not children. THIS OVERRIDE IS THE WHOLE INTEGRATION WITH Scrollbar – Scrollbar{Table{R,F,A}} works with ZERO new code in scroll.jl. See _tc_extent for why the header rows are counted and why dropping them would make the last hh rows of every table unreachable. O(1) on the FRAME PATH.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.delete_row!-Tuple{Table, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"delete_row!(w::Table, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete row i. False when out of range. REINDEXES via reindex_delete!. An AUTO column stays TOO WIDE until refresh_extent! – TextArea.widest makes exactly this trade (textarea.jl:176).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.delete_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.insert_row!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Table{R}, Int64, R}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"insert_row!(w::Table{R}, i::Int64, r)\n\n\nInsert r at i, clamped. REINDEXES the selection via reindex_insert! and raises the AUTO marks from the new row alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Table, Size}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"measure(w::Table, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\navail. A Table takes the space it is OFFERED and scrolls its content: an auto-HEIGHT table would be as tall as its data and would never scroll at all. This is also what licenses version's PAINT reactivity. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_blur!(w::Table)\n\n\nHide the cursor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Table, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_event!(w::Table, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nKeys: see _tc_key!. Consumes only when something actually moved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Table, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_event!(w::Table, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nMouse: see _tc_mouse!. A press on the header does nothing: a Table never sorts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_focus!(w::Table)\n\n\nShow the cursor, and scroll every ancestor pane until this table is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.push_row!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Table{R}, R}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"push_row!(w::Table{R}, r)\n\n\nAppend r. O(1) plus an O(cap) mark raise for that row alone – nothing rescans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.push_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_columns!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_columns!(w::Table)\n\n\nRe-seed the AUTO marks and invalidate the memo after grid_of(w).cols was written in place.\n\nRE-MEASURES THE SAMPLE, exactly as construction does, and not the headers alone. _tc_auto_reset! lowers every AUTO mark to its header seed, so a reset that stopped there would collapse every AUTO column to its own caption: changing one column's ALIGNMENT would silently resize its NEIGHBOURS. A column model rewritten is a column model measured, and the cost is the construction cost – O(min(sample, n) x AUTO columns), bounded, on an explicit call and never on a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_columns!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_extent!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_extent!(w::Table) -> Size\n\n\nReset and re-measure every AUTO mark over EVERY row, re-resolve, and return the new content_extent. O(rows x AUTO columns), each cell capped by _tc_measure.\n\nTHE OPT-IN EXACT RESCAN, and the same name and meaning as refresh_extent!(::TextArea) and refresh_extent!(::List). This is the ONLY thing that can make an AUTO column NARROWER, and it is the documented escape from the sampling rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_extent!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_rows!-Tuple{Table}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_rows!(w::Table)\n\n\nTHE single exit of every data change; also the public escape hatch for a direct mutation of rows. Bumps version (which invalidates the resolve memo), re-syncs the selection, re-clamps the scroll, follows the cursor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.set_columns!-Tuple{Table, Vector{Column}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"set_columns!(w::Table, cols::Vector{Column})\n\n\nReplace the columns. Resizes widths/xs/autos, re-seeds the AUTO marks and invalidates the memo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_columns!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.set_rows!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Table{R}, AbstractVector}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"set_rows!(w::Table{R}, rows::AbstractVector)\n\n\nReplace the contents. CLEARS the selection and cursor, rewinds the scroll and RE-SEEDS the AUTO marks: every index the selection held names a row that may no longer exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_rows!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.DataTable","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"mutable struct DataTable{R, F, K, A} <: RowsWidget\n\nA Table plus a SORT: the same data, the same columns, the same paint loop, viewed through an index PERMUTATION.\n\nWHAT IT ADDS OVER Table, EXHAUSTIVELY:\n\nkey::K                 `key(row, j)` -> something `isless` accepts\norder::Vector{Int}     view -> source. THE sort.\nrank::Vector{Int}      source -> view. The inverse.\nsort_col::Int          `0` == source order\nsort_dir::SortDir.T\na sort indicator in the header, and a header click that sorts.\n\nEverything else – the columns, the sizing, the header, the truncation, the selection, the navigation, the scrolling, the paint – is tablecore's, reached through tcrender_table! and the seam.\n\nWHY TWO TYPES AND NOT ONE WITH A NULLABLE order: because view_source is the ONLY behavioural difference, and it is k for one and order[k] for the other. One type would branch on a nullable field ONCE PER VISIBLE ROW PER FRAME, forever, on every table in every app, to serve the ones that never sort. Two types make the compiler take that branch ONCE, at specialisation time, and a Table that never sorts pays neither the branch nor the 2n Ints – 1.6 MB on 100 000 rows – that order and rank cost. That is not a new argument in this codebase, it is the house one (label.jl:98, Scrollbar{V}).\n\nTWO CALLBACKS, cell AND key, and the separation is the whole point:\n\ncell(row, j)::AbstractString  -- what the user SEES\nkey(row, j)                   -- what the sort COMPARES\n\nSorting a numeric column by its RENDERED string puts \"10\" before \"9\". That is a bug in every table library that conflates the two.\n\nkey IS A REQUIRED KEYWORD. It has NO default, and in particular it does NOT default to cell. A default that silently does the wrong thing is an honest confession of a dishonest default; a sort_by! that silently no-ops is a quiet nothing on the one call whose entire purpose is to change something. A required keyword makes the decision UNAVOIDABLE AT CONSTRUCTION and unmissable in review. A DataTable exists to sort; one without a sort key is a Table.\n\nkey(row, j) must return values mutually isless-comparable WITHIN a column. Across columns it may return anything: the sort closes over a FIXED j, so every comparison in a given sort_by! is homogeneous. A column of MIXED types THROWS a MethodError from isless – it is not \"merely slow\".\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ngrid::TableGrid\nrows::Vector\ncell::Any\nkey::Any\norder::Vector{Int64}\nrank::Vector{Int64}\nsort_col::Int64: The sorted column, or 0 for source order.\nsort_dir::ManyUI.SortDir.T: The sort direction. NONE iff sort_col == 0.\nversion::Reactive{Int64}: Bumped by every data OR selection change. Dirty.PAINT.\nsel::Selection\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_activate::Any: Called as on_activate(table) on ENTER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DataTable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.DataTable-Union{Tuple{A}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{Vector{R}, Vector{Column}}, Tuple{Vector{R}, Vector{Column}, A}} where {R, F, K, A}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"A sortable table of rows under cols, calling on_activate(table) on ENTER. Starts in SOURCE order.\n\nkey is REQUIRED and has no default: see the type's docstring. rows and cols are both ALIASED, never copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DataTable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_fit!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_fit!(w::DataTable) -> Bool\n\n\nResize order/rank to length(rows). True iff either was resized – in which case the grown slots are UNDEFINED and every caller must write order whole before _dt_rank! reads it. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_fit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_identity!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_identity!(w::DataTable) -> Bool\n\n\nRestore SOURCE order: order[k] = k. True iff any entry moved. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_identity!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_on_header-Tuple{DataTable, MouseEvent}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_on_header(w::DataTable, e::MouseEvent) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when e points at the pinned header rows of w – the caption row and the rule under it, which is exactly the block _tc_header_rows counts and exactly the block _tc_row_at refuses to call a body row.\n\n_tc_local, NEVER local_offset: inside a Scrollpane scrolled to y = 3 the unshifted border box would call a BODY row a header and sort on a click that meant to select. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_on_header"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_order!-Tuple{DataTable, AbstractVector{Int64}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_order!(w::DataTable, p::AbstractVector{Int64}) -> Bool\n\n\nWrite p into order. True iff any entry moved. p is a permutation of 1:length(rows) – sortperm's answer, or the identity. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_order!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_pad-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_pad(n::Int64) -> String\n\n\nn spaces; the empty string at or below zero. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_pad"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_perm-Tuple{DataTable, Int64, ManyUI.SortDir.T}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_perm(\n    w::DataTable,\n    j::Int64,\n    dir::ManyUI.SortDir.T\n) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nThe permutation column j in direction dir puts rows in, WITHOUT touching rows. See sort_by! for why the keys are materialised first and why MergeSort is spelled. O(n) keys + O(n log n). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_perm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_rank!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_rank!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nRebuild rank from order in ONE pass: rank[order[k]] = k. O(n), off the frame path. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_rank!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_reapply!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_reapply!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nRebuild order/rank from the CURRENT sort state: the identity when sort_col == 0, the permutation otherwise. THE reapply, and the whole of what a data change owes the sort. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_reapply!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._dt_reseed!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_dt_reseed!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nReset every AUTO mark to its header seed and re-measure SOURCE rows 1 : min(sample, n) – the seeding pass, exactly as at construction.\n\nTHE ONE PLACE the sample is re-scanned, and it is never a data change. Mirrors _tb_reseed!(::Table). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dt_reseed!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tc_header_reserve-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tc_header_reserve(w::DataTable, j::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\n1 for a sortable column: the indicator needs a cell to live in, and an AUTO column sized to its header text alone would have nowhere to draw it. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_reserve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tc_header_rows-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tc_header_rows(w::DataTable) -> Int64\n\n\n(show_header ? 1 : 0) + (show_header && rule ? 1 : 0). The rows of the content box that are pinned CHROME. 0, 1 or 2. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI._tc_header_text-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"_tc_header_text(w::DataTable, j::Int64) -> String\n\n\nThe caption, and the sort indicator when this is the sorted column.\n\nTHE INDICATOR IS PAINTED IN THE COLUMN'S LAST CELL BY _tc_put!, not APPENDED to the caption. Appending is the obvious spelling and is wrong twice: a narrow column would TRUNCATE THE INDICATOR AWAY – so the one column whose state you must see is the one that hides it – and the column's AUTO width would depend on its sort state, so CLICKING A HEADER WOULD RESIZE IT.\n\nTHE GUTTER IS RESERVED IN EVERY SORTABLE COLUMN, ALWAYS, and left blank when unsorted (_tc_header_reserve). Reserving it only when sorted would change the caption's width the moment you sort, so the caption would re-truncate and the header would twitch. ScrollMode.AUTO's doctrine, quoted: THE GUTTER IS STABLE, THE INK IS NOT. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_text"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"content_extent(w::DataTable) -> Size\n\n\nSize(cache_total, view_count + header_rows). OVERRIDES the container default; THIS OVERRIDE IS THE WHOLE INTEGRATION WITH Scrollbar – Scrollbar{DataTable{R,F,K,A}} works with ZERO new code in scroll.jl. O(1) on the FRAME PATH.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.delete_row!-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"delete_row!(w::DataTable, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete SOURCE row i. False when out of range. REINDEXES via reindex_delete! and rebuilds order/rank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.delete_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.insert_row!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{DataTable{R}, Int64, R}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"insert_row!(w::DataTable{R}, i::Int64, r)\n\n\nInsert r at SOURCE index i, clamped. REINDEXES the selection via reindex_insert!, then REAPPLIES the current sort.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{DataTable, Size}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"measure(w::DataTable, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\navail. A DataTable takes the space it is OFFERED and scrolls its content. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_blur!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nHide the cursor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{DataTable, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_event!(w::DataTable, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nKeys: see _tc_key!. Consumes only when something actually moved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{DataTable, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_event!(w::DataTable, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nA LEFT PRESS on the HEADER rows sorts the column under the pointer; everything else is _tc_mouse!. That one branch is DataTable's entire mouse delta. A press on a non-sortable column consumes nothing and does nothing.\n\nUses grid_of(w).xs/widths – LAST frame's – which is exactly the header the user saw and clicked. Before the first paint they are zeros and a click does nothing, which is correct: there was no header to click.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"on_focus!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nShow the cursor, and scroll every ancestor pane until this table is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.push_row!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{DataTable{R}, R}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"push_row!(w::DataTable{R}, r)\n\n\nAppend r. Raises the AUTO marks from the new row alone, then REAPPLIES the current sort.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.push_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_columns!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_columns!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nRe-seed the AUTO marks and invalidate the memo after grid_of(w).cols was written in place.\n\nRE-MEASURES THE SAMPLE, exactly as construction does, and not the headers alone. _tc_auto_reset! LOWERS every AUTO mark to its header seed, so a reset that stopped there would collapse every AUTO column to its own caption: re-speccing ONE column's alignment would silently resize its NEIGHBOURS. A column model rewritten is a column model measured, and the cost is the construction cost – O(min(sample, n) x AUTO columns), bounded, on an explicit call and never on a frame.\n\nThe Table twin's refresh_columns! states the same rule, and this is what DataTable itself does at construction and at set_rows!. No data-change path (push_row!/insert_row!/delete_row!) rescans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_columns!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_extent!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_extent!(w::DataTable) -> Size\n\n\nReset and re-measure every AUTO mark over EVERY row, re-resolve, and return the new content_extent. THE OPT-IN EXACT RESCAN, and the ONLY thing that can make an AUTO column NARROWER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_extent!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.refresh_rows!-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"refresh_rows!(w::DataTable)\n\n\nTHE single exit. Rebuilds order/rank to 1:n, REAPPLIES the current sort when sort_col != 0, bumps version, re-syncs the selection, re-clamps the scroll, follows the cursor. O(n log n) when sorted – a data change, never a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.set_columns!-Tuple{DataTable, Vector{Column}}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"set_columns!(w::DataTable, cols::Vector{Column})\n\n\nReplace the columns. Resizes widths/xs/autos, re-seeds the AUTO marks and invalidates the memo. Resets the sort when sort_col is no longer a valid column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_columns!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.set_rows!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{DataTable{R}, AbstractVector}} where R","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"set_rows!(w::DataTable{R}, rows::AbstractVector)\n\n\nReplace the contents. CLEARS the selection and cursor, rewinds the scroll, re-seeds the AUTO marks and rebuilds order/rank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.sort_by!-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"sort_by!(w::DataTable, j::Int64; dir) -> Bool\n\n\nSort the VIEW by column j in direction dir. j == 0 or dir === SortDir.NONE restores SOURCE order. Returns true iff the order changed.\n\nTHE ORDER, PRECISELY: the view order OR the sorted column OR the direction – \"something actually changed\", which is the convention every mutator in this family follows (set_scroll!, widget.jl:204). The three are ONE fact and the wider reading is the load-bearing one: a column whose keys are ALL EQUAL flips ASCENDING to DESCENDING without moving a single row, and a false there would skip the PAINT bump and leave the header showing an arrow that contradicts sort_direction(w). The indicator is state, not order.\n\nTHROWS ArgumentError when 1 <= j <= ncols and !cols[j].sortable, and BoundsError on a j outside 0:ncols. It does NOT silently no-op: a quiet nothing on the one call whose whole purpose is to change something is the worst available failure.\n\nDOES NOT MUTATE THE CALLER'S DATA, and the mechanism IS the whole answer: sortperm READS rows and returns a PERMUTATION. rows is never touched, never permuted, never copied. Sorting a COPY costs O(n) memory and O(n) moves of R and severs identity; sorting IN PLACE mutates a Vector the caller handed us and may still be using. The permutation costs 8n bytes, moves Ints, and view_source's one array load is the entire read side.\n\nTHE ALGORITHM IS SPELLED, NOT DEFAULTED:\n\nks = [w.key(w.rows[i], j) for i in 1:n]\np  = sortperm(ks; alg = MergeSort, rev = (dir === DESCENDING))\n\nMATERIALIZING ks FIRST IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT IS THE ALGORITHM. sort!(order; by = i -> key(rows[i], j)) runs by TWICE PER COMPARISON, because Base.Order.By has no Schwartzian transform. MEASURED: 17824 by calls at n=2000 versus 2000 for this spelling – ~9x. The comprehension also INFERS its eltype, so a numeric column sorts a concrete Vector{Float64}.\nalg = MergeSort IS SPELLED even though sortperm's default is already stable. Stability is a CONTRACT here, and a contract you get from a default is a contract you will lose.\nrev = true REVERSES THE ORDERING, NOT THE OUTPUT, so ties keep their SOURCE order in BOTH directions: sortperm([2,1,2,1,2]; rev=true, alg=MergeSort) == [1,3,5,2,4], NOT [5,3,1,4,2]. That is what \"stable\" has to mean for a user who sorts by Department then by Name and expects the Names still in order within a Department.\norder is RESET to 1:n before every sort, so sort_by!(w, 2, ASCENDING) gives the same answer whatever the history. THE PRICE: no multi-key sort by chaining. That feature is not lost, it is RELOCATED to where it costs nothing – key = (r, j) -> (r.dept, r.name) IS the multi-key sort, it is one line of user code, and unlike chaining it says what it means.\n\nTHE SELECTION DOES NOT MOVE, AND THERE IS NO CODE HERE THAT MOVES IT. Selection stores SOURCE indices, so a sort – a claim about ORDER – cannot touch a selection – a claim about ROWS. That is the entire reason the index space is source-based, and it is why this function has no remap! to get wrong. The cursor does not change ROW; it changes SCREEN POSITION, because view_rank changed. _tc_follow_cursor! then runs, so THE USER'S ROW STAYS UNDER THEIR EYES across the sort.\n\nTHE COLUMN WIDTHS DO NOT CHANGE: _tc_auto! samples SOURCE rows 1:sample, never view rows, so a width that moved under a sort is impossible by construction.\n\nO(n) key materialisation + O(n log n) sortperm + O(n) _dt_rank!. ALL OF IT ON A USER ACTION, once per header click. The frame path is untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.sort_by!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.sort_column-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"sort_column(w::DataTable) -> Int64\n\n\nThe sorted column, or 0 for source order. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.sort_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.sort_direction-Tuple{DataTable}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"sort_direction(w::DataTable) -> ManyUI.SortDir.T\n\n\nThe sort direction. NONE iff sort_column(w) == 0. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.sort_direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.sort_indicator-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"sort_indicator(w::DataTable, j::Int64) -> String\n\n\nTC_SORT_ASC, TC_SORT_DESC, or \"\" when column j is not the sorted one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.sort_indicator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.source_index-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"source_index(w::DataTable, k::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe caller's row index behind VIEW row k. The map a selection needs – and selected_rows(w) is ALREADY in source indices, so this is for reading the view, not for repairing it. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.source_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tables/#ManyUI.toggle_sort!-Tuple{DataTable, Int64}","page":"Table and DataTable","text":"toggle_sort!(w::DataTable, j::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nCycle column j: ASCENDING -> DESCENDING -> ASCENDING. What a header CLICK does.\n\nThere is NO click path to SortDir.NONE: a third state that looks identical to \"sorted by whatever it was before\" is a state a user cannot see and therefore cannot want. sort_by!(w, 0) restores source order for a caller who means it. Clicking a NEW column sorts ASCENDING.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle_sort!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/scrolling/#Scrolling-widgets","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"","title":"Scrolling widgets"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.SB_THUMB","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"Thumb glyph. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted in the suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.SB_THUMB"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.SB_TRACK_H","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"Track glyph. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted in the suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.SB_TRACK_H"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.SB_TRACK_V","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"Track glyph. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted in the suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.SB_TRACK_V"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.Scrollbar","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"mutable struct Scrollbar{V<:Widget} <: Widget\n\nThe visible scroll indicator for one axis of viewport.\n\nPARAMETRIC on the viewport type – the Button{F} pattern – so viewport is a CONCRETE field and content_extent/layout_of dispatch statically. This is also what lets ONE Scrollbar serve a Scrollpane's Container canvas AND a TextArea, which scrolls data rather than children: the scrollable seam is three functions, not a type.\n\nA SIBLING of the canvas, never a child of it: the core shifts only a node's children, so a bar is never inside a scrolled subtree and local_offset on it is always honest.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\naxis::ManyUI.ScrollAxis.T\nmode::ManyUI.ScrollMode.T\nviewport::Widget\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Scrollbar"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.Scrollbar-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{V, ManyUI.ScrollAxis.T}} where V<:Widget","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"A bar reporting on vp along axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Scrollbar"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.Scrollpane","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"Scrollpane(; ...) -> Scrollpane\nScrollpane(\n    child::Union{Nothing, Widget};\n    bar_y,\n    bar_x,\n    wheel_step,\n    wheel_step_x,\n    focusable,\n    id,\n    classes\n) -> Scrollpane\n\n\nA pane scrolling child, or an empty one.\n\nThe node geometry is:\n\nScrollpane                     display FLEX, direction COLUMN\n  +-- row                      FLEX ROW, grow 1\n  |     +-- canvas             grow 1, overflow SCROLL  <-- THE WINDOW\n  |     |     +-- holder       shrink 0, grow 0         <-- THE CONTENT\n  |     |           +-- child\n  |     +-- vbar               width 1        [iff bar_y !== NEVER]\n  +-- hbar                     height 1       [iff bar_x !== NEVER]\n\nTwo of those lines carry the whole design and neither is negotiable:\n\nshrink = 0f0 on the HOLDER is what creates the overflow. The default shrink is 1f0, so without it the flex kernel absorbs the overflow and squeezes the content down to the window – an 8-row document in a 3-row window collapses to 3 rows and there is nothing left to scroll.\nalign = Align.START on the canvas is what stops the holder's WIDTH being stretched to the window. Align.STRETCH, the default, would pin the content's width to the window's and horizontal scrolling could never happen at all.\n\nThe canvas is the WINDOW and the holder is the CONTENT, deliberately two nodes: one box cannot be both 3 rows tall (what you see) and 8 rows tall (what there is), and the scrollable seam reads the first from layout_of(canvas).content and the second from content_extent(canvas).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Scrollpane"},{"category":"type","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.Scrollpane-2","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"mutable struct Scrollpane <: Widget\n\nA scrolling viewport over exactly ONE child.\n\nHolds no offset of its own: the offset is node(w.canvas).scroll, the core field, so a wheel tick is one Dirty.PAINT mark and layout never runs. Scrollpane is therefore policy over a compositor that already knows how to shift an origin.\n\nFocusable by default, so a pane with no focusable children is still keyboard-scrollable.\n\nONE child: wrap several in a Container, exactly as CSS makes you.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nrow::Container\ncanvas::Container\nholder::Container\nbar_y::ManyUI.ScrollMode.T\nbar_x::ManyUI.ScrollMode.T\nwheel_step::Int64\nwheel_step_x::Int64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Scrollpane"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sb_metrics-Tuple{Scrollbar, Int64}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sb_metrics(w::Scrollbar, track::Int64) -> NTuple{4, Int64}\n\n\n(track, view, total, off) for w, read from the scrollable seam and nothing else.\n\nIt never measures anything and never touches the pane, which is why one Scrollbar serves a bare Container, a Scrollpane's canvas and a TextArea identically. off is clamped FOR DISPLAY only: the stored offset may legitimately be stale for one frame, and a thumb off the end of its track is worse than a thumb that led by one frame. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sb_metrics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sb_offset-NTuple{5, Int64}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sb_offset(\n    start::Int64,\n    len::Int64,\n    track::Int64,\n    view::Int64,\n    total::Int64\n) -> Int64\n\n\nThe scroll offset a thumb starting at start represents: the inverse of thumb_span's start mapping. Pure. Internal.\n\nExact at both ends by construction, because it inverts the same room/span ratio rather than deriving an independent one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sb_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._scroll_between-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_scroll_between(\n    vp::Widget,\n    w::Widget\n) -> Union{Nothing, Offset}\n\n\nThe shift w has ALREADY been given by scrolling nodes strictly between it and vp, exclusive of vp itself.\n\nORIGIN when vp is w's parent, and nothing when vp is not an ancestor of w at all – which is how scroll_into_view! tells \"already in the right place\" from \"not my descendant\" without a second walk. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._scroll_between"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sp_acts-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sp_acts(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target.\n\nCAPTURE is excluded deliberately: acting there would let an OUTER pane steal the wheel from the inner pane the pointer is actually over. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sp_acts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sp_box!-Tuple{Widget, BoxPatch}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sp_box!(w::Widget, p::BoxPatch)\n\n\nGive w the geometry p, so that it holds under BOTH layout paths.\n\ninline_box and box are set together ON PURPOSE, and neither alone is enough. layout! reads box and never applies inline_box, so a test that skips the cascade would see BOX_DEFAULT; and apply_stylesheet! OVERWRITES box with apply(BOX_DEFAULT, ..., inline_box), so a box set at construction is gone the first time anything cascades. Writing box as exactly the value an empty cascade would compute makes the two paths agree by construction rather than by luck.\n\nmerge rather than assignment: the patch accumulates, so a later call cannot silently drop the geometry an earlier one established.\n\nInternal: this is the pane's own machinery, never a user knob.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sp_box!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sp_is_content-Tuple{Scrollpane, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sp_is_content(p::Scrollpane, d::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when d is a strict descendant of p's HOLDER, i.e. genuinely the user's content rather than the pane's own machinery. Pure. Internal.\n\nrow, canvas, the bars and the holder itself all answer false, which is what stops reveal! on a focused scrollbar rewinding the pane to the top.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sp_is_content"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sp_key_delta-Tuple{Scrollpane, ManyUI.Key.T}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sp_key_delta(\n    w::Scrollpane,\n    code::ManyUI.Key.T\n) -> Union{Nothing, Offset}\n\n\nThe distance code scrolls w, or nothing when the key is not one of the pane's. Pure with respect to the tree. Internal.\n\nHOME/END are a HUGE delta rather than a computed target, and letting scroll_to! clamp it IS the implementation: \"go as far as you can\" needs no knowledge of how far that is. typemax(Int) ÷ 2 so that scroll_of(w) + d cannot overflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sp_key_delta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI._sp_move!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Dispatch, Offset}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"_sp_move!(w::Scrollpane, disp::Dispatch, d::Offset)\n\n\nMove w's canvas by d and consume disp IFF the offset actually changed.\n\nThe whole of scroll chaining is this one rule: a pane at its limit does not consume, so the event bubbles to the next pane out and that pane takes over. No pane knows another exists. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._sp_move!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{Scrollpane}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"content_extent(p::Scrollpane) -> Size\n\n\ncontent_extent(viewport(p)): what p shows, not the shell around it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"content_extent(w::Widget) -> Size\n\n\nThe scrollable content extent of w, in cells, measured from its CONTENT-BOX ORIGIN.\n\nDefault: the bounding box of the laid-out children's MARGIN boxes, straight from the LayoutMap the engine already computed – one pass over the children, no re-measure, no relayout. That is why a wheel tick is free.\n\nReads layout_of(k), the UNSHIFTED absolute box, so the extent is independent of the current scroll offset and a scroll can never feed back into the extent.\n\nA widget whose content is DATA rather than children overrides this; TextArea is the worked example. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.max_scroll-Tuple{Scrollpane}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"max_scroll(p::Scrollpane) -> Offset\n\n\nmax_scroll(viewport(p)): how far p can actually scroll.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.max_scroll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.max_scroll-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"max_scroll(w::Widget) -> Offset\n\n\nThe largest in-range scroll offset: content_extent - content box, clamped at zero per axis, via clamp_scroll. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.max_scroll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Scrollbar, Size}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"measure(w::Scrollbar, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nSize(1, 0) vertical, Size(0, 1) horizontal: a bar is one cell thick and claims nothing on its long axis. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.mount!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"mount!(p::Scrollpane, c::Widget) -> Scrollpane\n\n\nMount c INTO the canvas, not onto the pane: children(pane) is the pane's own machinery and the user never addresses it. Throws ArgumentError when the canvas already holds a child.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Scrollbar, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Scrollbar, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nJump the viewport so the thumb centres on the pointer; drag continues it. LEFT button only.\n\nlocal_offset(d) is measured from the bar's UNSHIFTED border box, which is correct here precisely because a scrollbar is never inside a scrolled subtree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Scrollpane, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nUP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT by wheel_step; PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN by one viewport LESS ONE ROW of overlap, so the reader keeps a landmark; HOME/END to the extremes of the vertical axis. Unmodified keys only, BUBBLE/AT_TARGET only, and consuming only on real movement.\n\nA focused TextInput consumes LEFT/RIGHT AT TARGET, so the pane never sees them. That precedence costs zero special-casing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Scrollpane, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nScroll on the wheel; shift swaps the axis.\n\nWheel events route to whatever is under the pointer and reach the pane on the way UP, so the INNERMOST pane gets first refusal. CONSUMES ONLY WHEN IT ACTUALLY MOVED: a pane at its limit lets the notch bubble to the next pane out – scroll chaining, out of the phase rule alone.\n\nCAPTURE is excluded deliberately: acting there would let an outer pane steal the wheel from the inner pane the pointer is over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.reveal_child!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"reveal_child!(w::Scrollpane, d::Widget)\n\n\nBring descendant d into the pane's window. Overrides the widget.jl hook; a no-op for the pane's own machinery (row, canvas, the bars).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.reveal_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_by!-Tuple{Widget, Offset}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_by!(w::Widget, d::Offset) -> Offset\n\n\nscroll_to!(w, scroll_of(w) + d). Returns the stored offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_by!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_into_view!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_into_view!(p::Scrollpane, w::Widget) -> Offset\n\n\nscroll_into_view!(viewport(p), w): scroll p the minimum needed to reveal descendant w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_into_view!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_into_view!-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_into_view!(vp::Widget, w::Widget) -> Offset\n\n\nScroll vp the MINIMUM needed to bring descendant w's margin box inside vp's content box, on both axes, via scroll_into_view. Returns the stored offset.\n\nReads layout_of, i.e. UNSHIFTED geometry, so it is correct whatever the current offset is, and idempotent. A no-op when w is not a descendant of vp.\n\nPanes BETWEEN vp and w have already moved w and their shift is subtracted here, which is why reveal! must run NEAREST FIRST: an inner pane has to finish moving before vp can measure where w ended up.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_into_view!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_of-Tuple{Scrollpane}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_of(p::Scrollpane) -> Offset\n\n\nscroll_of(viewport(p)): where p is currently scrolled to.\n\nThis is also what makes the generic scroll_by!(p, d) – scroll_to!(p, scroll_of(p) + d) – accumulate against the canvas's real offset instead of the pane's permanent zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_to!-Tuple{Scrollpane, Offset}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_to!(p::Scrollpane, o::Offset) -> Offset\n\n\nscroll_to!(viewport(p), o). Returns the offset actually stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_to!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.scroll_to!-Tuple{Widget, Offset}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"scroll_to!(w::Widget, o::Offset) -> Offset\n\n\nScroll w to o, CLAMPED per axis to 0:max_scroll(w). Returns the offset ACTUALLY STORED.\n\nReturning the stored offset is what makes scroll chaining fall out for free: a caller detects \"this pane is at its limit\" with scroll_to!(w, o) === before and simply does not consume the event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_to!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.thumb_span-NTuple{4, Int64}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"thumb_span(\n    track::Int64,\n    view::Int64,\n    total::Int64,\n    off::Int64\n) -> Tuple{Int64, Int64}\n\n\nThumb geometry on a track-cell track, for a view-cell window over total cells of content scrolled to off.\n\nReturns (start, len), 1-based inclusive within the track, or (0, 0) when there is no track or nothing to scroll.\n\nNORMATIVE:\n\nlen = clamp(round(Int, track * view / total), 1, track). NEVER zero: an invisible thumb is a broken scrollbar, and a 1-cell thumb on a 40-cell track is the honest rendering of a 40x document.\nstart maps 0:(total - view) onto 1:(track - len + 1), so off == 0 pins the thumb to the FIRST cell and off == total - view pins it to the LAST (start + len - 1 == track) EXACTLY. The two ends are the only positions a user can verify at a glance, so they are the two the arithmetic is written around.\n\nPure: four Ints, no widget, no layout, no buffer – the whole of this widget's behaviour is one table test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.thumb_span"},{"category":"method","location":"api/scrolling/#ManyUI.viewport-Tuple{Scrollpane}","page":"Scrolling widgets","text":"viewport(p::Scrollpane) -> Container\n\n\nThe scrolling node of p – what scroll_to!, max_scroll and content_extent take. p.canvas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.viewport"},{"category":"section","location":"api/widgets/#Built-in-widgets","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"","title":"Built-in widgets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/widgets/#Toggles","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"","title":"Toggles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/widgets/#Tabs","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"","title":"Tabs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/widgets/#Tree","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"","title":"Tree"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Label","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Label <: Widget\n\nWrapping text. measure wraps to avail.width via wrap_width.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ntext::Reactive{String}: The text; writing it marks the label dirty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Label"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Label-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Label(text::AbstractString; id, classes) -> Label\n\n\nA label showing text.\n\ntext is Dirty.LAYOUT-reactive: new text wraps differently, so it can change the label's extent and therefore its siblings' positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Label"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Static","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Static <: Widget\n\nNon-wrapping, single-line text; no wrap cost in measure.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ntext::Reactive{String}: The text; writing it marks the widget dirty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Static"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Static-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Static(text::AbstractString; id, classes) -> Static\n\n\nA single-line label showing text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Static"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Label, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::Label, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe wrapped extent of the text at avail.width: the widest wrapped line by the number of lines. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\nSize(0, 0) when there is no text or no width – a label that cannot be laid out is empty, not an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Static, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::Static, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nSize(text_width(text), 1), independent of avail. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\nA Static never wraps, so it never pays the allocation wrap_width costs Label on every measure – which is the whole reason the two types are separate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Container","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Container <: Widget\n\nA bare grouping widget: no content of its own, all behaviour from its BoxStyle and its children.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Container"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Container-Tuple{Vararg{Widget}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Container(children::Widget...; kwargs...) -> Container\n\n\nA container with children already mounted, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Container"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Container-Tuple{}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Container(; id, classes) -> Container\n\n\nAn empty container.\n\nIt defines neither measure nor render!, and that is the point: the defaults – the union of the children's outer measures, and a no-op paint – are already exactly right for a widget whose whole job is to hold other widgets. Direction, gap, padding, border and background all come from its BoxStyle, which is the cascade's business.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Container"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Button","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Button{F} <: Widget\n\nA pressable button. Parametric on the handler, so on_press is a CONCRETE field and never a boxed closure.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nlabel::Reactive{String}: The caption; writing it marks the button dirty.\npressed::Reactive{Bool}: True while the button is held.\non_press::Any: Called as on_press(button) when activated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Button"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Button-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractString, F}} where F","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A button captioned label that calls on_press(button) when activated.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring.\n\nlabel is Dirty.LAYOUT-reactive (a new caption is a new extent); pressed is Dirty.PAINT-reactive (it cannot move a single cell).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Button"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._bt_activates-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_bt_activates(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target – everywhere except the capture phase.\n\nA button activates on the way UP: capture belongs to ancestors that want to intercept, and a button is never an interceptor. AT_TARGET counts because a childless button IS the target, and the bubble phase excludes the target, so bubble alone would never visit it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._bt_activates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._bt_fire!-Tuple{Button, Dispatch}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_bt_fire!(w::Button, d::Dispatch)\n\n\nFire the handler and stop the event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._bt_fire!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Button, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::Button, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe caption's extent, Size(text_width(label), 1). Pure with respect to the tree.\n\nThis is a CONTENT extent, like every other measure. The box overhead – margin, border, padding – is added by the layout engine through outer_size; adding it here as well would count it twice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Button, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Button, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nActivate on ENTER or SPACE: calls w.on_press(w) and consume!(d).\n\nUnmodified keys only. ctrl+enter and friends belong to an application-level binding, and consuming them here would silently shadow it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Button, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Button, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nActivate on a LEFT press: calls w.on_press(w) and consume!(d).\n\nThe matching LEFT release clears pressed – that is what makes \"True while the button is held\" true – but activates nothing and consumes nothing: the press already did the work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.OVERLAY_MESSAGE","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"The default headline. Shared by the widget and by the tree-free fallback, so the two X2 paths cannot drift apart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.OVERLAY_MESSAGE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.OVERLAY_MIN_SIZE","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"X2. The smallest area in which MinSizeOverlay can lay out. Below this, render_min_size_overlay! is used instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.OVERLAY_MIN_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.OVERLAY_TINY_MESSAGE","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"The last thing worth saying when there is no room left to say the headline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.OVERLAY_TINY_MESSAGE"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.MinSizeOverlay","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct MinSizeOverlay <: Widget\n\nX2. The \"Increase Terminal Size\" fallback, as an ordinary widget.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nrequired::Reactive{Size}: The minimum the app needs.\nactual::Reactive{Size}: What the target currently offers.\nmessage::Reactive{String}: The headline message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.MinSizeOverlay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.MinSizeOverlay-Tuple{}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"MinSizeOverlay(; message, id) -> MinSizeOverlay\n\n\nA fresh overlay. Its id defaults to :_min_size_overlay so a stylesheet can target it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.MinSizeOverlay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._ov_dims-Tuple{Size, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_ov_dims(required::Size, actual::Size) -> String\n\n\nThe dimensions line: what the app needs against what it has. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ov_dims"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{MinSizeOverlay, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::MinSizeOverlay, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe extent of the message plus the dimensions line: the wider of the two, by two rows. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.should_suspend-Tuple{Size, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"should_suspend(actual::Size, min::Size) -> Bool\n\n\nX2. PURE predicate, so the threshold is testable with no App:\n\nshould_suspend(Size(12, 3), Size(20, 5)) === true\nshould_suspend(Size(80, 24), Size(20, 5)) === false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.should_suspend"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_CHECKED","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Checked glyph. Width CB_WIDTH BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_CHECKED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_FOCUS","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"The focused control: what SPACE will act on. TC_CURSOR's meaning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_FOCUS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_GAP","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Cells between the glyph and the caption.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_GAP"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_MIXED","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Mixed glyph. Width CB_WIDTH BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_MIXED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_UNCHECKED","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Unchecked glyph. Width CB_WIDTH BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_UNCHECKED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.CB_WIDTH","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Cells every glyph in this file occupies.\n\nALL FIVE GLYPHS ARE THE SAME WIDTH AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness: it is what makes measure independent of state, which is what LICENSES state's Dirty.PAINT reactivity – the exact criterion textinput.jl:158 states and list.jl:43 cites. A width-1 check against a width-3 [ ] would make a click on a checkbox a LAYOUT pass, and the suite asserts the widths so nobody discovers that by measuring frames. ASCII, not [check]/(dot): a checkbox that renders as a tofu box is worse than one that renders as an x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CB_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.RB_OFF","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Unselected radio. Width CB_WIDTH BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RB_OFF"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.RB_ON","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Selected radio. Width CB_WIDTH BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RB_ON"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Checkbox","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Checkbox{F} <: Widget\n\nA toggleable box with a caption. Parametric on the handler, so on_change is a CONCRETE field and never a boxed closure.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nlabel::Reactive{String}: The caption. LAYOUT-reactive: a new caption is a new extent.\nstate::Reactive{ManyUI.CheckState.T}: UNCHECKED, CHECKED or MIXED. PAINT-reactive; see CB_WIDTH.\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_change::Any: Called as on_change(checkbox) after a REAL change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Checkbox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Checkbox-Union{Tuple{AbstractString}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractString, F}} where F","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A checkbox captioned label that calls on_change(checkbox) after a real state change.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring. The label/state split is Button's exactly (button.jl:30-31): label is Dirty.LAYOUT-reactive (a new caption is a new extent), state is Dirty.PAINT-reactive (every glyph is CB_WIDTH wide, so it cannot move a cell).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Checkbox"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.RadioGroup","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct RadioGroup{F} <: Widget\n\nA one-of-N chooser. ONE widget, N rows, ZERO child widgets. Parametric on the handler, so on_change is a CONCRETE field and never boxed.\n\n(o) Small\n( ) Medium\n( ) Large\n\nWHERE THE MUTUAL EXCLUSION LIVES: in selected, which is ONE Int. There is no state in which two options are on, therefore there is no code that prevents it, therefore there is no bug in which that code fails. The alternative – a Radio widget per option with a checked::Bool and a back-pointer – makes \"exactly one\" an INVARIANT MAINTAINED BY A LOOP, and radio.state[] = CHECKED from application code leaves it broken PERMANENTLY. An Int needs no maintenance, so there is no maintenance to get wrong. This is Selection.cursor's argument (tablecore.jl:236) and List's \"a row is not a widget\" (list.jl:14), one type down: N options are N Strings and ZERO WidgetNodes – and, easy to miss, zero hit-test nodes, so hit_test stays O(depth) on every POINTER MOVE.\n\nTHERE IS NO Radio WIDGET IN THIS FILE and there is not going to be.\n\nNOT SCROLLABLE, NOT WINDOWED: render! is O(options), and an option IS a row, so O(options) IS O(viewport) here. Ten options is the design centre; two hundred options are a List. SIZES TO CONTENT and is NOT greedy: Size(CB_WIDTH + CB_GAP + widest, n). options is ALIASED, never copied.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\noptions::Vector{String}\nselected::Reactive{Int64}: The CHOSEN option, 1-based; 0 when nothing is chosen – and NOTHING is chosen initially, exactly as Selection chooses nothing (tablecore.jl:258). PAINT-reactive: every glyph is CB_WIDTH wide and the box is as wide as the widest caption whatever is picked, so this provably cannot move a cell.\n\ncursor::Reactive{Int64}: The cursor, 1-based; 0 when there are no options. The option SPACE would choose. ARROWS MOVE THIS WITHOUT CHOOSING, and that is why it is a SECOND field: selection-follows-arrow would make SPACE a NO-OP on the row it just moved to and fire on_change on EVERY KEYSTROKE – the trap List documents (list.jl:96). PAINT-reactive.\n\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_change::Any: Called as on_change(group) after a REAL choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RadioGroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.RadioGroup-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A radio group over any string vector, collected ONCE into a Vector{String}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RadioGroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.RadioGroup-Union{Tuple{Vector{String}}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{Vector{String}, F}} where F","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A radio group over options, calling on_change(group) after a real choice.\n\nFocusable by construction. selected seeds to 0 (nothing chosen); cursor seeds to row 1, or 0 when there are no options. options is ALIASED, not copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RadioGroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._cb_glyph-Tuple{ManyUI.CheckState.T}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_cb_glyph(s::ManyUI.CheckState.T) -> String\n\n\nThe glyph for s. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cb_glyph"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._rg_cursor!-Tuple{RadioGroup, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_rg_cursor!(w::RadioGroup, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nMove the cursor to i, clamped to 1:n. True iff it moved. Does NOT choose and does NOT fire on_change. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._rg_cursor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._rg_row_at-Tuple{RadioGroup, MouseEvent}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_rg_row_at(w::RadioGroup, e::MouseEvent) -> Int64\n\n\nThe 1-based option row under mouse event e; 0 for a miss.\n\n_tc_local, NEVER local_offset (events.jl:450): a RadioGroup inside a Scrollpane scrolled off the top would choose the wrong row with the unshifted border box. Bounds-checked against layout_of(w).content and the option count. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._rg_row_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.check_state-Tuple{Checkbox}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"check_state(w::Checkbox) -> ManyUI.CheckState.T\n\n\nThe current state. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.check_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.choose!-Tuple{RadioGroup, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"choose!(w::RadioGroup, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nChoose option i, clamped to 1:n, and move the cursor there – a choice is where you are. True iff the SELECTION changed; false when n == 0 or the option was already chosen. Fires on_change only on a real change.\n\nTHE single exit for a choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.choose!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.is_checked-Tuple{Checkbox}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"is_checked(w::Checkbox) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue iff the box is CHECKED. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_checked"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Checkbox, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::Checkbox, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe CONTENT extent: Size(CB_WIDTH, 1) bare, or CB_WIDTH + CB_GAP + text_width(label) wide with a caption.\n\nA content extent, like every other measure: the box overhead is outer_size's job and adding it here would count it twice (button.jl:52). NOT avail – a greedy checkbox would shrink a one-row Label beside it to ZERO rows. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{RadioGroup, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::RadioGroup, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe CONTENT extent: Size(0, 0) when empty, else `Size(CBWIDTH + CBGAP\n\nwidest, n). NOTavail`. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{Checkbox}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::Checkbox)\n\n\nClear the focus underline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{RadioGroup}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::RadioGroup)\n\n\nClear the focus underline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Checkbox, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Checkbox, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nSPACE toggles and consumes. ENTER does NEITHER.\n\nBOTH space forms are checked – Key.SPACE (what the parser emits) and Key.CHAR(' ') – exactly as Button does (button.jl:105). ENTER is left unconsumed on purpose: ENTER in a form is SUBMIT, and a checkbox that eats it makes ENTER the one key an app cannot bind. Unmodified keys only; TAB, ESCAPE and modified keys pass through untouched so the tab order stays alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Checkbox, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::Checkbox, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nA LEFT press toggles and consumes, anywhere in the box or caption.\n\nButton's rule (button.jl:119): the press does the work; the matching release activates nothing and consumes nothing. The box is one widget, so a click on the caption toggles it just like a click on the glyph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{RadioGroup, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::RadioGroup, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nArrows move the cursor; SPACE or ENTER choose it.\n\nUP/LEFT and DOWN/RIGHT move the cursor without choosing; HOME/END jump to the ends; SPACE (BOTH forms – Key.SPACE and Key.CHAR(' ')) or ENTER commit the choice. Consumes ONLY on a real move or choice, so UP on option 1 bubbles. Unmodified keys only; TAB, ESCAPE and modified keys pass through untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{RadioGroup, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::RadioGroup, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nA LEFT press on an option row chooses it, cursor and all, and consumes.\n\nA press outside the rows chooses nothing and does not consume. Button's rule (button.jl:119): the press does the work; the release does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{Checkbox}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::Checkbox)\n\n\nShow the focus underline. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{RadioGroup}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::RadioGroup)\n\n\nShow the focus underline. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY, as for Checkbox.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.radio_cursor-Tuple{RadioGroup}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"radio_cursor(w::RadioGroup) -> Int64\n\n\nThe cursor row; 0 iff there are no options. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.radio_cursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.selected-Tuple{RadioGroup}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"selected(w::RadioGroup) -> Int64\n\n\nThe chosen option index; 0 iff nothing is chosen. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.selected_option-Tuple{RadioGroup}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"selected_option(w::RadioGroup) -> Union{Nothing, String}\n\n\nThe chosen caption, or nothing. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected_option"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.set_state!-Tuple{Checkbox, ManyUI.CheckState.T}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"set_state!(w::Checkbox, s::ManyUI.CheckState.T) -> Bool\n\n\nSet the state, firing on_change only when it actually moves. True iff it changed.\n\nTHE single exit. The Reactive == guard already makes a redundant write free (reactive.jl:63); the explicit compare here is what stops on_change firing on a write that changes nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_state!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.toggle!-Tuple{Checkbox}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"toggle!(w::Checkbox) -> ManyUI.CheckState.T\n\n\nToggle the box and return the NEW state.\n\nCHECKED becomes UNCHECKED; anything else – INCLUDING MIXED – becomes CHECKED. See CheckState for why a program-set MIXED must toggle out to CHECKED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TABS_ACTIVE","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"The active caption. TC_SELECTED's meaning, one file over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TABS_ACTIVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TABS_FOCUS","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"The strip while focused: which caption the arrows will move from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TABS_FOCUS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TABS_PAD","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Cells of padding on each side of a caption. \" One \" is width 5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TABS_PAD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TabStrip","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct TabStrip <: Widget\n\nThe clickable row of captions. INTERNAL machinery of Tabs, exactly as Scrollpane's row/canvas are (scroll.jl:216), but a WIDGET rather than a render! branch of Tabs for one hard reason: _paint_node! hands render! the CONTENT box (paint.jl:134), so a Tabs that drew its own strip would have to reserve row 1 with padding.top = 1 – which puts the strip OUTSIDE the very buffer it would draw into.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ntitles::Vector{String}\nselected::Reactive{Int64}: The chosen tab, 1-based; 0 IFF isempty(titles). THE single source of truth – Tabs has NO selected field and reads this. One cell, one meaning, no mirror to keep in step.\nDirty.PAINT-reactive, and the licence is the one TextInput states verbatim (textinput.jl:158) and List cites (list.jl:43): measure must be independent of the state. It is – measure below is a function of titles alone. THE PANELS' Dirty.LAYOUT COMES FROM set_visible!, NOT FROM THIS CELL, which is why PAINT here is provable and not optimistic.\n\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TabStrip"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Tabs","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct Tabs <: Widget\n\nA tab strip plus panels.\n\nTabs SIZES TO CONTENT and is NOT greedy. It defines no measure and no render! – the Container defaults are already exactly right (container.jl:27), and the inactive panels are absent from the children's measure union because they are invisible. Give it grow: 1 or a height for a full-height tab view. A measure returning avail here would make a one-row Label beside a Tabs into a ZERO-row label.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nstrip::TabStrip\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Tabs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.Tabs-Tuple{Vararg{Pair{<:AbstractString, <:Widget}}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Tabs(\n    pairs::Pair{<:AbstractString, <:Widget}...;\n    id,\n    classes\n) -> Tabs\n\n\nA Tabs whose captions and panels are given as title => panel pairs, in order. The first tab is selected; the rest of the panels start hidden.\n\nThe strip is mounted as child 1 through invoke (the Scrollpane idiom, scroll.jl:37), because mount!(::Tabs, ::Widget) throws to force add_tab!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Tabs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tb_owner-Tuple{TabStrip}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tb_owner(w::TabStrip) -> Union{Nothing, Tabs}\n\n\nparent(w) as a Tabs, or nothing.\n\nTHE ONE isa IN THIS FILE, and its scope is why it is affordable: both types are in THIS file, at THIS layer, and the check runs on a KEYSTROKE or a CLICK, NEVER on the frame path. The owner is parent(w) BY CONSTRUCTION: Tabs mounts its strip as child 1 and nothing else ever mounts a TabStrip.\n\nA strip with no Tabs parent is INERT rather than an error: a bare TabStrip is a legal, if useless, widget, and a test that builds one must not throw. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tb_owner"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tb_sync!-Tuple{Tabs}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tb_sync!(w::Tabs)\n\n\nSet exactly the selected panel visible and every other hidden.\n\nset_visible! returns early on the value it already has (widget.jl:513), so this is O(tabs) compares and at most TWO Dirty.LAYOUT marks. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tb_sync!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tb_title_x-Tuple{Vector{String}, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tb_title_x(titles::Vector{String}, i::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe strip-local column at which caption i begins. Captions abut with no separator, so this is one running sum – the SAME one render! and tab_at walk. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tb_title_x"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.add_tab!-Tuple{Tabs, AbstractString, Widget}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"add_tab!(\n    w::Tabs,\n    title::AbstractString,\n    panel::Widget\n) -> Int64\n\n\nAppend a tab captioned title showing panel, and return its index.\n\nThe panel is mounted through invoke (the strip's own idiom), given grow = 1f0, and hidden unless it is the FIRST – the first tab becomes the selection. mark!(w.strip, Dirty.LAYOUT) because a new caption is a new strip extent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.add_tab!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{TabStrip, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::TabStrip, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nSize(sum(text_width(t) + 2 * TABS_PAD), 1).\n\nNOT avail, and this is browser-lesson 2 written into a signature: a measure that returns avail demands the whole viewport and flex then shrinks its neighbours – a one-row Label beside it becomes a ZERO-row label and VANISHES. A strip is exactly as wide as its captions and exactly one row tall. IT IS NOT GREEDY. Give the Tabs grow: 1, not the strip. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.mount!-Tuple{Tabs, Widget}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mount!(_::Tabs, _::Widget)\n\n\nPanels are added with add_tab!, never mount!: mounting one directly would leave the strip's titles and the children out of step. Throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.n_tabs-Tuple{Tabs}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"n_tabs(w::Tabs) -> Int64\n\n\nThe number of tabs. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.n_tabs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{TabStrip}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::TabStrip)\n\n\nThe strip lost focus: drop the focus underline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TabStrip, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TabStrip, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nKeyboard: LEFT/RIGHT step the selection (clamped, no wrap), HOME/END go to the ends, and '1'-'9' select by ordinal. Consumes ONLY when the selection actually moved, so RIGHT on the last tab bubbles.\n\nTAB, ESCAPE, modified keys and everything else are left untouched: a strip that ate TAB would trap focus forever.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TabStrip, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TabStrip, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nMouse: a LEFT press selects the caption under the pointer, located through _tc_local (NOT local_offset, which ignores scrolled ancestors). Consumes only on a real change.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{TabStrip}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::TabStrip)\n\n\nThe strip gained focus: light the focus underline and reveal it.\n\nreveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.select_tab!-Tuple{Tabs, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"select_tab!(w::Tabs, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect tab i, CLAMPED to 1:n_tabs. True iff the selection moved; false when it was unmoved or there are no tabs.\n\nTHE single exit: it writes w.strip.selected[] and then _tb_sync!. Clamped rather than thrown – set_cursor! clamps for the same reason (tablecore.jl:353).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_tab!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.selected-Tuple{Tabs}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"selected(w::Tabs) -> Int64\n\n\nThe chosen tab, 1-based; 0 iff there are no tabs. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.tab_at-Tuple{Vector{String}, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"tab_at(titles::Vector{String}, x::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe 1-based caption covering strip-local column x; 0 for none.\n\nPURE – a Vector{String} and an Int, no widget, no layout, no buffer. tab_at and render! walk the SAME running sum in the SAME direction, which is what makes \"click the caption you see\" true by construction rather than by two arithmetics agreeing by luck.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.tab_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.tab_panel-Tuple{Tabs, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"tab_panel(w::Tabs, i::Int64) -> Widget\n\n\nThe panel of tab i. The strip is child 1, so panel i is child i + 1. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.tab_panel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.tab_title-Tuple{Tabs, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"tab_title(w::Tabs, i::Int64) -> String\n\n\nThe caption of tab i. Pure. Throws on a bad index – a caller naming a tab that does not exist has a bug.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.tab_title"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TV_CLOSED","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Collapsed twisty. Width 1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TV_CLOSED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TV_GAP","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Cells between the twisty and the label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TV_GAP"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TV_INDENT","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Cells of indent per depth level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TV_INDENT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TV_LEAF","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A leaf's twisty: a space. Width 1, so the TEXT COLUMN IS INVARIANT between leaves and branches – a tree whose labels jog left by one on a leaf is a tree nobody can read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TV_LEAF"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TV_OPEN","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"Expanded twisty. Width 1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TV_OPEN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TreeNode","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct TreeNode{T}\n\nOne node of a TreeView's data model. NOT A WIDGET.\n\nList's argument (list.jl:14) one type up, and it is worth more here: a 10 000-node tree is 10 000 of THESE and ZERO WidgetNodes – no layout pass per expand, no 10 000 render! dispatches per frame, and – the part that is easy to miss – no 10 000 hit-test nodes, so hit_test stays O(depth) ON EVERY POINTER MOVE, not merely every frame.\n\nNO PARENT POINTER, DELIBERATELY. A parent field is a second source of truth that push!(n.kids, x) silently fails to maintain, and building a tree stops being a literal. TreeView recovers the parent FROM THE FLATTENED ROWS – the nearest earlier row with a smaller depth – in O(depth-from-here) on a KEYSTROKE, never on a frame. That is the right price for a field that cannot go stale because it does not exist.\n\nFields\n\nvalue::Any: The payload.\nkids::Array{TreeNode{T}, 1} where T\nexpanded::Bool: True when this node's children are part of the visible flattening. A LEAF is always collapsed and toggle_node! will not move it – isempty(kids) IS the leaf test, so there is no separate flag to fall out of step with the vector it describes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TreeNode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TreeNode-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, Array{TreeNode{T}, 1}}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A tree node carrying value, with kids in display order.\n\nexpanded defaults to false: a tree opens showing its roots and nothing beneath them, which is what \"collapsed\" means and what every file browser does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TreeNode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TreeRow","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"struct TreeRow{T}\n\nOne DISPLAYED row: a node and its indentation depth. isbits-adjacent (a reference and an Int), so Vector{TreeRow{T}} is dense. Internal to the widget's cache; never a user type.\n\nFields\n\nnode::TreeNode: The node this row shows.\ndepth::Int64: Indent level. A root is 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TreeRow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TreeView","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"mutable struct TreeView{T, F, A} <: RowsWidget\n\nA scrollable, focusable hierarchical view over TreeNode DATA.\n\nrender! touches rows[scroll.y+1 : scroll.y+height] and NOTHING ELSE, so paint is O(WINDOW), never O(nodes) – List's contract exactly, and a 10 000-node tree costs the same frame as a 10-node one.\n\nTHE FLATTEN IS O(VISIBLE), NOT O(WINDOW). A fully-expanded 10 000-node tree holds a 10 000-entry Vector{TreeRow}. It is 10 000 TreeRows and ZERO WidgetNodes, which is the claim that matters. A COLLAPSED SUBTREE CONTRIBUTES EXACTLY ONE ROW HOWEVER DEEP IT IS – that is not an optimisation, IT IS WHAT EXPAND/COLLAPSE MEANS.\n\nflattened is the memo bit and it is KEYED ON THE SHAPE, NOT ON version – deliberately: version bumps on every ARROW KEY (_tc_touch!), so keying on it would re-flatten 10 000 nodes PER KEYSTROKE. A cursor move is not a shape change.\n\nGREEDY BY DESIGN: measure(w, avail) = avail. List's argument (list.jl:239) – an auto-height tree would be as tall as its data and would NEVER SCROLL AT ALL. Give it height: 10 or a grow: 1 parent; do not put it beside a one-row Label you want to survive. This is also what licenses version's PAINT reactivity: a data change provably cannot move this box, so toggle_node! on a 10 000-node tree costs ZERO layout.\n\nSINGLE SELECTION ONLY; mode IS NOT A KWARG. Re-pinning a BitSet of N rows across a re-flatten has no answer for a selected row whose parent just collapsed. Under SINGLE the selection IS the cursor and one rule covers both.\n\nroots is ALIASED, never copied.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nroots::Array{TreeNode{T}, 1} where T\nformat::Any\nrows::Array{TreeRow{T}, 1} where T\nflattened::Bool: False when rows is stale. The memo bit of _tv_flat!.\nversion::Reactive{Int64}: Bumped by every data, expansion OR cursor change. THE cell. PAINT.\nsel::Selection\nwidest::Int64: Widest visible row in cells, INCLUDING indent and twisty. EXACT.\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_activate::Any: Called as on_activate(tree) on ENTER. NAMED on_activate BY      FORCE: _tc_key! reads w.on_activate (tablecore.jl:1080).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TreeView"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.TreeView-Union{Tuple{Array{TreeNode{T}, 1}}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{TreeNode{T}, 1}, A}} where {T, F, A}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"A tree over roots, calling on_activate(tree) on ENTER.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring. roots is ALIASED, not copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TreeView"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tc_extent_width-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tc_extent_width(w::TreeView) -> Int64\n\n\nThe widest visible row, in cells. OVERRIDES the RowsWidget default, which calls grid_of(w) – a tree has no columns, so that default is a MethodError. _tv_flat! computes the exact mark in the walk it was doing anyway; there is no scanned bit and no fixpoint to escape (list.jl:154). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_extent_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_cursor_node-Union{Tuple{TreeView{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_cursor_node(w::TreeView{T}) -> Any\n\n\nThe node under the cursor, or nothing. Internal, pure w.r.t. tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_cursor_node"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_flat!-Union{Tuple{TreeView{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_flat!(w::TreeView{T}) -> Array{TreeRow{T}, 1} where T\n\n\nw.rows, REBUILT IFF flattened is false. One Bool test and ZERO allocation on the hit; O(VISIBLE) on the miss. _lst_scan! (list.jl:200) is the precedent and the memo bit means the same thing.\n\nempty! + push! into the SAME Vector, never a fresh one: the buffer's capacity survives, so re-expanding the same subtree allocates nothing after the first time.\n\nwidest IS EXACT AND THERE IS NO scanned BIT: a tree MUST walk to know what is visible at all, so the width falls out of a walk that was happening anyway. One pass, exact answer, no memo to invalidate. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_flat!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_key_left!-Tuple{TreeView, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_key_left!(w::TreeView, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nLEFT: collapse, or if already shut / a leaf move to the parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_key_left!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_key_right!-Tuple{TreeView, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_key_right!(w::TreeView, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nRIGHT: expand, or if already open move to the first child. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_key_right!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_key_toggle!-Tuple{TreeView, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_key_toggle!(w::TreeView, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nSPACE: toggle the cursor node; consume iff it moved. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_key_toggle!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_parent_row-Tuple{TreeView, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_parent_row(w::TreeView, i::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe parent of visible row i: the NEAREST EARLIER row with a SMALLER depth; 0 when i is top-level or out of range. Pure w.r.t. tree.\n\nO(depth-from-here) worst case – it walks UP the flattened vector and stops at the first shallower row – on a KEYSTROKE, never on a frame. This is what TreeNode having no parent pointer costs, and it CANNOT BE STALE, which a field would be the first time someone push!ed a child. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_parent_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_rebuild!-Union{Tuple{TreeView{T}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, Union{Nothing, TreeNode{T}}}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_rebuild!(w::TreeView{T})\n_tv_rebuild!(\n    w::TreeView{T},\n    fallback::Union{Nothing, TreeNode{T}}\n)\n\n\nRe-flatten and RE-PIN the cursor by NODE IDENTITY: the row the user was on keeps the node they were on, whatever its flat index becomes.\n\nRE-PINNING IS THE WHOLE FUNCTION AND IT IS WHY MULTI IS NOT OFFERED. The cursor is a ROW INDEX and the rows renumber, so a rebuild that did not re-pin would leave the cursor on whatever slid into its slot.\n\nfallback is where the cursor goes when its node is NO LONGER VISIBLE – which is exactly collapse!'s case. _tv_set_expanded! passes THE COLLAPSED NODE, so collapsing an ancestor puts the cursor on the ancestor – one rule, testable, and unrepresentable as a bug.\n\nRe-clamps the scroll afterwards: this is the only call that can SHRINK the extent and strand an offset past the end (refresh_extent!(::List), list.jl:299). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_rebuild!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_row_of-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, TreeNode{T}}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_row_of(w::TreeView{T}, n::TreeNode{T}) -> Int64\n\n\nThe flat row showing n by IDENTITY; 0 when n is not visible. O(visible), on a keystroke, never a frame. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_row_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_set_all!-Tuple{TreeNode, Bool}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_set_all!(n::TreeNode, v::Bool)\n\n\nRecursively set expanded on n and every non-leaf below it. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_set_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_set_expanded!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, TreeNode{T}, Bool}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_set_expanded!(\n    w::TreeView{T},\n    n::TreeNode{T},\n    v::Bool\n) -> Bool\n\n\nSet n.expanded = v and re-flatten, re-pinning the cursor. False for a leaf or an unchanged flag. THE single exit of every expand/collapse, so none of the four steps can be forgotten. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_set_expanded!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI._tv_walk!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, TreeNode{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"_tv_walk!(w::TreeView{T}, n::TreeNode{T}, depth::Int64)\n\n\nPush n and, if it is expanded, its subtree, into w.rows; raise widest to fit each label. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tv_walk!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.collapse_all!-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"collapse_all!(w::TreeView)\n\n\nCollapse EVERY node. O(nodes) – on a user action, never a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.collapse_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.collapse_node!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, TreeNode{T}}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"collapse_node!(w::TreeView{T}, n::TreeNode{T}) -> Bool\n\n\nCollapse n. False for an already-shut node. Passes n as the rebuild's fallback, so a cursor inside the closing subtree lands on n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.collapse_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"content_extent(w::TreeView) -> Size\n\n\nSize(widest, n_visible). OVERRIDES the container default: a tree's content is DATA, not children. THIS OVERRIDE IS THE WHOLE INTEGRATION WITH Scrollbar – Scrollbar{TreeView{T,F,A}} works with ZERO new code in scroll.jl (list.jl:212). O(1) on the memo hit; ON THE FRAME PATH several times per frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.expand_all!-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"expand_all!(w::TreeView)\n\n\nExpand EVERY node. O(nodes) – on a user action, never a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.expand_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.expand_node!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, TreeNode{T}}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"expand_node!(w::TreeView{T}, n::TreeNode{T}) -> Bool\n\n\nExpand n. False for a leaf or an already-open node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.expand_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.is_expanded-Tuple{TreeNode}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"is_expanded(n::TreeNode) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when n is a non-leaf whose children are visible. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_expanded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.is_leaf-Tuple{TreeNode}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"is_leaf(n::TreeNode) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when n has no children. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_leaf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{TreeView, Size}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"measure(w::TreeView, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\navail. List's argument verbatim (list.jl:239): a tree takes the space it is OFFERED and scrolls its content, because an auto-HEIGHT tree would be as tall as its data and would never scroll at all. Give it height: 10 or a grow: 1 parent. This is also what licenses version's PAINT reactivity. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.node_at-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"node_at(w::TreeView{T}, k::Int64) -> Any\n\n\nThe TreeNode at flat row k, or nothing out of range. O(1). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.node_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::TreeView)\n\n\nHide the cursor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TreeView, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TreeView, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nThree interceptions, then _tc_key!. SPACE toggles the cursor node, RIGHT expands / walks to the first child, LEFT collapses / walks to the parent – a tree's arrows have meant expand/collapse since 1990 and no user will accept the horizontal-scroll binding _tc_key! gives them. UP/DOWN/PAGE/HOME/END/ENTER and shift-extend are DELEGATED unchanged. Consumes ONLY when something moved, so LEFT on a root leaf bubbles. TAB and ESCAPE fall through, which is what keeps the tab order alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TreeView, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TreeView, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nA LEFT PRESS on the TWISTY COLUMN toggles the row; anywhere else DELEGATES to _tc_mouse! (cursor, drag, wheel). The twisty test uses the SAME arithmetic render! paints it at, so \"click what you see\" is true by construction. _tc_local, NEVER local_offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::TreeView)\n\n\nShow the cursor, and scroll every ancestor pane until this tree is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.refresh_tree!-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"refresh_tree!(w::TreeView)\n\n\nRe-flatten from the current roots/kids. THE public escape hatch for \"I mutated roots/kids myself\" – refresh_rows!(::List)'s contract (list.jl:462), same meaning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.set_roots!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{TreeView{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"set_roots!(w::TreeView{T}, rs)\n\n\nReplace the roots. CLEARS the selection and rewinds the scroll: every flat index the selection held names a row that may no longer exist. set_items!(::List)'s argument (list.jl:394).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_roots!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.toggle_node!-Tuple{TreeView, Int64}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"toggle_node!(w::TreeView, k::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nFlip the expansion of flat row k. False for a leaf or a bad row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.tree_cursor-Union{Tuple{TreeView{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"tree_cursor(w::TreeView{T}) -> Any\n\n\nThe node under the cursor, or nothing. Pure w.r.t. tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.tree_cursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/widgets/#ManyUI.tree_rows-Tuple{TreeView}","page":"Built-in widgets","text":"tree_rows(w::TreeView) -> Array{TreeRow{T}, 1} where T\n\n\nThe flattened VISIBLE rows. ALIASED; do not mutate. Pure w.r.t. tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.tree_rows"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Data-widgets","page":"Data widgets","text":"List, Table and DataTable display collections. Reach for List when you have items, Table when you have columns, and DataTable when you also need sorting.\n\nThey differ from every other widget in one way that matters: a row is not a widget. A hundred-thousand-row table is a Vector of a hundred thousand elements and zero extra nodes in the tree.","title":"Data widgets"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Rows-are-data","page":"Data widgets","text":"A widget per row would mean 100 000 WidgetNodes, a layout pass over all of them on every edit, and 100 000 render! dispatches per frame. Instead these widgets hold their rows as plain data, override content_extent, and paint only the rows you can actually see.\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nsmall = List([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\nbig = List([\"item $i\" for i in 1:100_000])\n\n(small_nodes = length(descendants(small)),\n big_nodes = length(descendants(big)))\n\nBoth are zero. The cost of a frame is proportional to the window, not to the data, so a 100 000-row list paints in the same time as a 3-row one.\n\nThis is the same seam TextArea uses, which is why a Scrollbar works on any of them with no special-casing — Scrollbar is parametric on the thing it reports about, and these widgets simply override content_extent to say how big their data is.","title":"Rows are data"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#List","page":"Data widgets","text":"A scrollable, focusable list. Items are whatever you like; format turns one into a string.\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nl = List([\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\", \"delta\"])\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, l)\nlayout!(l, Region(1, 1, 12, 3))\n\nbuf = Buffer(Size(12, 3))\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, l)\n[join(String(buf.cells[x, y].content) for x in 1:12) for y in 1:3]\n\nThree rows of window, four items: it paints the three it can show.\n\nThe cursor moves with the arrows, home/end and pageup/pagedown, scrolling itself into view as it goes, and the wheel scrolls the list. Clicking a row selects it. on_activate fires on enter:\n\nList([\"alpha\", \"beta\"], w -> println(\"chose \", w.items[cursor_of(w)]))\n\nRows can be any type, with format doing the projection:\n\nPeople = [(name = \"Ada\", born = 1815), (name = \"Alan\", born = 1912)]\nList(People; format = p -> \"$(p.name) ($(p.born))\") |> row_count","title":"List"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Table","page":"Data widgets","text":"Columns with headers. Each Column carries its caption, width and alignment:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nrows = [(\"alice\", 55), (\"bob\", 31), (\"carol\", 31)]\ncols = [Column(\"name\"; width = cells(6)),\n        Column(\"age\"; width = cells(4), align = Align.END)]\n\nt = Table(rows, cols)\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, t)\nlayout!(t, Region(1, 1, 12, 4))\n\nbuf = Buffer(Size(12, 4))\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, t)\n[join(String(buf.cells[x, y].content) for x in 1:12) for y in 1:4]\n\nThe header behaves asymmetrically on purpose: it stays put while the rows scroll under it, but follows the columns when you scroll sideways. A header that scrolled away vertically would be useless; one that did not move horizontally would lie about which column you are looking at.","title":"Table"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Column-widths","page":"Data widgets","text":"A column width is a Length, the same type the layout engine uses:\n\nWidth Meaning\ncells(n) exactly n cells\npct(n) n% of the table\nfr(n) weight n of the leftover space\nAUTO sized to content\n\nAUTO is the one with a catch. Sizing a column to its content means measuring rows, and measuring 100 000 of them every frame would undo the entire point of these widgets. So AUTO measures a sample of rows (sample, defaulting to TC_AUTO_SAMPLE) rather than all of them. It is a good guess, not a guarantee: a wide value outside the sample gets truncated rather than widening the column. Give a column an explicit width when you need certainty.","title":"Column widths"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#DataTable","page":"Data widgets","text":"A Table that sorts. It needs a key telling it how to extract a sortable value from a row and a column index:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nrows = [(\"carol\", 31), (\"alice\", 55), (\"bob\", 31)]\ncols = [Column(\"name\"; width = cells(6)), Column(\"age\"; width = cells(4))]\n\ndt = DataTable(rows, cols; key = (r, j) -> r[j])\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, dt)\nlayout!(dt, Region(1, 1, 12, 4))\n\nsort_by!(dt, 1)          # by name, ascending\n\nbuf = Buffer(Size(12, 4))\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, dt)\n[join(String(buf.cells[x, y].content) for x in 1:12) for y in 1:4]\n\nThe ▲ marks the sorted column. toggle_sort! cycles a column between ascending and descending.","title":"DataTable"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Sorting-does-not-touch-your-data","page":"Data widgets","text":"Two properties, both load-bearing.\n\nYour vector is yours. Sorting permutes an index, never the rows you handed over:\n\nrows                       # untouched, still in its original order\n\nsource_index maps a displayed row back to where it came from, which is what you want when the user picks row 1 and you need to know which row that really is.\n\nThe sort is stable. Rows with equal keys keep their relative order, so sorting by age here leaves carol before bob — the order they arrived in — rather than shuffling ties arbitrarily:\n\nsort_by!(dt, 2)            # by age; 31 appears twice\n[(source_index(dt, k), rows[source_index(dt, k)]) for k in 1:view_count(dt)]\n\nSorting is O(n log n), and it runs when you ask — never per frame. Painting a sorted table costs exactly what painting an unsorted one costs.","title":"Sorting does not touch your data"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Cells-and-wide-characters","page":"Data widgets","text":"A value too wide for its column is truncated on a grapheme boundary, never mid-cluster. A CJK ideograph or an emoji occupies two cells, and a wide cluster that would straddle the column edge is dropped whole rather than sliced in half — a halved cluster would corrupt every column to its right.\n\nusing ManyUI\n(truncate_width(\"漢字テキスト\", 5), text_width(\"漢字\"))\n\nThis is why the framework has text_width and truncate_width and never uses Base.textwidth, which reports 1 for an emoji that occupies 2 cells and 8 for a ZWJ family that occupies 2.","title":"Cells and wide characters"},{"category":"section","location":"data/#Scrolling-them","page":"Data widgets","text":"Each of these widgets scrolls itself, so a Scrollbar can report on one directly:\n\nContainer(my_list, Scrollbar(ScrollAxis.VERTICAL, my_list))\n\nWrapping one in a Scrollpane is also legal and scrolls the widget's whole box instead — the two mechanisms compose rather than compete.","title":"Scrolling them"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#Layout","page":"Layout","text":"The layout engine resolves a widget tree into an absolute Region for every node, using a box model deliberately close to CSS.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#The-box-model","page":"Layout","text":"Every widget has four nested boxes, derived from its outer margin box:\n\n┌─ margin ──────────────────────────┐\n│  ┌─ border ─────────────────────┐ │\n│  │  ┌─ padding ───────────────┐ │ │\n│  │  │  ┌─ content ─────────┐  │ │ │\n│  │  │  │  the widget draws │  │ │ │\n│  │  │  └───────────────────┘  │ │ │\n│  │  └─────────────────────────┘ │ │\n│  └──────────────────────────────┘ │\n└───────────────────────────────────┘\n\nlayout_box is the single definition of that derivation; no other part of the framework re-derives it. region(w) returns the border box, and content_region(w) the content box a widget may actually paint into.\n\nMargins, padding and borders are measured in cells:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nouter = Region(1, 1, 20, 6)\nstyle = BoxStyle(; margin = Spacing(1), padding = Spacing(2),\n                 border = Border(BorderKind.ROUND, STYLE_NONE))\nlb = layout_box(style, outer)\n\n(margin = lb.margin_box, border = lb.border_box,\n padding = lb.padding_box, content = lb.content)\n\nEach ring shrinks the next: one cell of margin, one of border, then two of padding, taking a 20×6 outer box down to a 12×0 content box. Ask for more chrome than you have room for and the content box empties rather than going negative.","title":"The box model"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#Sizing","page":"Layout","text":"A Length resolves in one of four ways:\n\nConstructor Meaning\nAUTO size to content\ncells(n) exactly n cells\npct(n) n% of the parent's content box\nfr(n) weight n of the remaining free space\n\nfr is the flex unit. After fixed and percentage children are placed, whatever is left is split between the fr children in proportion to their weights.\n\nusing ManyUI\n(cells(10), pct(50), fr(1), AUTO)","title":"Sizing"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#Flex-containers","page":"Layout","text":"A container with display = Display.FLEX lays its children out along direction, distributing space with justify and aligning them across the axis with align:\n\n(direction = instances(Direction.T),\n justify = instances(Justify.T),\n align = instances(Align.T))\n\nlayout! computes the whole tree against a viewport:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nroot = Container(Label(\"first\"; id = :a), Label(\"second\"; id = :b);\n                 id = :root)\nlayout!(root, Region(1, 1, 20, 6))\n\n(root = region(root),\n a = region(query_one(root, \"#a\")),\n b = region(query_one(root, \"#b\")))","title":"Flex containers"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#Resize","page":"Layout","text":"When the terminal changes size, the entire tree's bounding boxes are recomputed against the new viewport. A ResizeEvent from any source — a SIGWINCH on a tty, a browser window resize reported over the WebSocket — funnels through the same path:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\ndriver = HeadlessDriver(Size(20, 5))\napp = App(Container(Label(\"x\")), driver)\nhandle!(app, ResizeEvent(Size(60, 20)))\nbuffer_size(app.back)","title":"Resize"},{"category":"section","location":"layout/#Pure-and-mutating-forms","page":"Layout","text":"compute_layout is pure: it returns a LayoutMap from widget to box and touches nothing. apply_layout! writes such a map into the tree, and layout! is the two composed. relayout! is the incremental form the frame loop actually uses — it recomputes only the dirty subtree, anchored at its existing margin box.","title":"Pure and mutating forms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/buffer/#Buffer,-diff-and-ANSI","page":"Buffer, diff and ANSI","text":"","title":"Buffer, diff and ANSI"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Concepts","page":"Concepts","text":"ManyUI borrows the browser's ideas and applies them to a grid of characters. If you know the DOM, CSS and addEventListener, you already know the shape of this framework.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#The-render-pipeline","page":"Concepts","text":"Every frame runs the same five stages. Each is a plain function over plain data, which is why almost all of ManyUI can be tested without a terminal:\n\nwidget tree ─▶ layout ─▶ cell buffer ─▶ diff ─▶ ANSI bytes ─▶ driver\n\nTree. A hierarchy of widgets, each holding a WidgetNode with its id, classes, parent and children.\nLayout. compute_layout resolves the CSS box model into an absolute Region for every node.\nPaint. paint! walks the laid-out tree and writes Cells into a Buffer.\nDiff. diff compares the previous frame to the new one and yields a Patch: the minimal set of changed cells.\nEncode. AnsiEncoder turns that patch into escape sequences, emitting a cursor move only when cells are not contiguous, and an SGR only when the style actually changed.\n\nThe diff is what keeps rendering cheap. An unchanged frame costs nothing at all:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nbefore = Buffer(Size(10, 2))\nafter = Buffer(Size(10, 2))\nclear!(before)\nclear!(after)\n\n# Identical buffers produce no work whatsoever.\nlength(diff(before, before).spans)\n\nChange two cells, and only those two cross the wire:\n\nwrite_text!(after, 1, 1, \"hi\", STYLE_NONE)\npatch = diff(before, after)\n(spans = length(patch.spans), cells = n_cells(patch))\n\nencoder = AnsiEncoder(ColorDepth.TRUECOLOR)\nlength(encode(encoder, patch))","title":"The render pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#The-driver-seam","page":"Concepts","text":"This is the idea the whole project rests on. A Driver is the only thing that knows what a rendering target is, and it has exactly nine required methods:\n\nusing ManyUI\nREQUIRED_DRIVER_METHODS\n\nA driver moves bytes, reports a size and its capabilities, and yields events. That is all. No method in the interface accepts or returns a Buffer, a Patch, a Widget or a Style, so a driver cannot reach into the framework and the framework cannot depend on a target.\n\nThree drivers exist today:\n\nDriver Target Lives in\nTerminalDriver the host tty ManyUI\nHeadlessDriver an in-memory buffer ManyUI\nWebSocketDriver a browser ManyUIWeb\n\nManyUIWeb implements those nine methods over a WebSocket and adds nothing else. That is why ManyUI has no HTTP dependency: a pure terminal application never pays for the web bridge. A driver can check its own conformance without reading ManyUI's source, which is how the bridge package proves it implements the interface:\n\ncheck_driver_interface(HeadlessDriver)   # empty means conformant\n\nHeadlessDriver is the proof the seam is honest. It renders into an IOBuffer, so the whole framework — including the event loop — is testable with no tty and no network. If a headless driver needs nothing from ManyUI's internals, neither does a web one.","title":"The driver seam"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Reactivity-and-dirty-flagging","page":"Concepts","text":"Mutating a widget does not repaint the world. mark! flags only that widget and the descendants a change can actually reach. Siblings, uncles and cousins are never touched, so a deep tree with one changed leaf costs one subtree of layout rather than a full pass.\n\nAncestors get a Dirty.SUBTREE breadcrumb so the next frame can find the dirty node, and that breadcrumb is promoted to real layout work only at an ancestor whose size genuinely depends on its content.","title":"Reactivity and dirty flagging"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#The-asynchronous-event-loop","page":"Concepts","text":"run! blocks on a Channel{Event} that the driver fills. The OS scheduler does the pacing: there is no fixed frame rate and no busy-wait. A burst — a resize storm, a large paste — drains through the channel and collapses into a single repaint.\n\nThe loop is parameterised over the driver, so App{HeadlessDriver} and App{WebSocketDriver} are separate concrete types and the frame path stays statically dispatched.","title":"The asynchronous event loop"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Wide-characters","page":"Concepts","text":"A terminal grid is not a string. An emoji or a CJK ideograph occupies two cells, and getting this wrong corrupts every column to its right. ManyUI measures by grapheme cluster, never by codepoint, and never trusts Base.textwidth:\n\nusing ManyUI\n[(s, grapheme_width(s), textwidth(s)) for s in (\"a\", \"漢\", \"❤️\", \"👨‍👩‍👧‍👦\")]\n\nThe last two are why this matters: Base.textwidth reports 1 for a VS16 emoji that renders in two cells, and 8 for a ZWJ family that renders in two.","title":"Wide characters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/tree/#Widget-tree,-box-model-and-layout","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"","title":"Widget tree, box model and layout"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.DIRTY_ALL","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"PAINT | LAYOUT | STYLE. Note that it excludes the SUBTREE breadcrumb.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DIRTY_ALL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.DirtyMask","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"Bitset over Dirty.T values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DirtyMask"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.WidgetNode","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mutable struct WidgetNode\n\nPer-widget state, held by COMPOSITION: every widget HAS a node. No abstract-field inheritance anywhere.\n\nparent/children are Widget-typed by necessity – a heterogeneous tree is the point – and every hot path recovers inference through node(w), whose return type is concrete.\n\nFields\n\nid::Symbol\nclasses::Set{Symbol}\ntype_name::Symbol\nparent::Union{Nothing, Widget}: Parent widget, or nothing at the root.\nchildren::Vector{Widget}\ninline_style::Style: Author-set style; highest specificity.\ninline_box::BoxPatch: Author-set box overrides; highest specificity.\ncomputed_style::Style: Post-cascade style.\nbox::BoxStyle: Post-cascade layout inputs.\nlayout::LayoutBox: Post-layout boxes, in ABSOLUTE coordinates.\nscroll::Offset: Cells this node's CHILDREN are shifted by at paint time. ORIGIN is unscrolled.\nOffset(3, 0) means \"scrolled right by 3\", so a child paints three cells to the LEFT. Shifts the CHILDREN and NEVER this node's own render frame – that is what keeps size(buf) inside render! invariant under scroll. A widget with no children that scrolls its own content reads this field itself.\nNEVER an input to compute_layout: the LayoutMap is invariant under scrolling, which is why a wheel tick is a repaint and not a relayout.\n\ndirty::UInt8: What is stale about this node.\nvisible::Bool: False hides the node and its subtree.\nfocusable::Bool: True makes the node part of the tab order.\napp::Any: The owning App, once mounted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.WidgetNode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.WidgetNode-Tuple{Symbol, Set{Symbol}, Symbol, Union{Nothing, Widget}, Vector{Widget}, Style, BoxPatch, Style, BoxStyle, LayoutBox, UInt8, Bool, Bool, Any}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"WidgetNode(\n    id::Symbol,\n    classes::Set{Symbol},\n    type_name::Symbol,\n    parent::Union{Nothing, Widget},\n    children::Vector{Widget},\n    inline_style::Style,\n    inline_box::BoxPatch,\n    computed_style::Style,\n    box::BoxStyle,\n    layout::LayoutBox,\n    dirty::UInt8,\n    visible::Bool,\n    focusable::Bool,\n    app\n) -> WidgetNode\n\n\nThe pre-scroll POSITIONAL form: scroll defaults to ORIGIN.\n\nExists because adding a field silently invalidates the auto-generated positional constructor, and call sites in test_paint.jl and css_tests.jl depend on the 14-argument shape. Keep the argument order in lockstep with the struct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.WidgetNode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.WidgetNode-Tuple{}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"WidgetNode(\n;\n    id,\n    classes,\n    type_name,\n    visible,\n    focusable\n) -> WidgetNode\n\n\nA fresh node: no parent, no children, nothing cascaded, nothing laid out, everything dirty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.WidgetNode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.getindex-Tuple{Widget, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"getindex(w::Widget, i::Int64) -> Widget\n\n\nchildren(w)[i]. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.parent-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"parent(w::Widget) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nParent of w, or nothing at the root. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._attach!-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_attach!(p::Widget, c::Widget)\n\n\nLink c (and its whole subtree) into p's tree: set the parent, bind the app, fire on_mount! in pre-order, then flag the new subtree for a cascade and a layout.\n\nInternal: the three public entry points (mount!, mount! variadic and insert_child!) all funnel through here so the lifecycle is defined exactly once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._attach!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._breadcrumb!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_breadcrumb!(w::Widget)\n\n\nDrop the Dirty.SUBTREE breadcrumb on every ancestor of w, and on nothing else.\n\nInternal. The breadcrumb is a routing hint, not dirt: is_dirty(a) stays false for an ancestor that only carries it, which is exactly what makes E1 (\"only that specific widget and its affected descendants\") literally true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._breadcrumb!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._find_first-Tuple{Widget, Vector{String}}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_find_first(\n    w::Widget,\n    cs::Vector{String}\n) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nDepth-first search for the first match, exiting as soon as it is found. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._find_first"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_axis_is_definite-Tuple{BoxStyle}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_axis_is_definite(bs::BoxStyle) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when bs's main-axis size resolves without measuring its children.\n\nCELLS and PERCENT are definite; AUTO sizes to content and FRACTION is a share of free space, so both genuinely depend on the subtree. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_axis_is_definite"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_axis_length-Tuple{BoxStyle}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_axis_length(bs::BoxStyle) -> Length\n\n\nThe Length governing bs's own main-axis size: width for a ROW, height for a COLUMN. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_axis_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._match_any-Tuple{Widget, Vector{String}}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_match_any(w::Widget, cs::Vector{String}) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w matches any compound in the parsed selector list. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._match_any"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._match_compound-Tuple{Widget, AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_match_compound(w::Widget, sel::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w satisfies every token of the validated compound selector sel. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._match_compound"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._parse_selector-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_parse_selector(sel::AbstractString) -> Vector{String}\n\n\nSplit a comma-separated selector list into validated compound selectors. Throws ArgumentError on anything this layer cannot express. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_selector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.add_class!-Tuple{Widget, Symbol}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"add_class!(w::Widget, c::Symbol) -> Widget\n\n\nAdd a class and mark Dirty.STYLE. Adding a class w already carries is a no-op: nothing cascades differently, so nothing is flagged. Returns w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.add_class!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.ancestors-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"ancestors(w::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nw's ancestors, from its parent up to the root. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ancestors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.app-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"app(w::Widget) -> Any\n\n\nThe App this widget is mounted on, or nothing. Walks the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.app"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.box-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"box(w::Widget) -> BoxStyle\n\n\nPost-cascade layout inputs of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.box"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.children-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"children(w::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nChildren of w, in paint and tab order. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.children"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.clamp_scroll-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"clamp_scroll(\n    pos::Int64,\n    viewport::Int64,\n    content::Int64\n) -> Int64\n\n\npos clamped to the range a scroll position may legally take: 0 when the content fits, 0:(content - viewport) otherwise.\n\nTHE definition of \"clamped to the content extent\". Pure, total, and shared by every scrolling widget so they cannot disagree – with no widget-to-widget dependency. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clamp_scroll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.classes-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"classes(w::Widget) -> Set{Symbol}\n\n\nCSS classes of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.classes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.clean!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"clean!(w::Widget)\n\n\nClear w's whole dirty mask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clean!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.clear_dirty-Tuple{UInt8, ManyUI.Dirty.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"clear_dirty(m::UInt8, k::ManyUI.Dirty.T) -> UInt8\n\n\nm with k cleared. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clear_dirty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.computed_style-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"computed_style(w::Widget) -> Style\n\n\nPost-cascade style of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.computed_style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.content_region-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"content_region(w::Widget) -> Region\n\n\nnode(w).layout.content. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.descendants-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"descendants(w::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nw's descendants in pre-order, excluding w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.descendants"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.dirty_root-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"dirty_root(root::Widget) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nThe HIGHEST node carrying real Dirty.LAYOUT – the root of the minimal relayout. nothing when the tree is layout-clean.\n\nFound by descending SUBTREE breadcrumbs, so it is O(depth), not O(n). When two independent branches are layout-dirty the descent stops at their common ancestor – the shallowest node a correct relayout can start from – even though that node carries only the breadcrumb. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.dirty_root"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.escalate_auto!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"escalate_auto!(w::Widget)\n\n\nWalk UP from w. An ancestor whose main-axis size is AUTO genuinely depends on its children, so its SUBTREE breadcrumb is PROMOTED to real LAYOUT.\n\nStops at the first ancestor with a definite (CELLS/PERCENT) size on the relevant axis – that ancestor keeps only SUBTREE, because its own box cannot move, and the walk goes no higher. This is what makes relayout! sublinear.\n\nA promoted ancestor gets LAYOUT but NOT PAINT: whether its box actually changed is apply_layout!'s call to make, not ours.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.escalate_auto!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.has_class-Tuple{Widget, Symbol}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"has_class(w::Widget, c::Symbol) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w carries class c. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.has_class"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.has_dirty-Tuple{UInt8, ManyUI.Dirty.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"has_dirty(m::UInt8, k::ManyUI.Dirty.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when k is set in m. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.has_dirty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.id-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"id(w::Widget) -> Symbol\n\n\nCSS id of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.id"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.insert_child!-Tuple{Widget, Int64, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"insert_child!(p::Widget, i::Int64, c::Widget) -> Widget\n\n\nMount c at index i. Returns p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.is_dirty-Tuple{Widget, ManyUI.Dirty.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"is_dirty(w::Widget, kind::ManyUI.Dirty.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when kind is set on w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_dirty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.is_dirty-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"is_dirty(w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when any of PAINT, LAYOUT or STYLE is set on w. Pure.\n\nDirty.SUBTREE alone is deliberately NOT dirt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_dirty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.is_focusable-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"is_focusable(w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w takes part in the tab order. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_focusable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.is_visible-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"is_visible(w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nFalse when w or its subtree is hidden. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_visible"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.layout_of-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"layout_of(w::Widget) -> LayoutBox\n\n\nThe four resolved boxes of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.layout_of"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.mark!","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mark!(w::Widget)\nmark!(w::Widget, kind::ManyUI.Dirty.T)\n\n\nE1. The full propagation – THE function Reactive and the widget API call. Rules, normative:\n\nPAINT  on w -> w ONLY. Never ancestors, never descendants.\nLAYOUT on w -> LAYOUT|PAINT on w AND ALL DESCENDANTS (their\n               absolute regions derive from w's, so they are\n               genuinely stale -- this is \"and its affected\n               descendants\" in the EARS text), then\n               `escalate_auto!(w)`.\nSTYLE  on w -> STYLE on w and all descendants (inheritance plus\n               descendant selectors).\n\nIn EVERY case each ANCESTOR receives Dirty.SUBTREE and NOTHING ELSE. Marking an ancestor LAYOUT would make E1 false as written.\n\nSIBLINGS ARE NEVER MARKED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mark!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.mark_dirty!","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mark_dirty!(w::Widget)\nmark_dirty!(w::Widget, kind::ManyUI.Dirty.T)\n\n\nE1. Set kind on w ONLY. Touches no ancestor and no descendant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mark_dirty!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.mark_subtree_dirty!-Tuple{Widget, ManyUI.Dirty.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mark_subtree_dirty!(w::Widget, kind::ManyUI.Dirty.T)\n\n\nE1. Set kind on w AND on every descendant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mark_subtree_dirty!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.mount!-Tuple{Widget, Vararg{Widget}}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mount!(p::Widget, cs::Widget...) -> Scrollpane\n\n\nMount several children in order. Returns p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.mount!-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mount!(p::Widget, c::Widget) -> Scrollpane\n\n\nAppend c to p, bind it to p's app, and call on_mount!. Returns p.\n\nThrows ArgumentError when c already has a parent: a widget lives in exactly one tree, and silently re-parenting it would leave the old parent's children stale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.mount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.node-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"node(w::Widget) -> WidgetNode\n\n\nTHE one required method per widget type. The default duck-types on the field name; override only for exotic widgets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.node"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"on_blur!(w::Widget)\n\n\nLifecycle hook: w has just lost focus. Default no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"on_focus!(w::Widget)\n\n\nLifecycle hook: w has just gained focus. Default: reveal!(w), so TAB-ing to a widget inside a scrolling viewport scrolls it into view with no wiring at the call site.\n\nOVERRIDING THIS REPLACES IT: a widget type with its own on_focus! MUST call reveal!(w) itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.on_mount!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"on_mount!(w::Widget)\n\n\nLifecycle hook: w has just been added to a tree. Default no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_mount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.on_unmount!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"on_unmount!(w::Widget)\n\n\nLifecycle hook: w is about to leave its tree. Default no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_unmount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.paint_offset-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"paint_offset(w::Widget) -> Offset\n\n\nThe accumulated shift w is PAINTED with: the sum of every STRICT ancestor's scroll, negated. ORIGIN outside any scrolled subtree.\n\nA node's OWN scroll is deliberately not included – it shifts that node's children, not itself. O(depth), and never on the frame path: _paint_node! threads the same value down for free. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.paint_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.painted_region-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"painted_region(w::Widget) -> Region\n\n\nWhere w's border box is actually PAINTED: region(w) shifted by paint_offset(w). Identical to region(w) outside a scrolled subtree.\n\nTHIS, not region(w), is what a hit test must compare a pointer against: layout computes ABSOLUTE regions and knows nothing about scrolling, so region(w) is where w WOULD be if nothing had scrolled. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.painted_region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.path_from_root-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"path_from_root(w::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nRoot to w, inclusive of both. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.path_from_root"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.query-Tuple{Widget, AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"query(root::Widget, sel::AbstractString) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nEvery widget in root's tree matching the CSS selector sel, in pre-order. Pure.\n\nSupports *, #id, .class, Type, compounds of those (Button.primary#ok) and comma-separated lists. Combinators live in css.jl; query is the dependency-free subset this layer can afford.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.query"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.query_one-Tuple{Widget, AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"query_one(\n    root::Widget,\n    sel::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nThe first widget matching sel, or nothing. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.query_one"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.query_one-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Widget, AbstractString, Type{T}}} where T<:Widget","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"query_one(\n    root::Widget,\n    sel::AbstractString,\n    _::Type{T<:Widget}\n) -> Widget\n\n\nThe first widget matching sel, asserted to be a T. Throws when absent or of the wrong type. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.query_one"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.region-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"region(w::Widget) -> Region\n\n\nnode(w).layout.border_box. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.remove_class!-Tuple{Widget, Symbol}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"remove_class!(w::Widget, c::Symbol) -> Widget\n\n\nRemove a class and mark Dirty.STYLE. Removing an absent class is a no-op. Returns w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.remove_class!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.replace_child!-Tuple{Widget, Widget, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"replace_child!(\n    p::Widget,\n    old::Widget,\n    new::Widget\n) -> Widget\n\n\nSwap old for new in place. Returns p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.replace_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.reveal!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"reveal!(w::Widget)\n\n\nAsk every ancestor of w, NEAREST FIRST, to bring w into view.\n\nNearest-first is load-bearing: an inner pane must finish moving before an outer pane measures where w ended up. Never on the frame path – focus changes are events, not frames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.reveal!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.reveal_child!-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"reveal_child!(_::Widget, _::Widget)\n\n\nHook: bring d, a descendant of w, into w's visible area. Default no-op; a scrolling viewport overrides it.\n\nThe core therefore knows that a node MAY be able to reveal a descendant, and nothing whatever about how – there is no isa Scrollpane anywhere below layer 7.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.reveal_child!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.root_of-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"root_of(w::Widget) -> Widget\n\n\nThe root of w's tree; w itself when it has no parent. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.root_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.scroll_into_view-NTuple{4, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"scroll_into_view(\n    pos::Int64,\n    viewport::Int64,\n    lo::Int64,\n    hi::Int64\n) -> Int64\n\n\nThe scroll position NEAREST pos that puts the 0-based inclusive extent lo:hi inside a viewport-cell window starting at pos. Pure.\n\nMINIMAL MOVEMENT: already-visible content does not move. When the extent is LARGER than the viewport its START wins – showing the top of an over-long item beats showing its bottom. Returns pos unchanged for a non-positive viewport (not laid out yet).\n\nThe asymmetry is deliberate and clamp cannot express it: clamp with an inverted lo/hi silently picks the wrong edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_into_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.scroll_of-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"scroll_of(w::Widget) -> Offset\n\n\nnode(w).scroll. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.scroll_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.set_dirty-Tuple{UInt8, ManyUI.Dirty.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"set_dirty(m::UInt8, k::ManyUI.Dirty.T) -> UInt8\n\n\nm with k set. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_dirty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.set_scroll!-Tuple{Widget, Offset}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"set_scroll!(w::Widget, o::Offset) -> Bool\n\n\nSet the shift applied to w's CHILDREN at paint time. Returns true iff it changed.\n\nClamped at ZERO per axis only. The UPPER bound needs the content extent, which this layer cannot see – scroll_to! in widgets/scroll.jl owns it. An over-scroll is a UX bug (blank cells), never corruption.\n\nMarks Dirty.PAINT and NOTHING ELSE, and this is the whole \"a wheel tick does not re-run layout\" claim: mark! with PAINT sets PAINT on w alone and drops only SUBTREE breadcrumbs on ancestors; it calls escalate_auto! ONLY for Dirty.LAYOUT. dirty_root then finds no real LAYOUT and returns nothing, so relayout! returns on its first line – and compute_layout never reads this field anyway.\n\nmark!(w, Dirty.PAINT) and NOT mark_subtree_dirty!: the normative rule above is \"PAINT on w -> w ONLY\", and frame! repaints the whole tree unconditionally in any case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_scroll!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.set_visible!-Tuple{Widget, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"set_visible!(w::Widget, v::Bool)\n\n\nShow or hide w and mark Dirty.LAYOUT. Setting the value w already has is a no-op.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_visible!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.toggle_class!-Tuple{Widget, Symbol}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"toggle_class!(w::Widget, c::Symbol) -> Bool\n\n\nToggle a class and mark Dirty.STYLE. Returns the new membership.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle_class!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.type_name-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"type_name(w::Widget) -> Symbol\n\n\nType name of w, as CSS type selectors see it. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.type_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.unmount!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"unmount!(c::Widget) -> Widget\n\n\nCall on_unmount! and detach c from its parent. Returns c.\n\nThe detached subtree keeps its own structure and is left dirty-clean; the OLD parent is marked Dirty.LAYOUT, because its remaining children restack.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.unmount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.walk-Tuple{Any, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"walk(f, w::Widget)\n\n\nCall f on w and every descendant, pre-order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.walk"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.walk_visible-Tuple{Any, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"walk_visible(f, w::Widget)\n\n\nLike walk, but skips invisible subtrees entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.walk_visible"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.Reactive","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"mutable struct Reactive{T}\n\nA reactive cell.\n\nAssigning a value that differs (!=) from the current one marks owner dirty with kind AND posts a RefreshEvent, so a write from a worker task actually repaints.\n\nConcretely typed: a widget field count::Reactive{Int} stays type-stable. owner is a small union, NOT Any.\n\nlabel.text[]          # read,  zero cost\nlabel.text[] = \"hi\"   # write, marks dirty, schedules a frame\n\nChoosing Dirty.PAINT for state that can change size is a correctness bug, so the default is the conservative Dirty.LAYOUT.\n\nFields\n\nvalue::Any: The current value.\nowner::Union{Nothing, Widget}: The widget marked dirty on a real change.\nkind::ManyUI.Dirty.T\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Reactive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.Reactive-Tuple{T} where T","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"An unbound cell holding v.\n\nThe parametric form pins T; the plain form infers it from v. Both default to the conservative Dirty.LAYOUT. Call bind_owner! (or attach_reactives!) to give the cell a widget to invalidate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Reactive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.getindex-Union{Tuple{Reactive{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"getindex(r::Reactive{T}) -> Any\n\n\nRead the value. Type-stable and allocation-free. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.setindex!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Reactive{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"setindex!(r::Reactive{T}, v) -> Any\n\n\nWrite the value, converting to T first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.setindex!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Reactive{T}, T}} where T","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"setindex!(r::Reactive{T}, v) -> Any\n\n\nE1. Write the value.\n\nNO-OP – no dirty mark, no RefreshEvent – when v == r.value. The == guard means a redundant write costs one comparison and zero frames.\n\nOn a real change: r.value = v; mark!(r.owner, r.kind); and when app(r.owner) !== nothing, post!(app, RefreshEvent()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.setindex!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.attach_reactives!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"attach_reactives!(w::Widget)\n\n\nWire every Reactive field of w to w. Call at the END of a widget constructor.\n\nUses fieldnames plus getfield, and skips non-Reactive fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.attach_reactives!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.bind_owner!-Tuple{Reactive, Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"bind_owner!(r::Reactive, w::Widget)\n\n\nPoint r at the widget it invalidates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.bind_owner!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.Border","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"struct Border\n\nA border: a line style plus the style its glyphs are drawn in. isbits.\n\nFields\n\nkind::ManyUI.BorderKind.T: Line style.\nstyle::Style: Style the glyphs are painted with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Border"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.BoxPatch","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"struct BoxPatch\n\nThe optional-every-field twin of BoxStyle: nothing means \"not specified\".\n\nDeliberately NOT isbits, and that is fine – it exists only at parse and cascade time, never in the render loop.\n\nFields\n\ndisplay::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Display.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.display when set.\ndirection::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Direction.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.direction when set.\njustify::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Justify.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.justify when set.\nalign::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Align.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.align when set.\nwidth::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.width when set.\nheight::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.height when set.\nmin_width::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.min_width when set.\nmin_height::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.min_height when set.\nmax_width::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.max_width when set.\nmax_height::Union{Nothing, Length}: Overrides BoxStyle.max_height when set.\nmargin::Union{Nothing, Spacing}: Overrides BoxStyle.margin when set.\npadding::Union{Nothing, Spacing}: Overrides BoxStyle.padding when set.\nborder::Union{Nothing, Border}: Overrides BoxStyle.border when set.\ngap::Union{Nothing, Int64}: Overrides BoxStyle.gap when set.\noverflow_x::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Overflow.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.overflow_x when set.\noverflow_y::Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Overflow.T}: Overrides BoxStyle.overflow_y when set.\ngrow::Union{Nothing, Float32}: Overrides BoxStyle.grow when set.\nshrink::Union{Nothing, Float32}: Overrides BoxStyle.shrink when set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BoxPatch"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.BoxPatch-Tuple{}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"BoxPatch(; kwargs...) -> BoxPatch\n\n\nKeyword constructor; every field defaults to nothing.\n\nAn unknown keyword throws ArgumentError: a stylesheet property that silently patched nothing would be invisible at every layer above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BoxPatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.BoxStyle","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"struct BoxStyle\n\nThe cascade's output and the layout engine's input. isbits, and it lives inline in WidgetNode – no pointer chasing during layout.\n\nFields\n\ndisplay::ManyUI.Display.T: Whether and how the box participates in layout.\ndirection::ManyUI.Direction.T: Main axis of the flex container.\njustify::ManyUI.Justify.T: Main-axis distribution of the children.\nalign::ManyUI.Align.T: Cross-axis alignment of the children.\nwidth::Length: Preferred width.\nheight::Length: Preferred height.\nmin_width::Length: Lower width bound.\nmin_height::Length: Lower height bound.\nmax_width::Length: Upper width bound.\nmax_height::Length: Upper height bound.\nmargin::Spacing: Space outside the border box.\npadding::Spacing: Space between the border and the content.\nborder::Border: Border drawn on the perimeter of the border box.\ngap::Int64: Cells inserted between adjacent children.\noverflow_x::ManyUI.Overflow.T: Horizontal overflow policy.\noverflow_y::ManyUI.Overflow.T: Vertical overflow policy.\ngrow::Float32: Flex grow factor.\nshrink::Float32: Flex shrink factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BoxStyle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.BoxStyle-Tuple{BoxStyle}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"BoxStyle(base::BoxStyle; kwargs...) -> BoxStyle\n\n\nFunctional update: a copy of base with the given fields replaced. Pure.\n\nAn unknown keyword throws ArgumentError rather than being ignored: a silently dropped BoxStyle(b; witdh = cells(3)) is a layout bug with no symptom at the call site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BoxStyle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.BoxStyle-Tuple{}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"BoxStyle(\n;\n    display,\n    direction,\n    justify,\n    align,\n    width,\n    height,\n    min_width,\n    min_height,\n    max_width,\n    max_height,\n    margin,\n    padding,\n    border,\n    gap,\n    overflow_x,\n    overflow_y,\n    grow,\n    shrink\n) -> BoxStyle\n\n\nKeyword constructor with the documented defaults.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BoxStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.LayoutBox","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"struct LayoutBox\n\nThe four resolved boxes of one node, in ABSOLUTE 1-based screen coordinates.\n\nINVARIANT: margin_box includes border_box includes padding_box includes content.\n\nFields\n\nmargin_box::Region: Outermost box; includes the margin.\nborder_box::Region: margin_box shrunk by the margin. The border is drawn ON its     perimeter.\npadding_box::Region: border_box shrunk by the border thickness.\ncontent::Region: padding_box shrunk by the padding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.LayoutBox"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.Length","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"struct Length\n\nA resolvable length. isbits.\n\nFields\n\nkind::ManyUI.Dimension.T: How to read value.\nvalue::Float32: Cells, percent, or fr weight, per kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.isempty-Tuple{BoxPatch}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"isempty(p::BoxPatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when p sets no field at all. Pure.\n\nBoxPatch is an immutable struct, so === is structural (field-wise egal, recursively) rather than pointer identity – comparing against the all-nothing value is exactly \"sets no field\", in one shot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isempty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.merge-Tuple{BoxPatch, BoxPatch}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"merge(a::BoxPatch, b::BoxPatch) -> BoxPatch\n\n\nMerge two patches: b wins wherever it is set. Pure.\n\nA monoid with identity BOX_PATCH_NONE, matching merge(::Style, ::Style). This is the box half of the cascade fold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Length}, AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"parse(_::Type{Length}, s::AbstractString) -> Length\n\n\nParse a length, throwing ArgumentError on garbage. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#Base.tryparse-Tuple{Type{Length}, AbstractString}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"tryparse(\n    _::Type{Length},\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, Length}\n\n\nParse a length, returning nothing instead of throwing.\n\nAccepts \"auto\", \"10\", \"10cells\", \"50%\", \"1fr\", \"3fr\". Case-insensitive; surrounding whitespace is ignored. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.tryparse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.apply-Tuple{BoxStyle, BoxPatch}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"apply(base::BoxStyle, p::BoxPatch) -> BoxStyle\n\n\nApply p onto base: p wins wherever it is set. Pure.\n\nThe identity is BOX_PATCH_NONE: apply(b, BOX_PATCH_NONE) === b.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.apply"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.border_glyphs-Tuple{ManyUI.BorderKind.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"border_glyphs(k::ManyUI.BorderKind.T) -> NTuple{8, Char}\n\n\nThe eight glyphs of a border kind: (tl, top, tr, right, br, bottom, bl, left) – clockwise from the top-left. Pure.\n\nNONE and BLANK both yield spaces. They differ in thickness, not in their glyphs: NONE reserves no cells and is never drawn, whereas BLANK reserves its cells and paints them blank.\n\nEvery glyph is width-1 by construction (verified in test_paint.jl); a width-2 glyph here would desynchronise the grid on every border.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.border_glyphs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.box_overhead-Tuple{BoxStyle}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"box_overhead(bs::BoxStyle) -> Spacing\n\n\nTotal non-content spacing: margin + border + padding. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.box_overhead"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.cells-Tuple{Real}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"cells(n::Real) -> Length\n\n\nAn absolute length in cells. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.cells"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.definite_size-Tuple{Length, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"definite_size(d::Length, available::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nResolve a definite length against available. CELLS and PERCENT only; PERCENT rounds via round(Int). Pure.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for AUTO and FRACTION: neither can be resolved without measuring content or knowing the free space, so a silent 0 would be a layout bug that never announces itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.definite_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.fr-Tuple{Real}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"fr(n::Real) -> Length\n\n\nA fraction of the remaining free space. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.fr"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.is_definite-Tuple{Length}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"is_definite(d::Length) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue for CELLS and PERCENT – the kinds resolvable without measuring content. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_definite"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.layout_box-Tuple{BoxStyle, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"layout_box(bs::BoxStyle, margin_box::Region) -> LayoutBox\n\n\nDerive all four boxes from an outer margin_box and a BoxStyle.\n\nTHE single definition of the box model: no other file may re-derive these. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.layout_box"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.outer_size-Tuple{BoxStyle, Size}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"outer_size(bs::BoxStyle, inner::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe margin-box size needed to hold a content box of inner. Pure.\n\nThis is the sole content-box -> outer-box conversion in the package, and so it is what pins BoxStyle.width/height to the CONTENT box (the CSS box-sizing: content-box default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.outer_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.pct-Tuple{Real}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"pct(n::Real) -> Length\n\n\nA percentage of the parent's content box. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.pct"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.thickness-Tuple{Border}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"thickness(b::Border) -> Spacing\n\n\nCells the border occupies per edge: NO_SPACING for NONE, one cell on every edge otherwise (BLANK included). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.thickness"},{"category":"type","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.LayoutMap","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"The pure layout pass's output: a node to its four resolved boxes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.LayoutMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._apportion-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_apportion(\n    weights::AbstractVector{<:Real},\n    total::Int64\n) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nShare total cells out over weights, largest-remainder-first with ties going to the LOWER index, so sum(result) == total exactly whenever sum(weights) > 0. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._apportion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._arrange!-Tuple{IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox}, Widget, BoxStyle, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_arrange!(\n    lm::IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox},\n    w::Widget,\n    bs::BoxStyle,\n    content::Region\n)\n\n\nSize and place every laid-out child of w inside content, then recurse. Pure with respect to the tree. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._arrange!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._bound_outer-Tuple{Length, Int64, Int64, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_bound_outer(\n    len::Length,\n    over::Int64,\n    ref::Int64,\n    lower::Bool\n) -> Int64\n\n\nResolve a min_*/max_* bound to an OUTER extent. An AUTO bound means \"unbounded\": 0 for a lower bound, typemax(Int) for an upper one. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._bound_outer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._content_extent-Tuple{Widget, Length, Bool, Bool, Size}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_content_extent(\n    w::Widget,\n    len::Length,\n    horiz::Bool,\n    main::Bool,\n    avail::Size\n) -> Int64\n\n\nResolve one axis of w to a CONTENT extent against the parent content box avail. FRACTION contributes nothing here – it claims free space later. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._cross_inset-Tuple{Spacing, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_cross_inset(sp::Spacing, horiz::Bool) -> Int64\n\n\nCross-axis component of a Spacing. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cross_inset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._cross_length-Tuple{BoxStyle, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_cross_length(bs::BoxStyle, horiz::Bool) -> Length\n\n\nThe Length sizing the cross axis of bs. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cross_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._cross_of-Tuple{Size, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_cross_of(s::Size, horiz::Bool) -> Int64\n\n\nCross-axis extent of s. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cross_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._is_reverse-Tuple{ManyUI.Direction.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_is_reverse(d::ManyUI.Direction.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when d runs the main axis backwards. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_reverse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._is_row-Tuple{ManyUI.Direction.T}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_is_row(d::ManyUI.Direction.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when the main axis of d is horizontal. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._laid_out-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_laid_out(w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w takes part in layout at all. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._laid_out"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_direction-Tuple{BoxStyle}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_direction(bs::BoxStyle) -> ManyUI.Direction.T\n\n\nThe direction a container actually arranges its children along: Display.BLOCK is COLUMN whatever direction says. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_inset-Tuple{Spacing, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_inset(sp::Spacing, horiz::Bool) -> Int64\n\n\nMain-axis component of a Spacing. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_inset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_length-Tuple{BoxStyle, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_length(bs::BoxStyle, horiz::Bool) -> Length\n\n\nThe Length sizing the main axis of bs. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._main_of-Tuple{Size, Bool}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_main_of(s::Size, horiz::Bool) -> Int64\n\n\nMain-axis extent of s. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._main_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._measure_outer-Tuple{Widget, Size}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_measure_outer(w::Widget, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe margin-box size w wants, given the parent content box avail. Used by the default measure. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._measure_outer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._place!-Tuple{IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox}, Widget, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_place!(\n    lm::IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox},\n    w::Widget,\n    margin_box::Region\n)\n\n\nRecord the four boxes of w in lm, then arrange its children. Pure with respect to the tree: lm is the only thing written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._place!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI._split_even-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"_split_even(total::Int64, k::Int64) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nSplit total cells into k as-equal-as-possible parts, the remainder going to the lowest indices. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._split_even"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.apply_layout!-Tuple{IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox}}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"apply_layout!(lm::IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox})\n\n\nThe impure shell: write lm into each WidgetNode.layout, clear Dirty.LAYOUT and Dirty.SUBTREE on every written node, and set Dirty.PAINT on any node whose LayoutBox actually CHANGED.\n\nNodes absent from lm are left untouched, breadcrumbs included: an ancestor's SUBTREE may still be carrying a PAINT-dirty descendant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.apply_layout!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.compute_layout-Tuple{Widget, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"compute_layout(\n    root::Widget,\n    viewport::Region\n) -> IdDict{Widget, LayoutBox}\n\n\nU2 + E4. PURE: tree plus viewport to boxes. Mutates NOTHING, and does not touch WidgetNode.layout. This is what makes the layout engine testable without an App, a Buffer, or a terminal.\n\nviewport is root's MARGIN box, so root's own width/height are not consulted: the root fills what it is given. Every other node's margin box is derived from its parent's CONTENT box, and layout_box(bs, margin_box) derives all four regions – no file may re-derive them.\n\nTwo passes per container:\n\nMEASURE (bottom-up): resolve CELLS/PERCENT against the parent content box; AUTO calls measure(child, avail). Every Length sizes the CONTENT box, and box_overhead is added on top of it to reach the margin box that the flex kernel distributes over.\nDISTRIBUTE (top-down): FRACTION items claim free space pro-rata by fr weight AFTER 1 and BEFORE grow; flex_distribute applies grow/shrink to the residual, clamped by min_*/max_*; justify_offsets places along the main axis; cross_align places and stretches along the cross axis.\n\nDisplay.BLOCK is FLEX with direction COLUMN, align STRETCH and the children's grow forced to zero – ONE code path, no special case. Display.NONE and invisible subtrees are ABSENT from the returned map and claim no main-axis space.\n\nOverflow is NOT clipped here: the map reports true geometry under every Overflow policy, and paint.jl clips through a BufferView.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.compute_layout"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.cross_align-Tuple{Int64, ManyUI.Align.T, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"cross_align(\n    size::Int64,\n    align::ManyUI.Align.T,\n    available::Int64\n) -> Tuple{Int64, Int64}\n\n\nCross-axis (offset, size) for one item. Align.STRETCH returns (0, available). An item larger than available is given offset zero under every mode rather than a negative offset. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.cross_align"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.flex_distribute-Tuple{Vector{Int64}, Vector{Float32}, Vector{Float32}, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"flex_distribute(\n    base::Vector{Int64},\n    grow::Vector{Float32},\n    shrink::Vector{Float32},\n    available::Int64\n) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nTHE heart of flex. Pure. Distributes available over the base sizes using the grow/shrink factors.\n\nWhen sum(base) < available the slack is shared out by grow weight. When sum(base) > available the excess is taken back by CSS SCALED shrink weight (shrink[i] * base[i]); an item whose share exceeds its own size freezes at zero rather than going negative, and the residue is re-offered to the items still able to shrink. When no item can absorb the slack at all the base sizes are returned unchanged – an overflowing layout is clipped at paint time, never mangled here.\n\nNORMATIVE: fractional leftovers resolve LARGEST-REMAINDER-FIRST, so sum(result) == available EXACTLY whenever any item can absorb the slack. Integer cells: no one-cell drift, ever. Ties in the remainder go to the LOWER index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.flex_distribute"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.justify_offsets-Tuple{Vector{Int64}, ManyUI.Justify.T, Int64, Int64}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"justify_offsets(\n    sizes::Vector{Int64},\n    justify::ManyUI.Justify.T,\n    gap::Int64,\n    available::Int64\n) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nMain-axis start offsets, 0-based within the container's content box, for each item, given the item sizes, gap and total available. Pure.\n\nThe free space is available - sum(sizes) - gap * (n - 1), CLAMPED AT ZERO: when the items overflow, every mode degenerates to START rather than placing an item at a negative offset. Free space is shared out largest-remainder-first, so the trailing edge never drifts a cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.justify_offsets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.layout!-Tuple{Widget, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"layout!(root::Widget, viewport::Region)\n\n\nU2 + E4. An unconditional FULL pass over the whole tree; assigns layout on every visible node.\n\nThis is what a ResizeEvent calls – \"instantly recalculate the bounding boxes of the ENTIRE layout tree\". Never consults dirty flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.layout!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{Widget, Size}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"measure(w::Widget, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nIntrinsic content size of w given the available space.\n\nOverride for leaf widgets – a Label returns its wrapped text extent via text_width. The default is the union of the children's OUTER (margin-box) measures laid out along display/direction, with gap between them; a widget with no laid-out child therefore measures Size(0, 0). Drives Dimension.AUTO.\n\navail is the CONTENT size of the parent, and it is what PERCENT child lengths resolve against.\n\nPure with respect to the tree: no dirty marks, no field writes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/tree/#ManyUI.relayout!-Tuple{Widget, Region}","page":"Widget tree, box model and layout","text":"relayout!(root::Widget, viewport::Region)\n\n\nE1 + U2. INCREMENTAL: recomputes from dirty_root(root) down ONLY.\n\ndirty_root(root) === nothing – no-op, returns immediately.\ndirty_root(root) === root – falls back to layout!.\notherwise – compute_layout on the dirty subtree, anchored at the subtree root's EXISTING margin_box, then apply_layout!.\n\nTwo functions, not one with a force::Bool keyword: E4 mandates a full recompute on resize, E1 mandates a minimal one on state change. Two requirements, two functions, two test suites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.relayout!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/app/#App-and-event-loop","page":"App and event loop","text":"","title":"App and event loop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/app/#ManyUI._precompile!-Tuple{}","page":"App and event loop","text":"_precompile!()\n\n\nForce compilation of the hot render path at precompile time.\n\nCalled once at the bottom of the module. Every directive is best-effort: a precompile call that fails to resolve returns false and is ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._precompile!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/events/#Events,-input-and-dispatch","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"","title":"Events, input and dispatch"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._KEY_NAMES","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"Every accepted key NAME, already lowercased. Keys not listed here are single characters, which are taken literally (case included) – only names fold case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._KEY_NAMES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._WHEEL_BUTTONS","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"The four wheel pseudo-buttons. Scroll-ness is a property of the button, never of the action.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._WHEEL_BUTTONS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.Dispatch","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"mutable struct Dispatch{E<:Event}\n\nMutable envelope around an IMMUTABLE event.\n\nConsumption state lives here, so an event can be replayed, logged, or fed to a second tree without contamination. Parametric on E so handlers dispatch on the concrete event type with no boxing.\n\nFields\n\nevent::Event\ntarget::Widget\ncurrent::Widget: The widget currently being visited.\nphase::ManyUI.Phase.T: Capture, at-target or bubble.\nconsumed::Bool: Set by consume!; stops propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Dispatch"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.Dispatch-Union{Tuple{E}, Tuple{E, Widget}} where E<:Event","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"Envelope e for target, starting in Phase.CAPTURE, unconsumed.\n\ncurrent starts at target so an envelope that is never walked still reads consistently; a walk overwrites it before each on_event!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Dispatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.FocusEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct FocusEvent <: Event\n\nThe terminal or browser tab gained or lost focus.\n\nFields\n\ngained::Bool: True on focus gained, false on focus lost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.FocusEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.KeyEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct KeyEvent <: Event\n\nA keystroke. char is meaningful only when code === Key.CHAR; otherwise it is '\\0'.\n\nFields\n\ncode::ManyUI.Key.T: Logical key.\nchar::Char: Character produced, when code === Key.CHAR.\nmods::Modifiers: Active modifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.KeyEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.Modifiers","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct Modifiers\n\nA bitset of Modifier.T values. isbits.\n\nFields\n\nbits::UInt8: Bitwise OR of the active Modifier.T values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Modifiers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.Modifiers-Tuple{Vararg{ManyUI.Modifier.T}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"Modifiers(ms::ManyUI.Modifier.T...) -> Modifiers\n\n\nThe bitset of the given modifiers. Duplicates collapse and the argument order is irrelevant: this is a set union. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Modifiers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.Modifiers-Tuple{}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"Modifiers() -> Modifiers\n\n\nNo modifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Modifiers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.MouseEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct MouseEvent <: Event\n\nA mouse action. x/y are 1-based ABSOLUTE screen cells; widget-local coordinates come from local_offset(d), never hand-rolled at the call site.\n\nFields\n\naction::ManyUI.MouseAction.T: Press, release, move or drag.\nbutton::ManyUI.MouseButton.T: Button involved, or a wheel direction.\nx::Int64: Absolute 1-based column.\ny::Int64: Absolute 1-based row.\nmods::Modifiers: Active modifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.MouseEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.PasteEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct PasteEvent <: Event\n\nA completed bracketed paste.\n\nFields\n\ntext::String: The pasted text, verbatim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.PasteEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.QuitEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct QuitEvent <: Event\n\nRequests loop shutdown. handle! sets app.running = false.\n\nFields\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.QuitEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.RefreshEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct RefreshEvent <: Event\n\nForces a full repaint. THE reconnect / wake seam: because it travels the same Channel{Event} as everything else, events(d) really is the only way into the App. handle!(app, ::RefreshEvent) calls invalidate! internally.\n\nFields\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RefreshEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.ResizeEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct ResizeEvent <: Event\n\nThe renderable area changed. Never parsed from bytes: injected by a driver through notify_resize!.\n\nFields\n\nsize::Size: The new renderable area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ResizeEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.TickEvent","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"struct TickEvent <: Event\n\nA timer tick.\n\nFields\n\ntime::Float64: Wall clock stamp of the tick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TickEvent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.:|-Tuple{Modifiers, ManyUI.Modifier.T}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"|(a::Modifiers, b::ManyUI.Modifier.T) -> Modifiers\n\n\nAdd a modifier to the set. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:|"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.:|-Tuple{Modifiers, Modifiers}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"|(a::Modifiers, b::Modifiers) -> Modifiers\n\n\nUnion of two modifier sets. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:|"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.in-Tuple{ManyUI.Modifier.T, Modifiers}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"in(m::ManyUI.Modifier.T, ms::Modifiers) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when m is in the set. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.isempty-Tuple{Modifiers}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"isempty(ms::Modifiers) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when no modifier is active. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isempty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{KeyEvent}, AbstractString}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"parse(_::Type{KeyEvent}, s::AbstractString) -> KeyEvent\n\n\nParse a key description.\n\nAccepts \"q\", \"ctrl+c\", \"shift+tab\", \"f1\", \"alt+enter\", \"ctrl+shift+left\", \"escape\", \"space\". Case-insensitive on names. Throws ArgumentError on garbage. This is what makes every binding and every press! test a one-liner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#Base.tryparse-Tuple{Type{KeyEvent}, AbstractString}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"tryparse(\n    _::Type{KeyEvent},\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, KeyEvent}\n\n\nParse a key description, returning nothing instead of throwing. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.tryparse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._mods-Tuple{Bool, Bool, Bool}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_mods(ctrl::Bool, alt::Bool, shift::Bool) -> Modifiers\n\n\nThe modifier bitset for the three keyword flags key and the key parser share. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._mods"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._parse_key_name-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_parse_key_name(\n    name::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, Tuple{ManyUI.Key.T, Any}}\n\n\nThe (code, char) a key name denotes, or nothing. A one-character name is a literal character; anything longer must be in _KEY_NAMES. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_key_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._parse_modifier-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_parse_modifier(\n    name::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, ManyUI.Modifier.T}\n\n\nThe Modifier.T a name denotes, or nothing. Case-insensitive. The four canonical names only – an unknown name is garbage, not a key. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_modifier"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._split_key_spec-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_split_key_spec(\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, Tuple{Union{BitVector, Vector}, String}}\n\n\nSplit a key description into its modifier names and its key name, or nothing when the shape is garbage.\n\n+ is both the separator and a legitimate key, so a trailing + is the key itself: \"+\" is plus, \"ctrl++\" is ctrl plus. \"ctrl+\" is ambiguous and is therefore rejected rather than guessed at. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._split_key_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.consume!-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"consume!(d::Dispatch)\n\n\nStop propagation. One-way: an event cannot be un-consumed, which is why a walk need only ever test is_consumed, never a phase-specific flag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.consume!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.event-Union{Tuple{Dispatch{E}}, Tuple{E}} where E","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"event(d::Dispatch{E}) -> Any\n\n\nThe wrapped event. Type-stable accessor. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.event"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.is_consumed-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"is_consumed(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue once consume! has been called. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_consumed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.is_scroll-Tuple{MouseEvent}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"is_scroll(e::MouseEvent) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue for the four wheel pseudo-buttons. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_scroll"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.key-Tuple{Char}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"key(c::Char; ctrl, alt, shift) -> KeyEvent\n\n\nA character keystroke. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.key-Tuple{ManyUI.Key.T}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"key(k::ManyUI.Key.T; ctrl, alt, shift) -> KeyEvent\n\n\nA non-character keystroke. char is '\\0', because it is meaningful only when code === Key.CHAR. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.local_offset-Tuple{Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"local_offset(d::Dispatch{MouseEvent}) -> Offset\n\n\nThe event position relative to region(d.current), as 1-based widget-local coordinates. Defined only for Dispatch{MouseEvent}.\n\nRelative to current, NOT to target: a capture-phase handler on an ancestor gets the point in ITS own box. Out-of-box points are returned as-is, never clamped – the caller decides what a miss means. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.local_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{Widget, Dispatch}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"on_event!(w::Widget, d::Dispatch)\n\n\nE3. THE handler hook, with a default no-op fallback. Users add methods on (their widget type, their event type):\n\non_event!(b::MyButton, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent}) = ...\n\nDispatch on both axes – no callback registry, no Dict{Symbol,Function}, no closures. Call consume!(d) to stop propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._path_within-Tuple{Widget, Widget}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_path_within(root::Widget, target::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\npropagation_path(target) truncated to begin at root, so a subtree can be propagated through without the event reaching the widgets above it.\n\nFalls back to the full path when root is not an ancestor of target. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._path_within"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._route-Tuple{Widget, Event, Union{Nothing, Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_route(\n    root::Widget,\n    _::Event,\n    _::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n) -> Widget\n\n\nEverything else – resize, focus, tick and any user-defined Event – is a whole-tree concern and goes to root, focus notwithstanding. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._route"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._route-Tuple{Widget, KeyEvent, Union{Nothing, Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_route(\n    root::Widget,\n    _::KeyEvent,\n    focus::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n) -> Widget\n\n\nKeystrokes go to the focused widget, and to root when nothing has focus. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._route"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._route-Tuple{Widget, MouseEvent, Union{Nothing, Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_route(\n    root::Widget,\n    e::MouseEvent,\n    _::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n) -> Widget\n\n\nA mouse event goes to whatever is under the pointer, and to root when the pointer is outside the tree entirely. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._route"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._route-Tuple{Widget, PasteEvent, Union{Nothing, Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_route(\n    root::Widget,\n    _::PasteEvent,\n    focus::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n) -> Widget\n\n\nA paste goes wherever a keystroke would. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._route"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._route-Tuple{Widget, Union{QuitEvent, RefreshEvent}, Union{Nothing, Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_route(\n    _::Widget,\n    _::Union{QuitEvent, RefreshEvent},\n    _::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n)\n\n\nRefreshEvent and QuitEvent are App-level only: the tree never sees them. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._route"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI._walk!-Tuple{Dispatch, Vector{Widget}}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"_walk!(d::Dispatch, path::Vector{Widget}) -> Bool\n\n\nThe capture, at-target and bubble walk over path, which runs root first and ends at path[1]. Stops the instant the event is consumed. Returns true iff it was. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._walk!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.dispatch_event!","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"dispatch_event!(root::Widget, e::Event) -> Bool\ndispatch_event!(\n    root::Widget,\n    e::Event,\n    focus::Union{Nothing, Widget}\n) -> Bool\n\n\nE3. Route, then propagate. Returns true iff consumed.\n\nNORMATIVE routing table:\n\nMouseEvent            -> `hit_test(root, e.x, e.y)`, falling back\n                         to `root` when nothing is hit\nKeyEvent, PasteEvent  -> `focus`, falling back to `root` when\n                         `focus === nothing`\nResizeEvent,\nFocusEvent, TickEvent -> `root`\nRefreshEvent,\nQuitEvent             -> NOT ROUTED (App-level only; returns false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.dispatch_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.focusable_widgets-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"focusable_widgets(root::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nThe tab order: every visible, focusable widget in pre-order. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.focusable_widgets"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.hit_test-Tuple{Widget, Int64, Int64}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"hit_test(\n    root::Widget,\n    x::Int64,\n    y::Int64\n) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nThe deepest VISIBLE widget whose painted_region contains the point, or nothing when nothing is hit.\n\nPre-order descent; Display.NONE and invisible subtrees are skipped entirely. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.hit_test"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.hit_test-Tuple{Widget, Offset}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"hit_test(root::Widget, o::Offset) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nThe deepest VISIBLE widget whose painted_region contains o. Pure.\n\npainted_region, NOT region: layout computes ABSOLUTE boxes and knows nothing about scrolling, so inside a scrolled subtree region(w) is where w WOULD be at scroll zero, while the pointer names a cell on the SCREEN. Comparing against the unshifted box targets whatever happens to occupy w's unscrolled slot – with a pane scrolled to y = 3, row 1 displays L4 but the click lands on L1.\n\nThe two agree exactly outside a scrolled subtree (paint_offset is then ORIGIN), so this is a no-op for every unscrolled tree. It costs O(depth) per node visited, on the MOUSE path only, never on the frame path – _paint_node! threads the same shift down for free and does not pay this.\n\nClipping needs no separate test: a node scrolled out of its pane is only reached by descending THROUGH the pane, whose own box is unshifted and already rejected the point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.hit_test"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.propagate!-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"propagate!(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nE3. Propagate an already-built envelope. Returns true iff consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.propagate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.propagate!-Tuple{Widget, Widget, Event}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"propagate!(root::Widget, target::Widget, e::Event) -> Bool\n\n\nE3. A pure TREE WALK – it knows nothing about App or focus. Returns true iff the event was consumed.\n\npath = propagation_path(target)\nCAPTURE: walk the path root to target, EXCLUDING target, with d.phase = Phase.CAPTURE\nATTARGET: `d.phase = Phase.ATTARGET`, call on target\nBUBBLE: walk the path target to root, EXCLUDING target, with d.phase = Phase.BUBBLE\n\nd.current is set to each visited widget before on_event!(w, d), and the walk STOPS the instant is_consumed(d).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.propagate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/events/#ManyUI.propagation_path-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Events, input and dispatch","text":"propagation_path(target::Widget) -> Vector{Widget}\n\n\nRoot to target, inclusive of both.\n\nSeparately named and PURE so the capture/bubble order assertion is a literal vector comparison against a stub tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.propagation_path"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Styling","page":"Styling","text":"Appearance is declarative. Rules are written in a CSS-like syntax, matched against widget ids, classes and types, and cascaded onto the tree by specificity.","title":"Styling"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Stylesheets","page":"Styling","text":"parse_css builds a Stylesheet; the css\"...\" string macro does the same at parse time:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nsheet = parse_css(\"\"\"\n    Container { layout: column; padding: 1; gap: 1; border: round cyan; }\n    Label     { color: cyan; }\n    .warn     { color: yellow; }\n    #title    { color: red; }\n\"\"\")\n\nlength(sheet.rules)\n\nBad input throws a CssParseError carrying the line and column, never a bare ArgumentError:\n\ntry\n    parse_css(\"Label { color: }\")\n    false\ncatch e\n    e isa CssParseError\nend","title":"Stylesheets"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Selectors-and-specificity","page":"Styling","text":"Three selector kinds are supported, and they rank exactly as CSS ranks them — id beats class beats type:\n\n[(src, specificity(parse_css(src).rules[1].selector))\n for src in (\"#title { color: red; }\",\n             \".warn { color: red; }\",\n             \"Label { color: red; }\")]\n\nSpecificity(id, class, type) is compared field by field. Between two rules of equal specificity, the later one wins.\n\nApplying the sheet resolves each node's computed style. A label that is #title, .warn and a Label matches all three rules, and the id wins:\n\nlabel = Label(\"hi\"; id = :title, classes = [:warn])\nroot = Container(label)\napply_stylesheet!(sheet, root)\n\ncomputed_style(label).fg\n\napply_stylesheet! walks the whole tree; recascade! is the incremental form the frame loop uses, doing the work only where the style is dirty.","title":"Selectors and specificity"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Properties","page":"Styling","text":"Property Value\ncolor foreground color\nbackground background color\nborder <kind> or <kind> <color>\ndisplay none, block, flex\nlayout a direction; shorthand for display: flex plus direction\ndirection row, column, row-reverse, column-reverse\njustify start, center, end, space-between, space-around, space-evenly\nalign start, center, end, stretch\nwidth, height a length\nmargin, padding 1, 2 or 4 cell counts, in CSS order\ngap cells between flex children\ngrow, shrink flex weights\noverflow visible, hidden, scroll\n\nAdding a property is adding a parse_property method — the table is extensible, and is used only by the parser, never in a render loop.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Colors","page":"Styling","text":"Colors come in four kinds:\n\nusing ManyUI\n(rgb(0xFF, 0x64, 0x00), ansi256(202), ansi16(9), COLOR_DEFAULT)","title":"Colors"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Color-degradation","page":"Styling","text":"Not every terminal speaks 24-bit color. Rather than refuse to draw, ManyUI maps a color down to whatever the target actually supports. degrade is a pure function of the color and the depth:\n\norange = rgb(0xFF, 0x64, 0x00)\n(truecolor = orange,\n at_256 = degrade(orange, ColorDepth.ANSI256),\n at_16 = degrade(orange, ColorDepth.ANSI16))\n\nThe 256-color step maps into the 6×6×6 cube and the grayscale ramp; the 16-color step picks the nearest perceptual match.\n\nThe depth is never guessed from ENV inside the render path. It comes from the driver's capabilities, so a web client reporting no truecolor support degrades exactly like a limited tty:\n\ncaps = capabilities(HeadlessDriver(Size(10, 2); depth = ColorDepth.ANSI16))\ncaps.color_depth\n\ndetect_color_depth is what a TerminalDriver uses to answer that question for a real terminal, from COLORTERM and TERM.","title":"Color degradation"},{"category":"section","location":"styling/#Text-attributes","page":"Styling","text":"Attributes live on Style:\n\ns = with(STYLE_NONE, Attr.BOLD, true)\n(bold = has(s, Attr.BOLD), italic = has(s, Attr.ITALIC))\n\nStyles merge with the child winning wherever it specifies something — the same rule the cascade itself is built from.","title":"Text attributes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/style/#Color,-style-and-CSS","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"","title":"Color, style and CSS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.ANSI16_PALETTE","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"The 16 system colors, in the xterm/VGA arrangement: 0:7 are the dim half, 8:15 the bright half. [I] data owned by this file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ANSI16_PALETTE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.ANSI256_PALETTE","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"The xterm 256-color palette: 0:15 system, 16:231 the 6x6x6 cube (16 + 36r + 6g + b), 232:255 the grey ramp (8 + 10i). [I].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ANSI256_PALETTE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.CUBE_LEVELS","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"The six per-channel levels of the 6x6x6 cube: 0, 95, 135, 175, 215,\n\n[I].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CUBE_LEVELS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.NAMED_COLORS","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"The color names parse(Color, s) and color(name) accept. [I].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.NAMED_COLORS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Color","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Color\n\nA 4-byte tagged color value. isbits, deliberately NOT an abstract hierarchy: Style is a field of Cell and a Matrix{Cell} is copied every frame.\n\nFields\n\nkind::ManyUI.ColorKind.T: How to read r, g, b.\nr::UInt8: Red channel, or the palette index for ANSI16/ANSI256.\ng::UInt8: Green channel.\nb::UInt8: Blue channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Color"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Color}, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse(_::Type{Color}, s::AbstractString) -> Color\n\n\nParse a color, throwing ArgumentError on garbage. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.tryparse-Tuple{Type{Color}, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"tryparse(\n    _::Type{Color},\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Union{Nothing, Color}\n\n\nParse a color. Returns nothing instead of throwing.\n\nAccepted forms: #rgb, #rrggbb, rgb(255,136,0), red, bright_black, ansi(9), color(200), default, transparent (-> COLOR_UNSET). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.tryparse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._linearize-Tuple{UInt8}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_linearize(v::UInt8) -> Float64\n\n\nDecode one sRGB byte to a linear 0..1 intensity. [I], and the reason the metric below is perceptual rather than a raw byte difference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._linearize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.ansi16-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"ansi16(i::Integer) -> Color\n\n\nA 16-color palette entry, 0 <= i <= 15. Throws ArgumentError otherwise. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ansi16"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.ansi256-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"ansi256(i::Integer) -> Color\n\n\nA 256-color palette entry, 0 <= i <= 255. Throws ArgumentError otherwise. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ansi256"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.ansi256_to_ansi16-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"ansi256_to_ansi16(c::Color) -> Color\n\n\nNearest 16-color entry. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ansi256_to_ansi16"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.color-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"color(name::Symbol) -> Color\n\n\nLook up a named color, e.g. color(:bright_black). Throws KeyError when unknown. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.color"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.color_distance-Tuple{Color, Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"color_distance(a::Color, b::Color) -> Float64\n\n\nWeighted-RGB distance in LINEAR (sRGB-decoded) space – raw-byte distance maps mid-greys to blue.\n\nNORMATIVE tie-break: when two candidates are equidistant the LOWER palette index wins. Without this, rgb_to_ansi256's test vectors are implementation-dependent. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.color_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.color_index-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"color_index(c::Color) -> UInt8\n\n\nPalette index of an ANSI16/ANSI256 color. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.color_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.degrade-Tuple{Color, ManyUI.ColorDepth.T}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"degrade(c::Color, depth::ManyUI.ColorDepth.T) -> Color\n\n\nX1. Map an authorial-intent color onto what depth can show.\n\nPure, TOTAL and IDEMPOTENT: degrade(degrade(c, d), d) === degrade(c, d).\n\nCalled ONLY from AnsiEncoder. Never in the buffer, never in layout. At MONOCHROME the result is white or black by luminance – never COLOR_DEFAULT, which would discard the fg/bg distinction.\n\nkind \\ depth TRUECOLOR ANSI256 ANSI16 MONOCHROME\nRGB identity rgb_to_ansi256 rgb_to_ansi16 lum >= 0.5 ? 15 : 0\nANSI256 identity identity ansi256_to_ansi16 as above\nANSI16 identity identity identity idx in (7, 15) ? 15 : 0\nDEFAULT identity identity identity identity\nUNSET identity identity identity identity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.degrade"},{"category":"function","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.detect_color_depth","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"detect_color_depth() -> ManyUI.ColorDepth.T\ndetect_color_depth(env::AbstractDict) -> ManyUI.ColorDepth.T\n\n\nPure, table-testable environment probe – no globals, no side effects.\n\nNORMATIVE rules, in order:\n\nNO_COLOR set (any value)         -> MONOCHROME\nCOLORTERM in {truecolor, 24bit}  -> TRUECOLOR\nTERM contains \"256color\"         -> ANSI256\nTERM == \"dumb\" or TERM unset     -> MONOCHROME\notherwise                        -> ANSI16\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.detect_color_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.is_set-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"is_set(c::Color) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when c specifies something. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_set"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.is_unset-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"is_unset(c::Color) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when c specifies nothing and must inherit. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_unset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.luminance-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"luminance(c::Color) -> Float64\n\n\nRelative luminance in 0..1, computed from sRGB-decoded (linear) channels. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.luminance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.rgb-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"rgb(r::Integer, g::Integer, b::Integer) -> Color\n\n\nA TrueColor value from three 0:255 channels. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.rgb"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.rgb-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"rgb(hex::Integer) -> Color\n\n\nA TrueColor value from a packed hex literal, e.g. rgb(0xff8800). Accepts 0 <= hex <= 0xffffff. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.rgb"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.rgb_to_ansi16-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"rgb_to_ansi16(c::Color) -> Color\n\n\nNORMATIVE: defined AS the composition, not independently.\n\nrgb_to_ansi16(c) === ansi256_to_ansi16(rgb_to_ansi256(c))\n\nOne tested table; TrueColor->16 and TrueColor->256->16 cannot disagree. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.rgb_to_ansi16"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.rgb_to_ansi256-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"rgb_to_ansi256(c::Color) -> Color\n\n\nNearest 256-color entry: the 6x6x6 cube, the 24 greys, and the 16 system colors. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.rgb_to_ansi256"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.to_rgb-Tuple{Color}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"to_rgb(c::Color) -> Color\n\n\nResolve ANSI16/ANSI256 to RGB via the palette; identity on RGB.\n\nThrows ArgumentError on UNSET/DEFAULT – they have no RGB value. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.to_rgb"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.AttrMask","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"Bitset over Attr.T values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.AttrMask"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Style","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Style\n\nA resolved text style: two colors plus a tri-state attribute set. isbits, 12 bytes.\n\nattrs holds attribute VALUES; mask holds which of them are EXPLICITLY SPECIFIED. The tri-state is required: without the mask, bold: false in a stylesheet cannot override an inherited bold – the same problem COLOR_UNSET solves for colors.\n\nFields\n\nfg::Color: Foreground color; COLOR_UNSET means inherit.\nbg::Color: Background color; COLOR_UNSET means inherit. Not inheritable.\nattrs::UInt16: Attribute values: bit set means the attribute is ON.\nmask::UInt16: Attribute mask: bit set means the attribute is SPECIFIED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Style-Tuple{}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"Style(\n;\n    fg,\n    bg,\n    bold,\n    dim,\n    italic,\n    underline,\n    blink,\n    reverse,\n    hidden,\n    strike\n) -> Style\n\n\nKeyword constructor. nothing leaves an attribute UNSPECIFIED; true and false both SET the mask, with the value on or off respectively.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.merge-Tuple{Style, Style}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"merge(base::Style, over::Style) -> Style\n\n\nRight-biased, per-property merge: over wins wherever it specifies.\n\nA monoid – associative, with identity STYLE_NONE. This IS the cascade fold. NORMATIVE:\n\nfg    = is_set(over.fg) ? over.fg : base.fg\nbg    = is_set(over.bg) ? over.bg : base.bg\nmask  = base.mask | over.mask\nattrs = (base.attrs & ~over.mask) | (over.attrs & over.mask)\n\nPure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.degrade-Tuple{Style, ManyUI.ColorDepth.T}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"degrade(s::Style, depth::ManyUI.ColorDepth.T) -> Style\n\n\nX1. Degrade fg and bg to depth.\n\nAt MONOCHROME, also drops DIM/ITALIC/BLINK/STRIKE/HIDDEN from the mask and keeps BOLD/UNDERLINE/REVERSE. Pure and idempotent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.degrade"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.has-Tuple{Style, ManyUI.Attr.T}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"has(s::Style, a::ManyUI.Attr.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when a is both specified AND on. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.has"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.inheritable-Tuple{Style}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"inheritable(s::Style) -> Style\n\n\nThe inheritable subset of s: the foreground and all text attributes. NOT the background. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.inheritable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.parse_attrs-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse_attrs(s::AbstractString) -> Tuple{UInt16, UInt16}\n\n\nParse a text-style property value into (attrs, mask).\n\n\"bold italic\" sets both bits in both words. A leading no- sets the mask with the value off: \"no-bold\" -> attrs = 0, mask = BOLD. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.parse_attrs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.resolve-Tuple{Style}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"resolve(s::Style) -> Style\n\n\nReplace UNSET colors with DEFAULT. Call once, at the root, before emission. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.resolve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.specified-Tuple{Style, ManyUI.Attr.T}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"specified(s::Style, a::ManyUI.Attr.T) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when a is specified at all, on or off. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.specified"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.with-Tuple{Style, ManyUI.Attr.T, Bool}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"with(s::Style, a::ManyUI.Attr.T, on::Bool) -> Style\n\n\nCopy of s with a specified and set to on. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.with"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.without-Tuple{Style, ManyUI.Attr.T}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"without(s::Style, a::ManyUI.Attr.T) -> Style\n\n\nCopy of s with a unspecified – clears both the value and the mask. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.without"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.CompoundSelector","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct CompoundSelector\n\nSimple selectors ANDed against ONE node: Button.primary#ok.\n\nFields\n\nparts::Vector{SimpleSelector}: Atoms that must all match the same node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CompoundSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.CssParseError","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct CssParseError <: Exception\n\nA stylesheet failed to parse, with the source position.\n\nFields\n\nmsg::String: What went wrong.\nline::Int64: 1-based line.\ncol::Int64: 1-based column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CssParseError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Rule","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Rule\n\nOne parsed rule.\n\nFields\n\nselector::Selector: What the rule matches.\nstyle::Style: Style properties it sets.\nbox::BoxPatch: Box properties it sets.\norder::Int64: Source order; breaks specificity ties.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Rule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Selector","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Selector\n\nA full selector, read left to right; the LAST compound is the subject.\n\nINVARIANT: length(combinators) == length(compounds) - 1.\n\nFields\n\ncompounds::Vector{CompoundSelector}: Compounds, in source order.\ncombinators::Vector{ManyUI.Combinator.T}: Relations between adjacent compounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Selector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.SimpleSelector","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct SimpleSelector\n\nOne selector atom.\n\nFields\n\nkind::ManyUI.SelectorKind.T: Universal, type, class or id.\nname::Symbol: The name matched; ignored for UNIVERSAL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.SimpleSelector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Specificity","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Specificity\n\nThe CSS specificity triple, ordered lexicographically.\n\nFields\n\nids::Int64: Number of id selectors.\nclasses::Int64: Number of class selectors.\ntypes::Int64: Number of type selectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Specificity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Stylesheet","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"struct Stylesheet\n\nAn ordered collection of rules. Immutable enough to be shared by every web session.\n\nFields\n\nrules::Vector{Rule}: Rules, in source order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Stylesheet"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.Stylesheet-Tuple{}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"Stylesheet() -> Stylesheet\n\n\nAn empty stylesheet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Stylesheet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._CssCursor","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"mutable struct _CssCursor\n\nA position-tracking cursor over stylesheet source. Internal to the parser; it is the only thing that knows about lines and columns.\n\nFields\n\nsrc::String\ni::Int64: Byte index of the next character.\nline::Int64: 1-based line of i.\ncol::Int64: 1-based column of i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._CssCursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.append!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Any}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"append!(ss::Stylesheet, rs) -> Stylesheet\n\n\nAppend several rules. Returns ss.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.append!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.isempty-Tuple{Stylesheet}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"isempty(ss::Stylesheet) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when the sheet has no rules. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isempty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.isless-Tuple{Specificity, Specificity}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"isless(a::Specificity, b::Specificity) -> Bool\n\n\nLexicographic order on (ids, classes, types). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isless"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.merge-Tuple{Stylesheet, Stylesheet}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"merge(a::Stylesheet, b::Stylesheet) -> Stylesheet\n\n\nConcatenate two stylesheets: b's rules come later, with their order shifted past a's. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.merge"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Selector}, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse(_::Type{Selector}, s::AbstractString) -> Selector\n\n\nParse a single selector. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.parse-Tuple{Type{Stylesheet}, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse(_::Type{Stylesheet}, s::AbstractString) -> Stylesheet\n\n\nParse a stylesheet. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.push!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Rule}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"push!(ss::Stylesheet, r::Rule) -> Stylesheet\n\n\nAppend a rule. Returns ss.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.push!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#Base.showerror-Tuple{IO, CssParseError}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"showerror(io::IO, e::CssParseError)\n\n\nReport the message with its line and column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.showerror"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._advance!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_advance!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Char\n\n\nConsume and return the next character, tracking line and column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._advance!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._border-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_border(v::AbstractString) -> Border\n\n\nParse border: <kind> or border: <kind> <color>. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._border"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._cascade_into!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Widget, Style}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_cascade_into!(\n    ss::Stylesheet,\n    w::Widget,\n    parent_style::Style\n)\n\n\nCascade w against ss under parent_style, write the result into its node, clear Dirty.STYLE, dirty it only if something actually changed, then recurse into its children so inheritance flows down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cascade_into!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._color-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_color(v::AbstractString) -> Color\n\n\nParse a color value. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._color"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._cursor-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_cursor(src::AbstractString) -> ManyUI._CssCursor\n\n\nA cursor at the start of src.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._enum-Union{Tuple{E}, Tuple{Type{E}, AbstractString, AbstractString}} where E","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_enum(\n    _::Type{E},\n    v::AbstractString,\n    prop::AbstractString\n) -> Any\n\n\nParse a CSS keyword into the module-scoped enum E: lowercase and dashes map to the SCREAMINGSNAKECASE value name. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._enum"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._eof-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_eof(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue once every character has been consumed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._eof"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._err-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_err(c::ManyUI._CssCursor, msg::AbstractString)\n\n\nFail at the cursor's current position.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._err"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._f32-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_f32(v::AbstractString, prop::AbstractString) -> Float32\n\n\nParse a Float32 value. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._f32"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._int-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_int(v::AbstractString, prop::AbstractString) -> Int64\n\n\nParse an integer value. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._int"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._is_ident_char-Tuple{Char}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_is_ident_char(ch::Char) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when ch may continue an identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_ident_char"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._is_ident_start-Tuple{Char}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_is_ident_start(ch::Char) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when ch may open an identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_ident_start"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._is_simple_start-Tuple{Char}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_is_simple_start(ch::Char) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when ch may open a simple selector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_simple_start"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._is_value_error-Tuple{Any}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_is_value_error(e) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue for the exceptions a value parser raises on BAD INPUT, as opposed to the ones a bug raises. parse(Color, _), parse(Length, _) and parse_attrs signal with ArgumentError; an out-of-range channel or cell count surfaces as InexactError/OverflowError. Anything else – MethodError, UndefVarError – is a defect in a parse_property method and MUST NOT be reported to the author as \"bad value\". Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._is_value_error"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._match_from-Tuple{Selector, Int64, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_match_from(s::Selector, i::Int64, w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nMatch compound i of s against w, then everything to its left against w's ancestors. A DESCENDANT combinator backtracks over every ancestor; a CHILD combinator considers only the parent. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._match_from"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._parse_compound!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_parse_compound!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> CompoundSelector\n\n\nConsume one compound selector: the atoms ANDed against a single node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_compound!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._parse_declarations!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_parse_declarations!(\n    c::ManyUI._CssCursor\n) -> Tuple{Style, BoxPatch}\n\n\nConsume a { ... } declaration block, folding it into one Style and one BoxPatch. Later declarations win over earlier ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_declarations!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._parse_selector!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_parse_selector!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Selector\n\n\nConsume one selector: compounds joined by > or by whitespace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._parse_selector!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._peek-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_peek(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Char\n\n\nThe next character, or '\\0' at end of input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._peek"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._peek2-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_peek2(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Char\n\n\nThe character after the next one, or '\\0'.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._peek2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._property-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Int64, Int64}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_property(\n    prop::AbstractString,\n    value::AbstractString,\n    line::Int64,\n    col::Int64\n) -> Tuple{Style, BoxPatch}\n\n\nDispatch one declaration through parse_property, re-throwing what it rejects as a CssParseError carrying the declaration's own line and column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._property"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._read_ident!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_read_ident!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> String\n\n\nConsume one identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._read_ident!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._read_value!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_read_value!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> String\n\n\nConsume a declaration value: everything up to the next ; or }.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._read_value!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._recascade_node!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Widget, Style}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_recascade_node!(\n    ss::Stylesheet,\n    w::Widget,\n    parent_style::Style\n)\n\n\nRecascade the STYLE-dirty part of w's subtree, descending SUBTREE breadcrumbs. The breadcrumbs are left in place: relayout! reads the same trail later in the same frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._recascade_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._rule_key-Tuple{Rule}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_rule_key(r::Rule) -> Tuple{Specificity, Int64}\n\n\nThe cascade sort key of a rule: (specificity, order). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._rule_key"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._skip_comment!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_skip_comment!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor)\n\n\nConsume a /* ... */ comment. The cursor must sit on its /.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._skip_comment!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._skip_ws!-Tuple{ManyUI._CssCursor}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_skip_ws!(c::ManyUI._CssCursor) -> Bool\n\n\nConsume whitespace and comments. Returns true when anything was consumed – which is what makes A B a descendant combinator and AB one identifier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._skip_ws!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._spacing-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_spacing(v::AbstractString, prop::AbstractString) -> Spacing\n\n\nParse a margin/padding shorthand of 1, 2 or 4 cell counts, in CSS order (top right bottom left). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._spacing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI._squeeze-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"_squeeze(v::AbstractString) -> String\n\n\nStrip every space out of v, so rgb(0, 90, 180) reaches parse(Color, _) in the form it accepts. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._squeeze"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.apply_stylesheet!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"apply_stylesheet!(ss::Stylesheet, root::Widget)\n\n\nU4. The impure shell. Walks TOP-DOWN – parents before children, so inheritance flows – writes the results into each node and clears Dirty.STYLE.\n\nMarks Dirty.LAYOUT where box actually CHANGED, and Dirty.PAINT where only computed_style changed. Compare-before-dirty is required: an unchanged cascade must cost zero frames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.apply_stylesheet!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.cascade","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"cascade(ss::Stylesheet, w::Widget) -> Tuple{Style, BoxStyle}\ncascade(\n    ss::Stylesheet,\n    w::Widget,\n    parent_style::Style\n) -> Tuple{Style, BoxStyle}\n\n\nU4. PURE: resolve w's computed style and box. Testable with a stub widget – no App, no tree walk.\n\nNORMATIVE per-node cascade order:\n\nstyle = inheritable(parent_style); box = BOX_DEFAULT\nfold matching_rules(ss, w) ascending by (specificity, order): style = merge(style, rule.style); box = apply(box, rule.box)\nstyle = merge(style, node(w).inline_style) and box = apply(box, node(w).inline_box) – inline ALWAYS wins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.cascade"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.matches-Tuple{CompoundSelector, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"matches(c::CompoundSelector, w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when every atom matches w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.matches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.matches-Tuple{Selector, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"matches(s::Selector, w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when w is the subject of s. Matched right-to-left, walking ancestors. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.matches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.matches-Tuple{SimpleSelector, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"matches(s::SimpleSelector, w::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when the atom matches w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.matches"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.matching_rules-Tuple{Stylesheet, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"matching_rules(ss::Stylesheet, w::Widget) -> Vector{Rule}\n\n\nRules matching w, sorted ASCENDING by (specificity, order) – apply them in the returned order. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.matching_rules"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.parse_css-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse_css(src::AbstractString) -> Stylesheet\n\n\nParse a stylesheet.\n\nThrows CssParseError, carrying line and column, on bad input. Never throws a bare ArgumentError. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.parse_css"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.parse_property-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{Val{P}, AbstractString}} where P","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"parse_property(\n    _::Val{P},\n    value::AbstractString\n) -> Tuple{Style, BoxPatch}\n\n\nThe extensible property table – adding a property is adding a method. Used ONLY by the parser, never in a render loop. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.parse_property"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.recascade!-Tuple{Stylesheet, Widget}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"recascade!(ss::Stylesheet, root::Widget)\n\n\nU4 + E1. Incremental: only STYLE-dirty subtrees, found via SUBTREE breadcrumbs. A no-op when the tree is style-clean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.recascade!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.specificity-Tuple{CompoundSelector}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"specificity(c::CompoundSelector) -> Specificity\n\n\nSpecificity of one compound. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.specificity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.specificity-Tuple{Selector}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"specificity(s::Selector) -> Specificity\n\n\nSpecificity of a full selector: the sum over its compounds. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.specificity"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/style/#ManyUI.@css_str-Tuple{Any}","page":"Color, style and CSS","text":"Parse a stylesheet at MACRO-EXPANSION time, so a bad stylesheet is a compile error:\n\ncss\"Button { color: red; }\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.@css_str"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Events","page":"Events","text":"Input propagates through the widget tree in two phases, exactly as it does in a browser: down from the root to the target (capture), then back up (bubble), stopping the moment something consumes it.","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Handling-an-event","page":"Events","text":"Handlers are methods on on_event!, dispatched on both the widget type and the event type. There is no callback registry and no closures:\n\nManyUI.on_event!(w::MyButton, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent}) = begin\n    if d.event.code === Key.ENTER\n        activate!(w)\n        consume!(d)      # stop here; nobody above sees this key\n    end\n    nothing\nend\n\nThe default on_event! is a no-op, so a widget only implements the events it cares about.","title":"Handling an event"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Capture,-target,-bubble","page":"Events","text":"propagate! walks the path from the root to the target and back:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nmutable struct Trace <: ManyUI.Widget\n    node::WidgetNode\n    log::Vector{Tuple{Symbol,Phase.T}}\nend\nTrace(name::Symbol, log) = Trace(WidgetNode(; id = name), log)\n\nfunction ManyUI.on_event!(w::Trace, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n    push!(w.log, (ManyUI.id(w), d.phase))\n    nothing\nend\n\nlog = Tuple{Symbol,Phase.T}[]\nroot, mid, leaf = Trace(:root, log), Trace(:mid, log), Trace(:leaf, log)\nmount!(root, mid)\nmount!(mid, leaf)\n\npropagate!(root, leaf, key('a'))\nlog\n\nThe root and mid see the key on the way down, the target sees it as AT_TARGET, and the walk unwinds back up. The target is visited exactly once.","title":"Capture, target, bubble"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Consumption","page":"Events","text":"consume!(d) ends propagation immediately. Anything that has not yet been visited never sees the event — that is how a focused input swallows a keystroke a global binding would otherwise act on.\n\nempty!(log)\n\nfunction ManyUI.on_event!(w::Trace, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n    push!(w.log, (ManyUI.id(w), d.phase))\n    ManyUI.id(w) === :mid && consume!(d)\n    nothing\nend\n\nconsumed = propagate!(root, leaf, key('a'))\n(consumed = consumed, visited = log)\n\nmid consumed the key during capture, so leaf was never reached and no bubble phase ran.","title":"Consumption"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Routing","page":"Events","text":"dispatch_event! picks the target before propagating, by event class:\n\nEvent Target\nMouseEvent the widget under the pointer, else the root\nKeyEvent, PasteEvent the focused widget, else the root\nResizeEvent, FocusEvent, TickEvent the root\nRefreshEvent, QuitEvent not routed; these are App-level only\n\nMouse events find their target by hit testing, which returns the deepest visible widget under the point. Because paint order is document order, a later sibling covers an earlier one — and hit_test honours that, so a click always reaches what the user can actually see.\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nui = Container(Label(\"title\"; id = :t), Button(\"OK\", identity; id = :ok))\nlayout!(ui, Region(1, 1, 20, 4))\n\nr = region(query_one(ui, \"#ok\"))\nhit_test(ui, r.x, r.y) === query_one(ui, \"#ok\")","title":"Routing"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Focus","page":"Events","text":"focusable_widgets is the tab order: every visible, focusable widget in pre-order. Hiding a subtree removes it from the order entirely.\n\napp = App(ui, HeadlessDriver(Size(20, 4)))\nfocus_next!(app)\nManyUI.id(focused(app))","title":"Focus"},{"category":"section","location":"events/#Where-events-come-from","page":"Events","text":"Events are parsed from a byte stream by InputParser, which is deliberately source-agnostic: it never touches stdin. A tty driver feeds it bytes read from the terminal; the web bridge feeds it bytes that arrived over a WebSocket. Both produce identical events.\n\nThe parser is incremental and keeps partial state, so a sequence split across two reads parses the same as one delivered whole:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nwhole = InputParser()\nsplit = InputParser()\n\nall_at_once = feed!(whole, Vector{UInt8}(\"\\e[A\"))       # up arrow\nbyte_at_a_time = vcat((feed!(split, [b]) for b in Vector{UInt8}(\"\\e[A\"))...)\n\nall_at_once == byte_at_a_time\n\nThat property is what makes the web target possible without ManyUI knowing the web exists.","title":"Where events come from"},{"category":"section","location":"api/enums/#Enumerations","page":"Enumerations","text":"Every finite value set in ManyUI is a module-scoped enum: a module holding an @enum T with SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE values. They tab-complete (ColorDepth.<TAB>), discriminate by type (d isa ColorDepth.T), and keep their value names out of the package namespace.","title":"Enumerations"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Align","page":"Enumerations","text":"Cross-axis alignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Align"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Attr","page":"Enumerations","text":"Text rendition attributes. Values are powers of two: they are used as bit positions in an AttrMask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Attr"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.BorderKind","page":"Enumerations","text":"Border line styles. BLANK still occupies its cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.BorderKind"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.CheckState","page":"Enumerations","text":"A Checkbox's state.\n\nAN ENUM AND NOT A Bool BECAUSE OF MIXED, and that value is not decoration: it is the \"select all\" box over a partially-selected set, which is exactly what a TreeView with checkable nodes needs. Two values would be a Bool and this module would be a lie.\n\nNORMATIVE: toggle! NEVER PRODUCES MIXED. The user has two reachable states; the third is set BY CODE, from a fact the user cannot express with one keystroke. MIXED toggles to CHECKED, because \"some are on\" answered by a human means \"now all of them\". A box a PROGRAM set to MIXED must be clickable OUT of it, or \"select all\" over a partial selection is a dead control.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.CheckState"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.ColorDepth","page":"Enumerations","text":"What a target can display. ORDERED, so depth < ColorDepth.TRUECOLOR is a legal capability test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ColorDepth"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.ColorKind","page":"Enumerations","text":"How a Color's bytes are to be read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ColorKind"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Combinator","page":"Enumerations","text":"How two compounds relate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Combinator"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Dimension","page":"Enumerations","text":"How a Length value is to be resolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Dimension"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Direction","page":"Enumerations","text":"Main-axis direction of a flex container.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Direction"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Dirty","page":"Enumerations","text":"What is stale about a node. Values are powers of two: they are combined in a DirtyMask.\n\nSUBTREE is a BREADCRUMB, not dirt: it means \"a descendant is dirty\", and it is what lets a frame pass skip clean subtrees in O(depth) instead of O(n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Dirty"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Display","page":"Enumerations","text":"Whether and how a box participates in layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Display"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Justify","page":"Enumerations","text":"Main-axis distribution. Split from Align so that align: space-between is unrepresentable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Justify"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Key","page":"Enumerations","text":"Logical key identity. CHAR means \"see KeyEvent.char\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Key"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Modifier","page":"Enumerations","text":"Keyboard modifier bits. Powers of two: used as a bitset in Modifiers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Modifier"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.MouseAction","page":"Enumerations","text":"What the mouse did.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.MouseAction"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.MouseButton","page":"Enumerations","text":"Mouse buttons, including the four wheel pseudo-buttons.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.MouseButton"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Overflow","page":"Enumerations","text":"What happens to content that exceeds its box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Overflow"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.Phase","page":"Enumerations","text":"Where an event is in its propagation. AT_TARGET exists because capture + bubble alone visit the target twice with no way for a handler to tell the two visits apart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Phase"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.PopupPlacement","page":"Enumerations","text":"Where a popup sits relative to the widget that opened it.\n\nBELOW and ABOVE are fixed; AUTO prefers BELOW and flips to ABOVE only when the popup would fall off the bottom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.PopupPlacement"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.ScrollAxis","page":"Enumerations","text":"Which axis a Scrollbar reports on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ScrollAxis"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.ScrollMode","page":"Enumerations","text":"When a Scrollbar's track and thumb are drawn.\n\nNOT the same axis of choice as Overflow: Overflow is a CLIPPING policy owned by the box model; this is a scrollbar VISIBILITY policy owned by the widget. Neither implies the other – NEVER still scrolls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ScrollMode"},{"category":"module","location":"api/enums/#ManyUI.SelectorKind","page":"Enumerations","text":"The four simple selector kinds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.SelectorKind"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Backends","page":"Backends","text":"A Driver makes targets swappable. A Backend makes them swappable without editing the call.","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#One-verb","page":"Backends","text":"launch runs an app on any target:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nui() = Container(Label(\"hello\"))\n\nlaunch(ui)                                   # this terminal\nlaunch(ui; backend = HeadlessBackend())      # nothing attached\n\nWith ManyUIWeb loaded, the same app runs in a browser and the only thing that changes is the backend argument:\n\nusing ManyUI, ManyUIWeb\n\nlaunch(ui; backend = WebBackend(port = 8000))\n\nNothing else moves. config and stylesheet describe the app, so they are spelled the same way whatever the target; a port or a tty describes the target, so it lives on the backend.","title":"One verb"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Why-a-factory","page":"Backends","text":"launch takes () -> Widget — a function that builds a tree — not a tree:\n\nlaunch(ui)          # yes\nlaunch(ui())        # no\n\nA terminal needs one app. A browser needs one app per connected client, each with its own tree, and it cannot know how many until they arrive. Only a factory serves both, which is why it is the universal unit rather than a convenience. It is also what keeps sessions isolated: two browsers share no mutable state because each got its own factory() call.","title":"Why a factory"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Blocking,-and-the-handle","page":"Backends","text":"By default launch blocks until the app quits and returns an exit code, like run!:\n\ncode = launch(ui)     # returns when the user quits\n\nPass wait = false to keep going and get a handle back:\n\nh = launch(ui; wait = false)\nisopen(h)     # still going?\nclose(h)      # ask it to stop\nwait(h)       # block until it has\n\nThe handle's type is the backend's business — an App for a terminal, a WebServer for the web — but every handle answers those same three verbs, so code that starts and stops an app need not know which backend it got. A handle is live when you get it: launch does not return until the loop is actually up, so isopen(h) is true on the next line and a close cannot race the loop into existence.","title":"Blocking, and the handle"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Writing-a-backend","page":"Backends","text":"Two things, and nothing else:\n\nstruct MyBackend <: ManyUI.Backend end\nManyUI.make_driver(::MyBackend) = MyDriver()\n\nThat is the whole contract for a target with one driver. There is no fallback for make_driver on purpose: a backend that forgets it gets a MethodError naming the type, rather than a silent default driver writing to somebody's terminal.\n\nA backend whose target multiplexes — one app per client, as the web does — has no single driver to make, so it defines its own launch method instead and never implements make_driver. ManyUIWeb.WebBackend is the worked example, and it lives in another package entirely: a backend joins by dispatch, with no cooperation from ManyUI beyond the abstract type.","title":"Writing a backend"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#The-old-entry-points-still-work","page":"Backends","text":"launch is a convenience over the seam, not a replacement for it. This still means exactly what it always did, and is what you want when you have a driver in hand rather than a description of one:\n\nrun!(App(ui(), TerminalDriver()))","title":"The old entry points still work"},{"category":"section","location":"backends/#Reference","page":"Backends","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/inputs/#Drop-down,-forms-and-popups","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"","title":"Drop-down, forms and popups"},{"category":"section","location":"api/inputs/#Drop-down","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"","title":"Drop-down"},{"category":"section","location":"api/inputs/#Form","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"","title":"Form"},{"category":"section","location":"api/inputs/#Popups","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"A popup is a second root painted over the tree and hit-tested before it – the layer a DropDown's list rides on. The App owns the one open popup; an owner opens it with open_popup! and is notified through on_popup_close!.","title":"Popups"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DD_ARROW_W","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"Cells the arrow and its separating space claim on the closed row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DD_ARROW_W"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DD_CLOSED","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"Arrow, closed. Width 1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DD_CLOSED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DD_FOCUS","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"The focused head. TC_CURSOR's meaning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DD_FOCUS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DD_MAX_ROWS","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"Rows a DropDown shows before its list scrolls, by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DD_MAX_ROWS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DD_OPEN","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"Arrow, open. Width 1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DD_OPEN"},{"category":"type","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DropDown","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"mutable struct DropDown{T, F, C} <: Widget\n\nA drop-down selection control: a one-row head, and an open list the App paints OVER the tree through the popup layer.\n\npanel is THE popup's content; NOT a child, and absent from children(w) – so it is not laid out with the tree, not painted with the tree, and NOT IN THE TAB ORDER. The popup is a paint-and-hit layer, not a focus layer: the DropDown KEEPS FOCUS while it is open and forwards, exactly as an HTML <select> does.\n\nselected is the COMMITTED option and row_cursor(w.panel.list) is the BROWSING position while open – TWO fields on purpose. ESCAPE and a click-away abandon the highlight and KEEP the selection; ENTER and a row click promote the highlight TO the selection. ONE field could not express \"arrowed to Large, pressed ESCAPE, still Medium\".\n\nopen and selected are Dirty.PAINT-reactive: the arrow is one cell, and measure is _lst_scan!'s widest-over-ALL-options plus a constant, a function of the DATA and not of the selection, so the box does not jump when you pick a shorter option.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\npanel::DropDownList{List{T, F, typeof(ManyUI._tc_noop)}} where {T, F}\nopen::Reactive{Bool}: True while the popup is open. PAINT-reactive: the arrow flips.\nselected::Reactive{Int64}: The COMMITTED option, 1-based; 0 when nothing is chosen.\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\nmax_rows::Int64\nplaceholder::String\non_change::Any: Called as on_change(dd) when the selection COMMITS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DropDown"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DropDown-Tuple{AbstractVector, Vararg{Any}}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"A drop-down over any AbstractVector, collected ONCE into a Vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DropDown"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DropDown-Union{Tuple{Vector{T}}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, C}} where {T, F, C}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"A drop-down over items, calling on_change(dropdown) when the selection COMMITS.\n\nitems is ALIASED by the underlying List, never copied. format, max_rows and placeholder shape the head and the open list; the head sizes to the WIDEST option (never avail), so a Label beside it keeps its rows.\n\nFocusable by construction; the open list is NOT – it is the popup and the popup is not a focus layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DropDown"},{"category":"type","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.DropDownList","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"mutable struct DropDownList{L<:Widget} <: Widget\n\nA DropDown's open list: the popup's CONTENT ROOT, its BORDER, and its click interceptor.\n\nINTERNAL machinery – the Scrollpane row/canvas/holder pattern (scroll.jl:216) – with one difference that matters: IT IS NOT IN app.root's TREE AT ALL. It is what Popup.content points at, it is UNPARENTED, and open_popup! throws if it ever stops being.\n\nIT EXISTS FOR EXACTLY ONE REASON, and the reason is two source lines. A row click must COMMIT, and a List click cannot: _tc_mouse! calls set_cursor!, which under SINGLE selects AND CONSUMES (tablecore.jl:1145), and _walk! RETURNS THE INSTANT is_consumed(d) (dispatch.jl:88) – so nothing above the list ever hears about it. This node sits between the popup's root and the List and takes the LEFT PRESS IN CAPTURE, BEFORE the list can eat it. \"Capture belongs to ancestors that want to intercept\" (button.jl:78) – a DropDown is precisely an interceptor of its own list, and this is the one widget in the codebase for which that sentence is a specification rather than an aside.\n\nIt earns its keep TWICE: it is also the frame (the border) the list needs, and the node popup_opaque! makes opaque.\n\nowner is Union{Nothing,Widget} and bound AFTER construction: DropDown holds this and this holds the DropDown, and a self-referential parametric type is not expressible in Julia. ONE dynamic dispatch per CLICK; NEVER on the frame path.\n\nlist is CONCRETE (L<:Widget), so _tc_row_at(w.list, e) dispatches STATICALLY – the Scrollbar{V}/Button{F}/List{T,F,A} pattern.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nlist::Widget\nowner::Union{Nothing, Widget}: The DropDown a row click commits to. Bound by the constructor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.DropDownList"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_acts-Tuple{Dispatch, KeyEvent}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_acts(d::Dispatch, e::KeyEvent) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target, with no modifiers.\n\nA DropDown activates on the way up, never in capture – the same rule Button uses – and leaves MODIFIED keys, TAB and BACK_TAB unconsumed so the tab order stays alive and ctrl+* bindings are not shadowed. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_acts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_caption-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_caption(w::DropDown) -> String\n\n\nThe head's caption: the placeholder when nothing is selected, else the committed option through the List's own formatter. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_caption"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_close!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_close!(w::DropDown) -> Bool\n\n\nClose OUR popup. True iff it was open.\n\nclose_popup!(a, w) – the GUARDED form – because the unguarded one would slam a popup a DIFFERENT widget opened in the same event burst, which is exactly what TABbing from one dropdown onto another does. Also clears open[] when there is no App, so a standalone widget cannot be stranded believing it is open. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_close!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_commit!-Tuple{DropDown, Int64}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_commit!(w::DropDown, k::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nPromote VIEW row k to the selection, close, and fire on_change IFF it really changed. True iff k was in range.\n\nTHE single exit of every commit – keyboard ENTER, keyboard SPACE and a row click all land here. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_commit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_key_closed!-Tuple{DropDown, Dispatch, KeyEvent}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_key_closed!(w::DropDown, d::Dispatch, e::KeyEvent)\n\n\nKeys while CLOSED: UP/DOWN cycle the committed selection and fire on_change – what an HTML <select> does closed – and ENTER or SPACE open the list. SPACE is Key.SPACE. Consumes ONLY what it handles. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_key_closed!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_key_open!-Tuple{DropDown, Dispatch, KeyEvent}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_key_open!(w::DropDown, d::Dispatch, e::KeyEvent)\n\n\nKeys while OPEN: UP/DOWN move the list's browsing cursor, ENTER and SPACE COMMIT the highlight, ESCAPE closes without committing. SPACE is Key.SPACE (and the legacy Key.CHAR(' ') form), never one or the other. Consumes ONLY what it handles. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_key_open!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_open!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_open!(w::DropDown) -> Bool\n\n\nOpen the popup. False when it is already open, when there are no options, or when there is no App.\n\nfocus!(a, w) FIRST, AND IT IS NOT A COURTESY: there is no click-to-focus in ManyUI, so without it a MOUSE-OPENED dropdown leaves focus on whatever had it and UP/DOWN/ENTER/ESCAPE go somewhere else – an open list you cannot drive. focus! is a no-op when w is already focused, so the keyboard path pays nothing. BEFORE open_popup!, so that on_blur! on the previously focused widget – which may be another DropDown closing its own popup through the GUARDED close_popup!(app, owner) – cannot slam the popup we are about to open.\n\nA DROPDOWN WITH NO APP CANNOT OPEN, stated rather than worked around: what survives standalone is the whole of the CLOSED widget – it constructs, measures, paints its selection, and UP/DOWN cycle that selection and fire on_change.\n\nPuts the list's cursor on the CURRENT selection, so DOWN-DOWN-ENTER from a selected option moves TWO, not to the top. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_open!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_popup_size-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_popup_size(w::DropDown) -> Size\n\n\nSize the OPEN popup occupies, INCLUDING the frame's border.\n\nclamp(n, 1, max_rows) + 2 rows and AT LEAST as wide as the head, which is what makes a dropdown look attached to its anchor. THIS IS THE OWNER'S DECLARATION that Popup.size is a field for: asking the content would ask a List, whose measure returns avail (list.jl:246) – the entire viewport. popup_region then clamps this to the screen, so a 400-item list on a 24-row terminal is not a special case here.\n\n_lst_scan! is MEMOIZED (list.jl:200), so this is O(1) per open after the first. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_popup_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._dd_select!-Tuple{DropDown, Int64}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_dd_select!(w::DropDown, k::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nThe CLOSED-state selection cycle: clamp to 1:n, early-out on no change, write selected[], fire on_change. Does NOT touch the popup. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._dd_select!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.is_open-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"is_open(w::DropDown) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while the popup is open. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_open"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{DropDown, Size}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"measure(w::DropDown, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nThe head's extent: the widest option (or the placeholder) plus the arrow. NOT avail – a greedy measure would shrink a Label beside this to zero rows.\n\nO(1) after the first call, because _lst_scan! is MEMOIZED on List.scanned (list.jl:200), and THIS IS THE FRAME PATH. Writing maximum(text_width, items) here is forbidden.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_blur!(w::DropDown)\n\n\nLose focus: the popup's lifetime is bounded by its owner's focus, which is what makes TAB-while-open correct with ZERO code in app.jl – TAB is not consumed -> :focus_next -> focus! -> on_blur! -> here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{DropDown, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_event!(w::DropDown, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nForward the keyboard: OPEN and CLOSED behave differently and each consumes only what it handles, so TAB, BACK_TAB and modified keys stay unconsumed and the tab order – and close-on-TAB – keep working.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{DropDown, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_event!(w::DropDown, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nA LEFT press on the head TOGGLES the popup: it opens a closed list and closes an open one. A press outside is _popup_dismiss!'s job; a press ON the head is delivered here (that clause of _popup_dismiss! returns false), which is why clicking the head of an open dropdown closes it here instead of dismiss-then-reopen. Wheel and drag fall through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{DropDownList, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_event!(w::DropDownList, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nIntercept a LEFT PRESS on a row IN CAPTURE and COMMIT it, before the List can select-and-consume it.\n\nis_scroll FIRST, so a wheel notch over the open list falls through to the List at target and _tc_wheel! scrolls it – a wheel over a dropdown's list is a scroll, not a choice. A DRAG likewise falls through. _tc_row_at and NOT row_cursor: at capture the List has not moved its cursor yet, so row_cursor is still the OLD row; _tc_row_at reads the POINTER, over painted_region, and is correct inside a scrolled pane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_focus!(w::DropDown)\n\n\nGain focus: show as focused and reveal into any scrolling ancestor. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_popup_close!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_popup_close!(w::DropDown)\n\n\nThe App closed our popup – by _dd_close!, a press outside, a resize, a blur, an unmount, or another widget opening one. NOTIFICATION ONLY, and provably non-looping because app.popup is cleared first.\n\nREVERTS the list's cursor to the COMMITTED option, which is what makes ESCAPE and click-away both ABANDON the highlight. After _dd_commit! the two are already equal and this is a no-op – so COMMIT NEEDS NO SPECIAL CASE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_popup_close!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_unmount!-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_unmount!(w::DropDown)\n\n\nClose on unmount: a popup outliving its owner is a list anchored to a widget that is no longer in any tree. Relies on unmount! calling on_unmount! BEFORE it clears node(w).app (widget.jl:367, 374), so app(w) still finds the App and the popup actually goes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_unmount!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.options-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"options(w::DropDown) -> Vector\n\n\nThe options, ALIASED. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.options"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.selected-Tuple{DropDown}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"selected(w::DropDown) -> Int64\n\n\nThe committed option index, 0 when nothing is chosen. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.selected_item-Union{Tuple{DropDown{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"selected_item(w::DropDown{T}) -> Any\n\n\nThe committed option, or nothing when nothing is chosen. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected_item"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.set_items!-Tuple{DropDown, AbstractVector}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"set_items!(w::DropDown, xs::AbstractVector)\n\n\nReplace the options. CLOSES the popup first, clears the selection, and marks the head for relayout EXPLICITLY.\n\nThe list is UNBOUND while closed (close_popup! clears node(w).app), so its Reactive marks nothing and posts nothing, and the DropDown's own measure just changed – hence the explicit mark! and post!. Clearing selected is set_items!(::List)'s argument verbatim (list.jl:394): an index into data that no longer exists names the WRONG option.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_items!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.set_open!-Tuple{DropDown, Bool}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"set_open!(w::DropDown, v::Bool) -> Bool\n\n\nOpen or close the popup. true iff the state changed. Public.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_open!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.Form","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"mutable struct Form{S, V} <: Widget\n\nA Container that can be submitted and read. Parametric on the two handlers, so on_submit and on_validate are CONCRETE fields and never boxed closures – the Button{F} pattern.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nfields::Vector{Pair{Symbol, Widget}}\non_submit::Any: Called as on_submit(form) when submit! passes validation.\non_validate::Any: Called as on_validate(form)::Bool. False VETOES the submit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.Form-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{S}} where {S, V}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"A Container that can be submitted and read.\n\nWHAT THIS ADDS OVER A Container, EXHAUSTIVELY, BECAUSE THE HONEST ANSWER IS \"NOT MUCH\":\n\nsubmit!(f) – on_validate(f) and, iff it returns true, on_submit(f).\nform_value(w) – a PROTOCOL, and the only reusable thing here.\nform_values(f) – every named field's value, once, at the boundary.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT ADD, and the claims are worth refuting because every framework makes them:\n\n\"It groups fields.\"   – so does a Container. It IS one, and it defines no render! and no measure for Container's reason.\n\"It traverses focus.\" – focusable_widgets is a pre-order walk_visible. TAB ALREADY WORKS through a bare Container. This is not a feature, it is the core.\n\"ENTER submits.\"      – IT DOES NOT, AND IT CANNOT. TextInput consumes ENTER unconditionally and _walk! STOPS DEAD on a consume, so a bubble handler here is NEVER REACHED from a text field. Capture-phase would fire and silently kill TextInput.on_submit, which is shipped and tested. So this widget DEFINES NO on_event! AT ALL, and you wire TextInput(\"\", _ -> submit!(f)) – one closure, explicit, and exactly as many characters as the magic would have been. A testitem PINS this limitation, so it is a fact and not a comment somebody deletes.\n\nValidation is ONE PREDICATE AND A VETO. No rule objects, no error vector, no :invalid class, no DSL. Validation is a function you already know how to write; a Form that ships a validation framework ships a DSL, and this codebase has no DSL.\n\nIF THAT LIST DOES NOT EARN A FILE FOR YOU, USE A Container. A Form IS one, and nothing built on a Form is out of a Container's reach. It earns its forty lines because form_value is worth a protocol and because \"wire ENTER through the field that consumes it\" is the thing every app gets wrong once.\n\nThe Form exists BEFORE its fields, so the closure has something to capture:\n\nf = Form(save_it)\nadd_field!(f, :name, TextInput(\"\", _ -> submit!(f)))\nadd_field!(f, :remember, Checkbox(\"Remember me\"))\nmount!(f, Button(\"Save\", _ -> submit!(f)))\n\nNo chicken-and-egg, no registry, no Ref.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Form"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI._fm_ok-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"_fm_ok(_::Widget) -> Bool\n\n\nThe default validator: everything passes. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._fm_ok"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.add_field!-Tuple{Form, Symbol, Widget}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"add_field!(f::Form, name::Symbol, w::Widget) -> Form\n\n\nMount w as a field NAMED name and return f.\n\nchildren(f) is the TAB ORDER – pre-order, unchanged – and f.fields is the NAME MAP; they are the same widgets in the same order. Plain mount!(f, w) still works and adds an UNNAMED field: a field with no name is one form_values does not report, which is right for a Button.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.add_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.field-Tuple{Form, Symbol}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"field(f::Form, name::Symbol) -> Union{Nothing, Widget}\n\n\nThe field named name, or nothing. O(fields); a form has ten. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.form_value","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"THE value protocol: what w MEANS to a form. Add ONE method to make a widget a form field.\n\nTHROWS MethodError BY DEFAULT ON PURPOSE, and does NOT return nothing: a Label has no value, and a form that silently reports nothing for a field the author expected to read is a form that SAVES A BLANK. A MethodError names the type.\n\nform_value and not value: value is far too generic a name for a UI package to export, and form_values below would then read as its plural. grid_of (tablecore.jl) is the precedent – a seam is a FUNCTION, not a supertype, which is what lets six unrelated widget types share it.\n\nTHE METHODS LIVE HERE, NOT IN EACH WIDGET'S FILE, and the layering and the manifest agree for once: \"what this widget means to a form\" is the FORM'S concept, not the text field's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.form_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.form_values-Tuple{Form}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"form_values(f::Form) -> Dict{Symbol, Any}\n\n\nEvery NAMED field's form_value, keyed by its name.\n\nALLOCATES and is TYPE-UNSTABLE BY CONSTRUCTION – a form is heterogeneous by definition, its Strings and CheckState.Ts and Ints sharing one Dict{Symbol,Any}. A BOUNDARY call, made once on submit and NEVER on a frame path, which is what licenses both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.form_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.submit!-Tuple{Form}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"submit!(f::Form) -> Bool\n\n\nValidate, then submit. on_validate(f) || return false; on_submit(f); return true. THE whole validation story: one predicate and a veto. True iff the submit ran.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.submit!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.Popup","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"mutable struct Popup\n\nOne open popup: a content root painted over the tree, the owner that opened it, the size the owner declares for it, and a placement.\n\ncontent is UNPARENTED – not in app.root's tree – which is what lets it be laid out and painted as a second root without disturbing the first. owner is the widget notified when the popup closes (on_popup_close!). size is the owner's declaration, not the content's measure: a List's measure returns the whole viewport, so the owner is the only thing that knows how big its own popup should be.\n\nFields\n\ncontent::Widget\nowner::Widget\nsize::Size\nplacement::ManyUI.PopupPlacement.T\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Popup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.Popup-Tuple{Widget, Widget, Size}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"Popup(\n    content::Widget,\n    owner::Widget,\n    size::Size;\n    placement\n) -> Popup\n\n\nPopup(content, owner, size; placement)\n\n\nA popup over content, owned by owner, size cells, placed by placement (default AUTO).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Popup"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.on_popup_close!-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"on_popup_close!(_::Widget)\n\n\non_popup_close!(_)\n\n\nNotify w that its popup has closed. The default is a no-op; a widget that opens popups (a DropDown) overrides it to reset its own state. Called AFTER app.popup is cleared, so an override may reopen without looping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_popup_close!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/inputs/#ManyUI.popup_region-Tuple{Region, Size, ManyUI.PopupPlacement.T, Size}","page":"Drop-down, forms and popups","text":"popup_region(\n    head::Region,\n    size::Size,\n    placement::ManyUI.PopupPlacement.T,\n    viewport::Size\n) -> Region\n\n\npopup_region(head, size, placement, viewport)\n\n\nThe on-screen Region a popup of size occupies when opened off head (the owner's border box) inside viewport. Pure, so placement is testable with no App.\n\nABOVE always sits above. BELOW and AUTO both ANCHOR below and flip above only when the popup would run off the bottom and there is room above – an anchored dropdown wants to open downward but must stay on screen, so \"below\" is a preference, not a demand. Either way the result is clamped to the screen: a list taller than the terminal is not a special case, it is just clipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.popup_region"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Scrolling","page":"Scrolling","text":"Scrolling in ManyUI is not a widget. Every WidgetNode carries a scroll::Offset, and paint! accumulates it down the tree and applies it to that node's children. compute_layout never reads the field.\n\nThat single fact is the whole design: the LayoutMap is invariant under scrolling, so a wheel tick is a repaint and never a relayout. A Scrollpane is policy over a compositor that already knows how to shift an origin — where the offset may go, who moves it, and what the indicator looks like.","title":"Scrolling"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Scrollpane","page":"Scrolling","text":"A Scrollpane is a window over exactly one child. Mount several by wrapping them in a Container, exactly as CSS makes you.\n\nHere is a twelve-line document in an eight-row window, painted through a HeadlessDriver — no tty, no terminal, the full pipeline:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nlog = Container([Static(\"line $i\"; id = Symbol(\"line$i\")) for i in 1:12]...)\npane = Scrollpane(log; id = :log)\n\ndriver = HeadlessDriver(Size(24, 8))\napp = App(pane, driver)\nframe!(app)                # cascade, layout, paint, diff, emit\napp.back                   # the frame just painted\n\nFour lines never fit, and the bar on the right says so: a five-cell thumb on an eight-cell track is 8 / 12 of the document, drawn to scale.\n\nviewport(pane) is the node that actually scrolls — the pane's internal canvas. It is what scroll_to!, max_scroll and content_extent take:\n\nvp = viewport(pane)\n(window = layout_of(vp).content,      # what you can see\n content = content_extent(vp),        # what there is\n max = max_scroll(vp))                # how far it can go\n\nThe window and the content extent are two different numbers, and a pane can scroll exactly when they differ. content_extent is the bounding box of the laid-out children's margin boxes, read straight from the LayoutMap the engine already computed — no re-measure, no relayout.","title":"Scrollpane"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#A-wheel-tick-is-a-repaint,-not-a-relayout","page":"Scrolling","text":"Feed the app a wheel event and watch what it dirties:\n\nbefore = layout_of(vp)\n\nhandle!(app, MouseEvent(MouseAction.PRESS, MouseButton.WHEEL_DOWN,\n                        1, 1, MOD_NONE))\n\n(scroll = scroll_of(vp),\n paint = is_dirty(vp, Dirty.PAINT),\n layout = is_dirty(vp, Dirty.LAYOUT),\n relayout_root = dirty_root(pane),   # nothing: relayout! returns at once\n boxes_moved = layout_of(vp) !== before)\n\nDirty.PAINT and nothing else. dirty_root finds no real layout work, so relayout! returns on its first line and every box in the tree keeps the value it had. The next frame simply paints the same boxes at a different origin:\n\nframe!(app)\napp.back\n\nThree rows moved up, the thumb moved down, and the diff sent only the cells that changed. This matters more than it looks: a Container is auto-sized, so a layout mark would promote all the way to the root — a full-tree relayout per wheel notch.\n\nLayout and paint disagree about where a scrolled widget is, and that is deliberate: region(w) is where layout put it, painted_region(w) is where it ended up.\n\nsixth = children(log)[6]\n(laid_out = region(sixth),\n painted = painted_region(sixth),\n shift = paint_offset(sixth))\n\nhit_test compares the pointer against painted_region, so a click lands on what the user can actually see rather than on whatever occupies that widget's unscrolled slot.\n\nManyUI.id(hit_test(pane, 1, 1))","title":"A wheel tick is a repaint, not a relayout"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Wheel-and-keyboard","page":"Scrolling","text":"Scrollpane handles MouseEvent and KeyEvent:\n\nInput Effect\nwheel up/down wheel_step cells vertically (default 3)\nwheel left/right, or shift+wheel wheel_step_x cells (default 6)\nup / down / left / right wheel_step cells\npageup / pagedown one window less one row, so a landmark stays\nhome / end the extremes of the vertical axis\n\nEach of those returns whether the event was consumed:\n\nscroll_to!(vp, ORIGIN)\n\n(down = dispatch_event!(pane, parse(KeyEvent, \"down\")),\n to_the_end = dispatch_event!(pane, parse(KeyEvent, \"end\")),\n end_again = dispatch_event!(pane, parse(KeyEvent, \"end\")),\n scroll = scroll_of(vp))\n\nThe second end returns false: the pane was already at its limit, so it did not move and did not consume the event. That rule is the whole of scroll chaining — a pane at its end lets the notch bubble to the next pane out, and no pane needs to know another exists.\n\nKeys are handled on the way up and only when unmodified, so a focused TextInput consuming left/right at target takes precedence with no special-casing anywhere, and ctrl+a still reaches your own bindings.","title":"Wheel and keyboard"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Clamping","page":"Scrolling","text":"Clamping is split across two layers, on purpose.\n\nset_scroll! is the core mutator. It clamps at zero per axis and nothing more — it cannot see the content extent from where it lives:\n\n(changed = set_scroll!(vp, Offset(0, -5)), stored = scroll_of(vp))\n\nscroll_to! owns the upper bound, because it can ask content_extent. It clamps to 0:max_scroll(w) and returns the offset actually stored:\n\n(asked_for = Offset(0, 999), stored = scroll_to!(vp, Offset(0, 999)))\n\nReturning the stored offset is what lets a caller detect \"this pane is at its limit\" with scroll_to!(w, o) === before. scroll_by! is the same thing relative to the current offset.\n\nBoth are built from two pure functions you can call yourself, and which every scrolling widget shares so they cannot disagree:\n\n(clamped = clamp_scroll(99, 4, 12),        # window 4, content 12\n minimal = scroll_into_view(0, 4, 7, 7))   # show row 7 in a 4-row window\n\nclamp_scroll is the definition of \"in range\". scroll_into_view moves the minimum needed: content already visible does not move at all, and an item taller than the window shows its top rather than its bottom.","title":"Clamping"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Scrolling-something-into-view","page":"Scrolling","text":"scroll_into_view!(vp, w) scrolls vp the minimum needed to bring a descendant's margin box inside its content box. It reads unshifted layout geometry, so it is correct whatever the current offset is, and idempotent.\n\nYou rarely call it. on_focus! defaults to reveal!(w), which asks every ancestor — nearest first — to reveal w through the reveal_child! hook, and Scrollpane implements that hook. So tabbing to a widget buried in nested panes scrolls it into view with no wiring at the call site and no isa Scrollpane anywhere in the core:\n\nbuttons = Container([Button(\"B$i\", b -> nothing) for i in 1:8]...)\np2 = Scrollpane(buttons; bar_y = ScrollMode.NEVER)\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, p2)\nlayout!(p2, Region(1, 1, 9, 3))\n\non_focus!(children(buttons)[8])          # what TAB does\nscroll_of(viewport(p2))\n\nNearest-first is load-bearing: an inner pane must finish moving before an outer pane can measure where the widget ended up. If your widget overrides on_focus!, it must call reveal!(w) itself — overriding replaces the default. TextInput and TextArea are the worked examples.","title":"Scrolling something into view"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#The-Scrollbar","page":"Scrolling","text":"A Scrollbar is an ordinary widget, parametric on the thing it reports on, and a sibling of the canvas rather than a child of it — so it is never inside a scrolled subtree and never shifts with the content.\n\nIt knows nothing about Scrollpane. It reads three functions and nothing else:\n\ncontent_extent(w)::Size      -- how big the content is\nlayout_of(w).content::Region -- how big the window is\nscroll_of(w)::Offset         -- where the window currently is\n\nThat seam is three functions rather than a supertype, which is exactly why one Scrollbar serves a bare Container, a Scrollpane's canvas and a TextArea — whose content is a Vector{String} and not children at all.\n\nThe geometry is a pure function of four integers, so you can check it without a widget, a layout or a buffer:\n\n[thumb_span(8, 8, 12, off) for off in 0:4]   # track, window, content\n\n(start, len), 1-based within the track. Two rules are normative: the thumb is never zero cells long — an invisible thumb is a broken scrollbar, and a one-cell thumb on a forty-cell track is the honest rendering of a forty-screen document — and the two ends are exact. At offset 0 the thumb starts at cell 1; at the maximum offset it ends on the last cell of the track. Those are the only two positions a user can verify at a glance, so the arithmetic is written around them.\n\nScrollMode decides when the bar is drawn:\n\nMode Gutter Ink\nScrollMode.NEVER none none — the wheel and keys still scroll\nScrollMode.AUTO always reserved only when there is something to scroll\nScrollMode.ALWAYS always reserved always, full-length when it all fits\n\nAUTO reserves the gutter unconditionally and toggles only the ink. This is CSS's scrollbar-gutter: stable, not overflow: auto, and the reason is oscillation: removing the gutter rewraps the content, which may then no longer overflow, which brings the gutter back. The price is one wasted column on a pane that does not overflow. Take it.","title":"The Scrollbar"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#overflow:-scroll-on-any-node","page":"Scrolling","text":"overflow is a BoxStyle field on every node and css.jl already parses it, so a design where the property silently did nothing unless the widget happened to be a Scrollpane would make the stylesheet lie. Any node can carry an offset — it is CSS's scrollTop and scrollLeft:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nsheet = css\"\"\"\n#box { overflow: scroll; }\n#doc { shrink: 0; }\n\"\"\"\n\ndoc = Container([Static(\"row $i\") for i in 1:5]...; id = :doc)\nbox = Container(doc; id = :box)\napply_stylesheet!(sheet, box)\nlayout!(box, Region(1, 1, 6, 2))\n\n(window = layout_of(box).content,\n content = content_extent(box),\n max = max_scroll(box))\n\nscroll_to!(box, Offset(0, 2))\nbuf = Buffer(6, 2)\nclear!(buf)\npaint!(buf, box)\nbuf\n\nshrink: 0 on the document is not decoration and it is the trap worth knowing about: the default shrink is 1, so the flex kernel absorbs the overflow and squeezes a five-row document into a two-row window — after which there is nothing left to scroll. Scrollpane sets it for you on an internal holder node, which is why it works out of the box.\n\nOverflow and ScrollMode are deliberately different axes of choice. Overflow is a clipping policy owned by the box model — HIDDEN and SCROLL both clamp the child clip to the content box, VISIBLE lets a child spill. ScrollMode is a scrollbar visibility policy owned by the widget. Neither implies the other: ScrollMode.NEVER still scrolls.","title":"overflow: scroll on any node"},{"category":"section","location":"scrolling/#Writing-a-scrolling-widget","page":"Scrolling","text":"Store the offset in node(w).scroll through set_scroll! or scroll_to!, and nothing else writes the field.\n\nA node's scroll shifts its children and never its own render frame. That is what keeps size(buf) inside render! equal to the widget's real content box, invariant under scroll — so a scrolled Label does not rewrap and a scrolled Button does not re-centre. A widget with no children that scrolls its own content reads scroll_of(w) in its own render! and slices explicitly; TextArea does exactly that, which is also how it stays O(window) instead of O(document).\n\nOverride content_extent when your content is data rather than children, and a Scrollbar will report on it with no further code.\n\nWhen a genuinely clipped node paints, paint! hands render! a ScrolledView rather than a BufferView: a window whose local origin and writable clip are independent, where the clip is the only authority and the frame is only a coordinate system. Writes outside the clip are silently dropped and reads return CELL_BLANK, so a widget cannot paint outside its box even if it tries — and a wide grapheme that would straddle a clip edge is dropped whole rather than halved, at the left edge as well as the right.","title":"Writing a scrolling widget"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Widgets","page":"Widgets","text":"ManyUI ships a small library of built-in widgets. They are ordinary widgets with no privileged access: anything they do, your own widget types can do too.","title":"Widgets"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Label","page":"Widgets","text":"Wrapping text. It measures and paints through wrap_width and write_text!, never through Base.textwidth, so a wide grapheme that would straddle the right edge moves to the next line rather than being cut in half.\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nl = Label(\"Hello, ManyUI!\")\nmeasure(l, Size(40, 4))\n\nIts text is reactive: assigning to it marks the label dirty for layout, because new text wraps differently and can move its siblings.\n\nl.text[] = \"Some considerably longer text that will wrap\"\nmeasure(l, Size(20, 4))","title":"Label"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Container","page":"Widgets","text":"A box that holds children, optionally with a border and a title. It is the workhorse of layout — give it a layout: and a gap: and it arranges whatever you put in it.\n\nui = Container(Label(\"first\"), Label(\"second\"))\nlayout!(ui, Region(1, 1, 20, 6))\nlength(children(ui))","title":"Container"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Button","page":"Widgets","text":"A focusable, clickable widget that calls its callback when pressed — whether by mouse, routed through hit testing, or by ENTER when it holds focus.\n\nWiring one to a Label gives a counter, and shows the reactive loop end to end: the callback writes the label's text, the write marks it dirty, and the next frame repaints it.\n\nclicks = Ref(0)\nreadout = Label(\"Count: 0\"; id = :count)\n\nb = Button(\"Click me\", _ -> begin\n               clicks[] += 1\n               readout.text[] = \"Count: $(clicks[])\"\n               nothing\n           end; id = :go)\n\nroot = Container(readout, b)\nlayout!(root, Region(1, 1, 20, 4))\n\nr = region(b)\nfor _ in 1:3\n    dispatch_event!(root, MouseEvent(MouseAction.PRESS, MouseButton.LEFT,\n                                     r.x, r.y, MOD_NONE))\nend\n\n(clicks = clicks[], text = readout.text[])\n\nA button also fires on ENTER when it holds focus, so the same counter works without a mouse:\n\ndispatch_event!(root, parse(KeyEvent, \"enter\"), b)\nreadout.text[]\n\nis_focusable(b) is true, so it takes part in the tab order:\n\nis_focusable(b)","title":"Button"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Scrollpane","page":"Widgets","text":"A window over exactly one child, for content bigger than the room you have. Wrap several children in a Container, as CSS makes you.\n\nThe offset lives on the tree, not in the pane — so a wheel tick marks Dirty.PAINT and layout never runs. viewport(pane) is the node that scrolls, and it is what the scrolling API takes:\n\nlines = Container([Static(\"line $i\") for i in 1:8]...)\npane = Scrollpane(lines)\napply_stylesheet!(STYLESHEET_EMPTY, pane)\nlayout!(pane, Region(1, 1, 9, 3))\n\nvp = viewport(pane)\n(window = layout_of(vp).content.height, content = content_extent(vp).height)\n\nEight rows of content in a three-row window, so five rows can scroll past. scroll_to! clamps and returns what it actually stored:\n\n(max = max_scroll(vp), stored = scroll_to!(vp, Offset(0, 99)))\n\nThe wheel, the arrows, pageup/pagedown and home/end all work, and a pane at its limit does not consume the event — so it bubbles to the pane outside it and scroll chaining costs no code at all. Scrolling covers the whole story.","title":"Scrollpane"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Scrollbar","page":"Widgets","text":"The visible indicator for one axis. It is a sibling of what it reports on, never a child, so it never scrolls with the content — and it is parametric on its viewport, so one Scrollbar serves a Container, a Scrollpane's canvas or a TextArea alike. It reads three functions and touches nothing else.\n\nScrollpane builds its own, but the geometry is a pure function you can check on its own — (start, len) on the track, for a window over some content at an offset:\n\nthumb_span(3, 3, 8, 0)","title":"Scrollbar"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#TextInput","page":"Widgets","text":"Single-line entry, with a caret, a placeholder and a submit handler. It takes the width it is offered and scrolls horizontally rather than resizing, so a keystroke costs zero layout.\n\nfield = TextInput(\"hi\", i -> nothing; placeholder = \"name?\")\ninsert_text!(field, \"!\")\n(text = field.text[], cursor = field.cursor[])\n\nThe cursor is a 0-based count of grapheme clusters, so it steps over a wide emoji in one move and never lands inside one.","title":"TextInput"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#TextArea","page":"Widgets","text":"Multi-line entry over a Vector{String}. It scrolls by indexing its lines, so painting is O(window) and a huge document costs the same frame as a small one.\n\nnotes = TextArea(\"alpha\\nbeta\")\ninsert_newline!(notes)\n(text = text(notes), extent = content_extent(notes))\n\nIt overrides content_extent, which is the whole of its integration with Scrollbar. Text entry covers both widgets, and the grapheme rules they are built on.","title":"TextArea"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#MinSizeOverlay","page":"Widgets","text":"The \"Increase Terminal Size\" screen. When the rendering area drops below the root's minimum, the App suspends normal rendering and paints this instead — no layout of your tree runs at all until there is room for it again.\n\nshould_suspend(Size(12, 3), Size(20, 5))\n\nshould_suspend(Size(80, 24), Size(20, 5))\n\nYou rarely construct it yourself; AppConfig(; min_size = ...) decides when it appears.\n\nOVERLAY_MIN_SIZE\n\nBelow a certain size even the overlay cannot be laid out, so a tree-free painter takes over. The framework never crashes because the window got small.","title":"MinSizeOverlay"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#List,-Table-and-DataTable","page":"Widgets","text":"The data widgets, covered in full on the Data widgets page. List shows items, Table shows columns, DataTable also sorts.\n\nl = List([\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\"])\n(rows = row_count(l), nodes = length(descendants(l)))\n\nnodes is zero, and stays zero at a hundred thousand items: unlike every other widget here, their rows are data rather than widgets, so a frame costs the same however much data you hand them.","title":"List, Table and DataTable"},{"category":"section","location":"widgets/#Writing-your-own","page":"Widgets","text":"A widget is a mutable struct holding a WidgetNode, plus whatever state it needs:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nmutable struct Spinner <: ManyUI.Widget\n    node::WidgetNode\n    frame::Int\nend\nSpinner() = Spinner(WidgetNode(; type_name = :Spinner), 1)\n\nconst FRAMES = ('|', '/', '-', '\\\\')\n\nManyUI.measure(w::Spinner, avail::Size) = Size(1, 1)\n\n# In ManyUITUI or a specific projection, you would implement render!:\n# function ManyUITUI.render!(w::Spinner, buf::AbstractMatrix{Cell})\n#     write_text!(buf, 1, 1, string(FRAMES[w.frame]), STYLE_NONE)\n#     nothing\n# end\n\nTwo methods make it real: measure, which reports how much room it wants, and render!, which paints into the content box it was granted. The buffer's (1, 1) is the content origin, and its size is the content box — so a widget cannot paint outside its own region, and type_name makes it addressable from CSS as Spinner { ... }.","title":"Writing your own"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data/#Columns-and-selection","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The machinery List, Table and DataTable share.","title":"Columns and selection"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_AUTO_SAMPLE","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Rows an AUTO column measures at a data change. 200: 200 formatter calls cost about what ONE frame of a 40-row table costs, so it is invisible; and a column's width is decided by its TYPICAL value, which 200 samples estimate as well as 100 000 do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_AUTO_SAMPLE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_CURSOR","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The cursor row, drawn only while FOCUSED. Merged, so it composes with TC_SELECTED on a row that is both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_CURSOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_ELLIPSIS","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Truncation marker. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted in the suite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_ELLIPSIS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_HEADER","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The header rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_HEADER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_MEASURE_CAP","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The measurement cap an AUTO column uses BEFORE the first layout, when there is no content box to cap against yet.\n\n_tc_measure's cap is normally the content-box width, but an AUTO column is seeded at CONSTRUCTION, and at construction layout_of(w) is still empty – so the rule \"cap at the viewport\" has no viewport to name and a cap of 0 would measure every column at nothing. This is that fallback and nothing else: it keeps the seed O(cap) per cell rather than O(length), and refresh_extent!(w) re-measures against the real box for anyone who needs the exact answer. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_MEASURE_CAP"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_RULE","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Default header rule glyph. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_RULE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_SELECTED","page":"Columns and selection","text":"A selected row. MERGED onto the cells under it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_SELECTED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_SHIFT","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The modifier set that EXTENDS. See _tc_key!'s modifier policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_SHIFT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_SORT_ASC","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Ascending indicator. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_SORT_ASC"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_SORT_DESC","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Descending indicator. Width-1 BY CONSTRUCTION, asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_SORT_DESC"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_WHEEL_STEP","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Cells one vertical wheel notch scrolls a row widget. Scrollpane's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_WHEEL_STEP"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TC_WHEEL_STEP_X","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Cells one horizontal wheel notch scrolls a row widget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TC_WHEEL_STEP_X"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.Column","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Column(; ...) -> Column\nColumn(\n    header::AbstractString;\n    width,\n    align,\n    min_width,\n    max_width,\n    sortable\n) -> Column\n\n\nA column captioned header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Column"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.Column-2","page":"Columns and selection","text":"struct Column\n\nOne column of a Table: a caption, a width policy, an alignment.\n\nIMMUTABLE: a column is a SPEC. Changing one is grid_of(t).cols[j] = Column(...) followed by refresh_columns!(t).\n\nwidth is a Length – cells(12), pct(25), fr(1), AUTO – because those are already the framework's four answers to \"how wide\", already parsed by the CSS engine and already resolved by definite_size/_apportion. Inventing a fifth vocabulary for columns would be inventing flexbox next to flexbox.\n\nalign is Align.T, REUSED rather than reinvented: cross_align (layout.jl:163) does the placement, START/CENTER/END are left/centre/right, and Align.STRETCH degenerates to START FOR FREE because cross_align(n, STRETCH, w) returns (0, w) and a text painter reads only the offset. There is no CellAlign enum because there is nothing for one to say that Align does not.\n\nmin_width/max_width are Lengths, not Ints: a bound the box model spells as a Length must not be downgraded here. AUTO means unbounded – 0 low, typemax(Int) high.\n\nsortable is ignored by Table, which never sorts. It is one Bool per COLUMN, not per row, and it buys DataTable two things: which headers reserve an indicator cell, and which headers a click sorts.\n\nFields\n\nheader::String: Header caption. ONE row; a newline is undefined.\nwidth::Length: Width policy. See _tc_resolve!.\nalign::ManyUI.Align.T: How the cell text AND the header sit in the column.\nmin_width::Length: Lower bound. AUTO is unbounded.\nmax_width::Length: Upper bound. AUTO is unbounded. On an AUTO column this is ALSO the measurement cap – see _tc_measure.\n\nsortable::Bool: DataTable only: may this column be sorted?\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Column"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.RowsWidget","page":"Columns and selection","text":"abstract type RowsWidget <: Widget\n\nA widget whose content is a sequence of DATA ROWS rather than children.\n\nTHE SEAM, and it is functions, not fields – scroll.jl's own move (\"the scrollable seam is three functions, not a type\"), one layer up. Every subtype MUST define, each a one-liner:\n\nselection_of(w)::Selection\nrow_count(w)::Int             # SOURCE rows. O(1). FRAME PATH.\nview_count(w)::Int            # rows in view order. O(1).\nview_source(w, k::Int)::Int   # view index -> source index\nview_rank(w, s::Int)::Int     # source index -> view index\n_tc_touch!(w)::Nothing        # bump the PAINT cell\n_tc_header_rows(w)::Int       # pinned chrome rows at the top\n_tc_extent_width(w)::Int      # the extent's WIDTH, read not computed\n\nand MUST carry these DIRECT fields:\n\nnode::WidgetNode\nversion::Reactive{Int}        # Dirty.PAINT -- see below\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}\n\nversion/focused MUST be direct fields of the widget and MUST NOT be moved into a held struct: attach_reactives! walks fieldnames(typeof(w)) ONE level and binds only direct Reactive fields (reactive.jl:100). A Reactive one level down silently never gets an owner and never marks anything dirty. THAT FAILURE HAS NO ERROR MESSAGE.\n\nThis is NOT a scrollable supertype and must not become one. The scrollable seam stays the three functions scroll.jl:57-66 names – content_extent, layout_of(w).content, scroll_of(w) – which is why Scrollbar{List{T,F,A}} needs ZERO new code in scroll.jl. RowsWidget is a different axis: it is the seam for SELECTION and NAVIGATION, which are identical across the three rather than merely similar.\n\nThe seam methods take ::RowsWidget and have NO default, so a subtype that forgets one gets a MethodError – and a Button never answers them at all.\n\nFields\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.RowsWidget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.Selection","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Selection() -> Selection\nSelection(mode::ManyUI.SelectMode.T) -> Selection\nSelection(mode::ManyUI.SelectMode.T, n::Int64) -> Selection\n\n\nA selection of mode over n rows, cursor and anchor on row 1 (or 0 when n == 0). NOTHING is selected initially, in every mode – including SINGLE: a list that selects row 1 before the user has touched it has made a choice on their behalf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Selection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.Selection-2","page":"Columns and selection","text":"mutable struct Selection\n\nA row cursor and a set of selected rows, over n SOURCE rows.\n\nNORMATIVE: every index here is a SOURCE index – an index into the user's rows/items – and NEVER a view index. That is what makes a sort harmless: sort_by! permutes the VIEW and this does not move. The alternative – view indices – silently reassigns the selection to DIFFERENT ROWS the moment the user clicks a header, which is data corruption with a pretty animation.\n\nPURE: no widget, no layout, no buffer – four Ints and a BitSet, so the whole selection model is one table test. thumb_span (scroll.jl:572) set that bar and this meets it.\n\nn IS A FIELD, and that is deliberate: a Selection that does not know how many rows there are makes every mutator take a caller-supplied n you can get wrong – set_cursor!(s, 5, 999) on a 3-row list parks the cursor out of range, silently. resize_selection! is the ONE way n changes.\n\ncursor/anchor are 0 when there is no row, never nothing: 0 keeps the fields concrete Int and every operation total at n == 0.\n\nrows is a BitSet and not a Set{Int} or a Vector{Bool}. MEASURED, not assumed: in allocates ZERO for BOTH BitSet and Set{Int}, so the usual allocation argument for BitSet is WRONG and is not made here. BitSet wins on the two axes that survive: an empty selection over 100 000 rows costs ~0 (a Vector{Bool}/BitVector costs 12.5 kB), and a contiguous anchor:target extend is a bitmap range rather than 100 000 hash inserts. All of Base; no dependency.\n\nFields\n\nmode::ManyUI.SelectMode.T\nn::Int64: Rows this selection is sized for. resize_selection! maintains it.\ncursor::Int64: The cursor's SOURCE row, 1-based. 0 IFF n == 0.\nanchor::Int64: The fixed end of a shift-extend, a SOURCE row. 0 IFF n == 0.\nrows::BitSet\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Selection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TableGrid","page":"Columns and selection","text":"mutable struct TableGrid\n\nEverything a Table and a DataTable need about COLUMNS, held BY COMPOSITION so the two widgets share one column model instead of declaring it twice and drifting.\n\nNOT a Widget: no node, no dirty, no render!. It holds NO Reactive and MUST NOT ever hold one – attach_reactives! would not see it (reactive.jl:100).\n\nIt holds no rows and no selection: those stay on the widget, because Selection is shared with List (which has no columns) and rows is what DataTable sorts a permutation OVER.\n\nFields\n\ncols::Vector{Column}\nsep::String\nsep_w::Int64\nshow_header::Bool\nrule::Bool\nrule_glyph::String\nsample::Int64\nautos::Vector{Int64}\nwidths::Vector{Int64}\nxs::Vector{Int64}\ncache_version::Int64: The version the memo was resolved at; -1 is never.\ncache_width::Int64: The content-box width it was resolved for; -1 is never.\ncache_total::Int64: sum(widths) + sep_w * (ncols - 1). What _tc_extent reads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TableGrid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.TableGrid-Tuple{Vector{Column}}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"TableGrid(\n    cols::Vector{Column};\n    sep,\n    show_header,\n    rule,\n    rule_glyph,\n    sample\n) -> TableGrid\n\n\nA grid over cols. cols is ALIASED, never copied.\n\nThrows ArgumentError when rule is true and rule_glyph is not width-1, and when sample < 0. Throwing beats a quiet nothing: mount!(::Scrollpane, ...) throws for the same class of reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TableGrid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_acts-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_acts(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target. CAPTURE belongs to ancestors that want to intercept, and a list is never an interceptor. _ta_acts/_sp_acts, third copy. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_acts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_auto!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_auto!(w::RowsWidget, first::Int64, last::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nMeasure every AUTO column of w from SOURCE rows first:last and RAISE its mark. True iff a mark rose. MONOTONE: nothing here lowers a mark.\n\nOnly AUTO columns pay: the loop continues on any column whose width is CELLS, PERCENT or FRACTION, so a table of fixed columns calls the cell function ZERO times outside the paint loop.\n\nEvery measurement is CAPPED via _tc_measure(s, cap) with cap = min(definite max_width, content-box width). O(cap) per cell, never O(length). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_auto!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_auto_reset!-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_auto_reset!(w::RowsWidget)\n\n\nReset every AUTO mark to its SEED – the header text plus _tc_header_reserve – and invalidate the resolve memo. THE ONLY thing that can make a column NARROWER. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_auto_reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_bar_style-Tuple{Selection, Int64, Bool}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_bar_style(s::Selection, src::Int64, foc::Bool) -> Style\n\n\nThe style to MERGE across a highlighted row: TC_SELECTED, TC_CURSOR, or both merged.\n\nstyle_region! and NOT fill_region!: it MERGES onto the cells already there, KEEPING cur.content and cur.width (buffer.jl:476), so a highlight over a wide cluster restyles its HEAD and leaves its continuation a continuation. frame! blanks the back buffer every frame (app.jl:552), so a never-written padding cell is a blank and reversing a blank IS the bar – the full-width highlight costs one style_region! per highlighted VISIBLE row and no fill pass at all. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_bar_style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_body_height-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_body_height(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nRows of the SCROLLING body: the content box less the header. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_body_height"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_bound-Tuple{Column, Int64, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_bound(col::Column, wj::Int64, avail::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nw clamped into column j's min_width:max_width, resolved against avail. An AUTO bound is unbounded: 0 low, typemax(Int) high. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_bound"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_cap-Tuple{Column, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_cap(col::Column, avail::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe measurement cap for column j in an avail-cell content box: min(definite max_width, content-box width).\n\nTC_MEASURE_CAP stands in for the content box before the first layout, when avail is still 0 and there is no viewport for the rule to name. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_cap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_cell_text-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_cell_text(\n    w::RowsWidget,\n    src::Int64,\n    j::Int64\n) -> AbstractString\n\n\nThe cell text of SOURCE row src, column j.\n\nSOURCE, not view: the painter resolves view_source ONCE per row (it needs it for the selection anyway), so this is IDENTICAL for Table and DataTable and neither defines it. THAT is why there is no per-widget cell_text and no second paint loop.\n\nw.cell is a CONCRETE field of a parametric struct, so this is a STATIC dispatch. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_cell_text"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_col_at-Tuple{RowsWidget, MouseEvent}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_col_at(w::RowsWidget, e::MouseEvent) -> Int64\n\n\nThe 1-based column index under mouse event e, from grid_of(w).xs and widths – LAST frame's, which is exactly the header the user SAW and clicked. 0 for none, and 0 before the first paint. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_col_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_cursor_view-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_cursor_view(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nThe cursor's VIEW row; 0 when there is none. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_cursor_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_extent-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_extent(w::RowsWidget) -> Size\n\n\nSize(_tc_extent_width(w), view_count(w) + _tc_header_rows(w)).\n\nTHE + header_rows IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT A ROUNDING ERROR. It is FORCED, and here is the proof rather than the assertion: Scrollbar._sb_metrics (scroll.jl:624-634) reads content_extent(w.viewport) and layout_of(w.viewport).content DIRECTLY and NEVER calls max_scroll. There is therefore NO seam that can tell a bar the body is hh rows shorter than the box, and OVERRIDING max_scroll WOULD NOT WORK. Counting the header restores\n\nmax_scroll.y = extent.height - content.height = (n + hh) - H\nlast body row visible  <=>  scroll.y + (H - hh) == n\n                       <=>  scroll.y == n + hh - H\n\n– the SAME number. So max_scroll, clamp_scroll, scroll_to!, scroll_into_view and thumb_span are all already right, and Scrollbar{Table{R,F,A}} needs not one line in scroll.jl. Drop the + hh and THE LAST hh ROWS OF EVERY TABLE ARE UNREACHABLE.\n\nTHE PRICE, stated rather than discovered: content_extent(t).height is a white lie – row_count(t) is the honest accessor and it is public and exported – and the thumb over-reports the visible fraction by hh/(n + hh), ~5% on a 20-row table with a header, decaying to nothing as the data grows. That is the cost of not inventing a second scrollable seam for one row.\n\nO(1). ON THE FRAME PATH, several times – see _tc_resolve!. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_extent_width-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_extent_width(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nThe WIDTH _tc_extent reports, READ and never computed.\n\nFor a grid it is grid_of(w).cache_total, resolved against the CURRENT content box – which is what makes max_scroll(t).x correct BEFORE the first paint. A List overrides with its widest high-water mark, because it has no columns to resolve.\n\nNOT in the architect's seam list, and it is the one addition this file makes to it: _tc_extent is specified as \"grid_of(w).cache_total for a grid and the row-extent mark for a List\", which is a per-widget branch the contract names but never gives a name to. grid_of has no List method, so the branch cannot be written any other way without a type-unstable Union{Nothing,TableGrid} probe on the FRAME PATH. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_extent_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_follow_cursor!-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_follow_cursor!(w::RowsWidget)\n\n\nScroll the MINIMUM needed to bring the cursor's VIEW row into the BODY window, via scroll_into_view. A no-op before the first layout and with no cursor.\n\nset_scroll! and NOT scroll_to!, mirroring _ta_follow_caret! (textarea.jl:483): scroll_into_view(off, H - hh, r-1, r-1) already returns a value in 0 : n + hh - H, which IS max_scroll(w).y by the identity in _tc_extent, so the clamp is provably redundant and would cost a content_extent call on every keystroke.\n\nVERTICAL ONLY. THE CURSOR IS A ROW, NOT A CELL: there is no column cursor, so there is nothing to follow horizontally. Horizontal scrolling is the wheel's and the bar's job.\n\nThe two halves of the header arithmetic MUST agree: _tc_extent says n + hh, this says the window is H - hh. The suite asserts the identity. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_follow_cursor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_header_reserve-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_header_reserve(w::RowsWidget, j::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nCells reserved at the RIGHT of column j's header when seeding its AUTO mark. 0 by default. DataTable returns 1 for a sortable column, so a column sized to its header alone still has a cell for the indicator. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_reserve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_header_rows-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_header_rows(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nRows of the content box that are pinned CHROME rather than data. 0 by default – a List has no header. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_header_text-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_header_text(w::RowsWidget, j::Int64) -> String\n\n\nThe header text of column j. grid_of(w).cols[j].header by default; DataTable overrides to add its indicator. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_header_text"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_key!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_key!(w::RowsWidget, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent}) -> Bool\n\n\nUP/DOWN by one; PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN by one body LESS ONE ROW of overlap; HOME/END to the ends; SPACE toggles (MULTI); ENTER calls on_activate(w); LEFT/RIGHT scroll HORIZONTALLY by one cell. SHIFT on any movement key EXTENDS from the anchor. Returns true iff it consumed.\n\nEach of the three widgets is exactly:\n\non_event!(w::List, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})::Nothing =\n    (_tc_key!(w, d); nothing)\n\nCONSUMES ONLY WHEN SOMETHING ACTUALLY MOVED. That is _sp_move!'s rule (scroll.jl:435) applied to a cursor, and it is the whole of chaining: a List on its LAST row lets DOWN bubble to an outer Scrollpane. ENTER is the one exception and it is not one: firing on_activate IS the something that happened.\n\nMODIFIER POLICY, a DELIBERATE and BOUNDED departure from the \"unmodified keys only\" rule TextInput/TextArea/Scrollpane follow: bare keys act, and SHIFT ALONE additionally extends. The rule exists so ctrl+a reaches an application binding (textinput.jl:477); the guard here leaves ctrl+, alt+ and super+* ENTIRELY to the app, and shift+arrow has exactly one meaning in every list ever shipped and is not a plausible accelerator.\n\nCTRL+A IS NOT BOUND. select_all!(w) is public; bind! it.\n\nTAB and ESCAPE FALL THROUGH UNCONSUMED, which is what keeps the tab order alive.\n\nTHERE IS NO CELL CURSOR. LEFT/RIGHT scroll; they do not walk columns. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_key!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_local-Tuple{Widget, MouseEvent}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_local(w::Widget, e::MouseEvent) -> Offset\n\n\nThe pointer's position in w's CONTENT-box frame, 1-based, honouring every scrolled ancestor. (1, 1) is the first cell render! can write.\n\nlocal_offset(d) IS FORBIDDEN IN THIS TIER AND THIS IS WHY. It is measured from region(d.current), the UNSHIFTED border box (events.jl:450). Scrollbar may use it only because \"a scrollbar is never inside a scrolled subtree\" (scroll.jl:683) – and a List inside a Scrollpane IS inside one. Inside a pane scrolled to y = 3 every click would select the row three above the pointer. painted_region's own docstring settles it: \"THIS, not region(w), is what a hit test must compare a pointer against\" (widget.jl:262).\n\nO(depth), on a mouse event, NEVER on a frame. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_local"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_measure-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_measure(s::AbstractString, cap::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\ntext_width(truncate_width(s, cap)) – the width of s, CAPPED at cap cells. O(cap), NEVER O(length(s)).\n\nTHE bound on every AUTO measurement, and it is not a micro-optimisation: truncate_width (unicode.jl:138) breaks out of its grapheme loop the moment the budget is exceeded, so this is thousands of times cheaper than text_width on a 100 000-character cell. A 10 KB description column would otherwise cost 10 KB of grapheme iteration per measured cell to discover a fact truncate_width already knows. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_mouse!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_mouse!(w::RowsWidget, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent}) -> Bool\n\n\nWheel scrolls the body, SHIFT swaps the axis; a LEFT PRESS puts the cursor on the row under the pointer and selects it; SHIFT extends from the anchor; CTRL toggles (MULTI); LEFT DRAG extends, so a press-drag paints a range. Returns true iff it consumed. Consumes ONLY when something changed.\n\nA press on the HEADER rows returns false WITHOUT touching the selection, so DataTable can act on it in its own on_event!. There is no on_header_click! seam: per-concrete-type on_event! methods over this shared internal have no shadowing problem to escape.\n\nA plain press routes through set_cursor!(w, k) and NOT select_only!(w, k): the two are IDENTICAL under SINGLE and MULTI (both re-pin the cursor and the anchor and select k alone), and they differ only under NONE, where select_only! is a no-op and a click would then not even move the cursor. A browsable list whose rows cannot be clicked onto is not browsable.\n\n_tc_local, NEVER local_offset. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_mouse!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_moved!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Bool}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_moved!(w::RowsWidget, changed::Bool) -> Bool\n\n\nPure op, then _tc_touch! and _tc_follow_cursor!. THE single exit of every cursor/selection change, so neither can be forgotten at a call site. _ta_moved! (textarea.jl:449) is the precedent, line for line. Returns changed. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_moved!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_noop-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_noop(_::Widget)\n\n\nThe default on_activate: do nothing. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_noop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_only!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_only!(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect i and nothing else, without touching the cursor or the anchor. True iff the set changed. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_only!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_page-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_page(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nRows one PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN travels: one body LESS ONE ROW of overlap, so the reader keeps a landmark. At least one, so an unlaid-out widget still moves. _ta_page and _sp_key_delta both say this; this is the third and last copy. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_page"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_replace_range!-Tuple{Selection, Int64, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_replace_range!(s::Selection, lo::Int64, hi::Int64)\n\n\nReplace the selection with the contiguous SOURCE range lo:hi, clipped to 1:n. A bitmap range, not hi - lo + 1 inserts. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_replace_range!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_resolve!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_resolve!(w::RowsWidget, avail::Int64) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nResolve every column of w to a cell width and an x offset for an avail-cell content box, MEMOIZED on (version, avail). Writes grid.widths, grid.xs, grid.cache_total and returns grid.widths.\n\nBOTH render! AND _tc_extent CALL THIS, and that is what makes max_scroll(t).x correct BEFORE the first paint. Reading a widths field that only render! writes would make max_scroll(t).x == 0 – \"cannot scroll\" when it can – until the first frame.\n\nNOT Pure, and this says so rather than hiding it: content_extent is documented Pure in scroll.jl:83 and this override writes a memo. The memo is OBSERVATIONALLY pure – same inputs, same output – and its key is (version, content-box width), NEITHER of which is a function of the scroll offset. That is the same property content_extent itself relies on (\"the extent is independent of the current scroll offset and a scroll can never feed back into the extent\"), and it is why the cache is safe. A future reader who trusts the base docstring will be surprised once; that is the price of not lying about max_scroll.\n\npaint.jl guarantees render! and _tc_extent agree on avail: a widget \"gets a frame that is its FULL content box, so size(buf) is the widget's real content box and is INVARIANT UNDER SCROLL\" (buffer.jl:232), so size(buf)[1] == layout_of(w).content.width always and the key never thrashes within a frame.\n\nON A HIT: O(1) to decide, ZERO allocation, and the scratch comes back. ON A MISS: _apportion allocates – once per edit or resize, never per frame. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_resolve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_resolve_now!-Tuple{TableGrid, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_resolve_now!(g::TableGrid, avail::Int64)\n\n\nResolve every column of g for an avail-cell content box, writing widths, xs and cache_total. PURE with respect to the tree: a TableGrid and an Int in, three scratch fields out, which is what makes the whole of SS4 a table test.\n\n1. `cells(n)`  -> `definite_size`. Exact, O(1).\n2. `pct(p)`    -> `definite_size` against the CONTENT BOX.\n3. `AUTO`      -> the cached mark `g.autos[j]`.\n4. `fr(n)`     -> a share of `max(0, inner - sum(the above))` via\n                  `_apportion`, the SAME largest-remainder-first\n                  kernel `_arrange!` step 3 uses (layout.jl:417), so\n                  `sum == leftover` EXACTLY and a column never\n                  drifts a cell.\n\ninner = avail - sep_w * (ncols - 1): the separators are paid for before the columns bid, which is _arrange!'s inner_main = main_avail - bs.gap * (n - 1) (layout.jl:388) verbatim.\n\nNORMATIVE: cache_total MAY EXCEED avail, and that excess IS the horizontal scroll range. flex_distribute is NOT called: with no shrink and no grow factor it is provably the identity (layout.jl:73), so calling it to claim reuse would be ceremony. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_resolve_now!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_row_at-Tuple{RowsWidget, MouseEvent}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_row_at(w::RowsWidget, e::MouseEvent) -> Int64\n\n\nThe 1-based VIEW row under mouse event e; 0 for the header, the chrome, the margins or past the last row.\n\nArithmetic, not a hit test: scroll.y + local.y - hh. There are no row widgets to hit-test, which is \"a row is not a widget\" seen from the mouse's side – hit_test walks nodes, so widget-per-row would be O(n) on every POINTER MOVE, not merely every frame. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_row_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_row_style-Tuple{RowsWidget, Style, Int64, Bool}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_row_style(\n    w::RowsWidget,\n    st::Style,\n    src::Int64,\n    foc::Bool\n) -> Style\n\n\nThe style row src is painted in: st, REVERSED when selected, and additionally UNDERLINE on the cursor row while focused.\n\nTwo independent bits, because they are two independent facts: in MULTI the cursor is frequently NOT on a selected row, and a user who cannot see which row ENTER will act on has no cursor at all. An unfocused list shows its selection and hides its cursor – exactly as TextInput hides its caret on blur (textarea.jl:246). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_row_style"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_scroll_x!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_scroll_x!(w::RowsWidget, d::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nScroll w horizontally by d cells. True iff the offset moved. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_scroll_x!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_show-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_show(s::AbstractString) -> AbstractString\n\n\nx as an AbstractString, without copying one that already is: string(s::String) === s, so a List{String} formats for ZERO allocation per row. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_show"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_sync!-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_sync!(w::RowsWidget)\n\n\nresize_selection!(selection_of(w), row_count(w)). Called at the TOP of render!, _tc_key! and _tc_mouse!.\n\nTHE self-healing guard, and it is the cure for the one footgun this whole family shares: items/rows are ALIASED and mutated behind version's back will not repaint. This makes a cursor past the end STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE even then – it downgrades the footgun from CORRUPTION to STALENESS. O(1) – one Int compare – when the count is unchanged, which is what makes it affordable every frame.\n\nMutating state inside render! is licensed by Scrollpane.render! (scroll.jl:389-415), which does exactly this and defends it at length: nothing here is a Reactive, so this marks NOTHING and cannot loop, and it converges. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_sync!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_touch!","page":"Columns and selection","text":"Bump w's PAINT cell. No default. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_touch!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_truncated-Tuple{SubString{String}, AbstractString}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_truncated(\n    t::SubString{String},\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when truncate_width actually cut something.\n\nncodeunits, NOT text_width(s) > cw: the obvious spelling walks the WHOLE untruncated string. _uw_string_backed (unicode.jl:123) returns a String/SubString{String} input UNCOPIED, so t shares s's codeunits and the comparison is exact. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_truncated"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_visible_cols-Tuple{TableGrid, Int64, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_visible_cols(\n    g::TableGrid,\n    off_x::Int64,\n    width::Int64\n) -> Tuple{Int64, Int64}\n\n\nlo:hi, the columns the window [off_x + 1, off_x + width] touches. ONE O(ncols) scan per frame, so the per-row loop runs only over visible columns. (1, 0) when none. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_visible_cols"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI._tc_wheel!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Dispatch{MouseEvent}, MouseEvent}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"_tc_wheel!(\n    w::RowsWidget,\n    d::Dispatch{MouseEvent},\n    e::MouseEvent\n) -> Bool\n\n\nScroll the body on a wheel notch; SHIFT swaps the axis. Consumes only when the offset actually moved, so a list at its limit lets the notch bubble to the next pane out – scroll chaining, out of the phase rule alone. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_wheel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.clear_selection!-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"clear_selection!(w::RowsWidget) -> Bool\n\n\nDeselect everything; the CURSOR STAYS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clear_selection!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.clear_selection!-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"clear_selection!(s::Selection) -> Bool\n\n\nDeselect everything; the CURSOR STAYS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clear_selection!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.grid_of","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The TableGrid of w. Table/DataTable only; a List has no columns and answers with a MethodError. No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.grid_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.is_focused-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"is_focused(w::RowsWidget) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while w holds the focus. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_focused"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.is_selected-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"is_selected(w::RowsWidget, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when SOURCE row i of w is selected. O(1), 0 bytes. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.is_selected-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"is_selected(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when SOURCE row i is selected.\n\nO(1), ZERO allocation (MEASURED). FRAME PATH: one call per VISIBLE row. Total for any i, including i <= 0 and i > n. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.move_cursor!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"move_cursor!(w::RowsWidget, d::Int64; extend) -> Bool\n\n\nMove the cursor by d VIEW rows, clamped. move_cursor!(w, typemax(Int) ÷ 2) is End and cannot run off it – ÷ 2 so that rank + d cannot overflow, which is _sp_key_delta's trick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.move_cursor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.n_rows-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"n_rows(s::Selection) -> Int64\n\n\nThe number of SOURCE rows s is sized for. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.n_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.n_selected-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"n_selected(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nHow many rows of w are selected. NEVER the frame path. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.n_selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.n_selected-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"n_selected(s::Selection) -> Int64\n\n\nHow many rows are selected. O(1) in NONE/SINGLE; O(n/64) in MULTI. NEVER the frame path. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.n_selected"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.reindex_delete!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"reindex_delete!(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nAs reindex_insert!, for a row DELETED at SOURCE index i. i itself is dropped from the selection; everything above moves down one; n shrinks. The cursor, if it was ON i, stays at i and is re-clamped, so it lands on the row that took i's place – or on the new last row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.reindex_delete!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.reindex_insert!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"reindex_insert!(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nShift the selection, cursor and anchor for a row INSERTED at SOURCE index i: everything at or above i moves up one, and n grows.\n\nAn index-keyed selection is only correct while the indices mean what they meant. Source indices are structurally safe against REORDERING; they are NOT safe against insertion, and this is the price, paid by the mutation rather than by the frame. O(n_selected).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.reindex_insert!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.resize_selection!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"resize_selection!(s::Selection, n::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nResize to n SOURCE rows: drop selected rows above n, clamp the cursor and anchor into 1:n (0 when n == 0). O(1) – ONE Int compare – when n is unchanged, which is what lets render! call it every frame as a self-healing guard. See _tc_sync!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.resize_selection!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.row_anchor-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"row_anchor(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nThe anchor's SOURCE row of w; 0 when there is no row. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.row_anchor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.row_anchor-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"row_anchor(s::Selection) -> Int64\n\n\nThe anchor's SOURCE row; 0 iff there is no row. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.row_anchor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.row_count","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The number of SOURCE rows of w. O(1). FRAME PATH. No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.row_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.row_cursor-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"row_cursor(w::RowsWidget) -> Int64\n\n\nThe cursor's SOURCE row of w; 0 when there is no row. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.row_cursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.row_cursor-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"row_cursor(s::Selection) -> Int64\n\n\nThe cursor's SOURCE row; 0 iff there is no row. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.row_cursor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.sel_extend_ids!-Tuple{Selection, Any}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"sel_extend_ids!(s::Selection, ids) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect exactly the SOURCE rows in ids, replacing the selection. MULTI only; SINGLE falls back to select_only!(s, last(ids)).\n\nids is ANY iterable of Int, and that is the whole reason tablecore never learns what a permutation is:\n\nsel_extend_ids!(s, min(a, b):max(a, b))            # List/Table\nsel_extend_ids!(s, (w.order[k] for k in lo:hi))    # DataTable\n\nBoth ARGUMENTS allocate ZERO and Selection is none the wiser. Ids outside 1:n are DROPPED, never stored.\n\nNORMATIVE: an extend REPLACES, it does not union. Shift+click after a run of ctrl+clicks replaces the selection with the anchor range – what every file manager does. toggle_row! is the documented escape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.sel_extend_ids!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_all!-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_all!(w::RowsWidget) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect every row. MULTI only. ctrl+a is NOT bound to this: bind! it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_all!-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_all!(s::Selection) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect 1:n. MULTI only. O(n/64). A user gesture, NEVER a frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_mode-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_mode(w::RowsWidget) -> ManyUI.SelectMode.T\n\n\nThe selection policy of w. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_mode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_mode-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_mode(s::Selection) -> ManyUI.SelectMode.T\n\n\nThe selection policy of s. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_mode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_only!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_only!(w::RowsWidget, k::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect VIEW row k and nothing else. A no-op under NONE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_only!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.select_only!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"select_only!(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nSelect i and NOTHING else; re-pin the cursor and the anchor to it. A  no-op under NONE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.select_only!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.selected_rows-Tuple{RowsWidget}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"selected_rows(w::RowsWidget) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nThe selected SOURCE rows of w, ASCENDING. ALLOCATES; this is what an application calls after ENTER, never what render! calls. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.selected_rows-Tuple{Selection}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"selected_rows(s::Selection) -> Vector{Int64}\n\n\nThe selected SOURCE rows, ASCENDING. ALLOCATES. NEVER the frame path; this is what an application calls after ENTER. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selected_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.selection_of","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The Selection of w. No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.selection_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.set_cursor!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"set_cursor!(w::RowsWidget, k::Int64; extend) -> Bool\n\n\nMove the cursor to VIEW row k, clamped. 0 is Home, typemax(Int) is End.\n\nVIEW, not source: \"go to the third row on screen\" is what a cursor means, and the selection stores SOURCE. view_source is the whole translation. A no-op returning false when view_count(w) == 0.\n\nextend = true REPLACES the selection with the VIEW range from the anchor to k, mapped back through view_source – and NOT with the SOURCE range set_cursor!(::Selection, i; extend = true) would use. On a List or a Table the two are identical, because view_source is the identity. On a DataTable they are NOT: the user shift-arrowing down the screen means the rows BETWEEN the two ON SCREEN, which is what the generator below says and what a source range would get wrong.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_cursor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.set_cursor!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"set_cursor!(s::Selection, i::Int64; extend) -> Bool\n\n\nMove the cursor to SOURCE row i, CLAMPED to 1:n; 0 when n == 0. 0 is Home and typemax(Int) is End – \"go as far as you can\" needs no knowledge of how far that is, which is _sp_key_delta's trick (scroll.jl:475) reused rather than reinvented.\n\nextend = false re-pins the anchor to i and, under SINGLE/MULTI, selects i ALONE. extend = true leaves the anchor and REPLACES the selection with anchor:i (MULTI), or moves the cursor alone (SINGLE/NONE – there is no range to extend to).\n\nNORMATIVE: anchor:i is a SOURCE range, which is the only thing a Selection can mean – it has no view. A widget whose view is a PERMUTATION must extend over VIEW rows instead, and set_cursor!(::RowsWidget, k; extend = true) is where that happens.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_cursor!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.toggle_row!-Tuple{RowsWidget, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"toggle_row!(w::RowsWidget, k::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nFlip VIEW row k's membership. MULTI only. See toggle_row!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle_row!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.toggle_row!-Tuple{Selection, Int64}","page":"Columns and selection","text":"toggle_row!(s::Selection, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nFlip i's membership; re-pin the cursor and anchor to it. MULTI only: under SINGLE this is a no-op returning false, because toggling the one selected row off would leave a single-select list with nothing selected, which is a contradiction. A no-op under NONE.\n\ni is NOT clamped: clamping a toggle would flip the WRONG row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.toggle_row!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.view_count","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The number of rows in VIEW order. O(1). No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.view_count"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.view_rank","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The VIEW row of SOURCE row s. O(1). No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.view_rank"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#ManyUI.view_source","page":"Columns and selection","text":"The SOURCE row behind VIEW row k. O(1). No default. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.view_source"},{"category":"section","location":"api/geometry/#Geometry-and-Unicode","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"","title":"Geometry and Unicode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Offset","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"struct Offset\n\nA 2D displacement in cells. isbits.\n\nFields\n\nx::Int64: Horizontal component.\ny::Int64: Vertical component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Offset"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Region","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"struct Region\n\nAn axis-aligned rectangle. 1-based and inclusive: the origin is the top-left cell (x, y) and the last cell is (x + width - 1, y + height - 1). isbits.\n\nFields\n\nx::Int64: Left edge, 1-based.\ny::Int64: Top edge, 1-based.\nwidth::Int64: Horizontal extent, in cells.\nheight::Int64: Vertical extent, in cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Region"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Region-Tuple{Size}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Region(s::Size) -> Region\n\n\nThe region covering a Size anchored at the origin: Region(1, 1, w, h).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Region"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Size","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"struct Size\n\nA width x height extent in terminal cells. isbits.\n\nAlways width/height, never cols/rows.\n\nFields\n\nwidth::Int64: Horizontal extent, in cells.\nheight::Int64: Vertical extent, in cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Size"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Spacing","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"struct Spacing\n\nPer-edge insets, in cells, in CSS order. isbits.\n\nFields\n\ntop::Int64: Top inset.\nright::Int64: Right inset.\nbottom::Int64: Bottom inset.\nleft::Int64: Left inset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Spacing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Spacing-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Spacing(vert::Int64, horz::Int64) -> Spacing\n\n\nCSS two-value shorthand: vert on top/bottom, horz on left/right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Spacing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Spacing-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Spacing(all::Int64) -> Spacing\n\n\nUniform spacing on all four edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Spacing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.:+-Tuple{Offset, Offset}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"+(a::Offset, b::Offset) -> Offset\n\n\nComponentwise sum of two offsets. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:+"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.:+-Tuple{Size, Size}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"+(a::Size, b::Size) -> Size\n\n\nComponentwise sum of two sizes. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:+"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.:+-Tuple{Spacing, Spacing}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"+(a::Spacing, b::Spacing) -> Spacing\n\n\nFieldwise sum of two Spacing values. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:+"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.:--Tuple{Offset, Offset}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"-(a::Offset, b::Offset) -> Offset\n\n\nComponentwise difference of two offsets. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:-"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.in-Tuple{Offset, Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"in(o::Offset, r::Region) -> Bool\n\n\nContainment test, inclusive at both ends. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.intersect-Tuple{Region, Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"intersect(a::Region, b::Region) -> Region\n\n\nOverlap of two regions, or EMPTY_REGION when disjoint.\n\nNEVER returns nothing: clipping is on the per-cell hot path and must stay type-stable. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.intersect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.isempty-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"isempty(r::Region) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when r covers no cell (width <= 0 || height <= 0). Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isempty"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.issubset-Tuple{Region, Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"issubset(a::Region, b::Region) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when every cell of a lies in b. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#Base.union-Tuple{Region, Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"union(a::Region, b::Region) -> Region\n\n\nBounding box of two regions. An empty operand is the identity. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.union"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.area-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"area(r::Region) -> Int64\n\n\nCell count of r, clamped at zero per axis. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.area"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.bottom-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"bottom(r::Region) -> Int64\n\n\nBottom row of r, inclusive: r.y + r.height - 1. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.bottom"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.clamp_size-Tuple{Size, Size, Size}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"clamp_size(s::Size, lo::Size, hi::Size) -> Size\n\n\nPer-axis clamp of s between lo and hi. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clamp_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.clamp_to-Tuple{Region, Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"clamp_to(r::Region, outer::Region) -> Region\n\n\nClip r to outer; identical to intersect. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.clamp_to"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.grow-Tuple{Region, Spacing}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"grow(r::Region, s::Spacing) -> Region\n\n\nOutset r by s. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.grow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.horizontal-Tuple{Spacing}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"horizontal(s::Spacing) -> Int64\n\n\nTotal horizontal inset: left + right. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.horizontal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.origin-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"origin(r::Region) -> Offset\n\n\nTop-left cell of r as an Offset. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.origin"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.right-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"right(r::Region) -> Int64\n\n\nRightmost column of r, inclusive: r.x + r.width - 1. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.right"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.shrink-Tuple{Region, Spacing}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"shrink(r::Region, s::Spacing) -> Region\n\n\nInset r by s. Width and height CLAMP TO ZERO, never go negative: layout underflow must degrade to an empty region, not to garbage that crashes a writer. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.shrink"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.size_of-Tuple{Region}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"size_of(r::Region) -> Size\n\n\nExtent of r as a Size. Deliberately NOT Base.size: a Region is not an array. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.size_of"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.split_col-Tuple{Region, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"split_col(r::Region, at::Int64) -> Tuple{Region, Region}\n\n\nSplit r vertically; at is the number of COLUMNS in the left piece. The two pieces partition r exactly. Pure.\n\nat is clamped to 0:r.width, so neither piece ever has a negative extent and area(left) + area(right) == area(r) always holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.split_col"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.split_row-Tuple{Region, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"split_row(r::Region, at::Int64) -> Tuple{Region, Region}\n\n\nSplit r horizontally; at is the number of ROWS in the top piece. The two pieces partition r exactly. Pure.\n\nat is clamped to 0:r.height, so neither piece ever has a negative extent and area(top) + area(bottom) == area(r) always holds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.split_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.translate-Tuple{Region, Offset}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"translate(r::Region, o::Offset) -> Region\n\n\nMove r by o, keeping its extent. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.translate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.vertical-Tuple{Spacing}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"vertical(s::Spacing) -> Int64\n\n\nTotal vertical inset: top + bottom. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.vertical"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.UW_RI_FIRST","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"First regional indicator, U+1F1E6 (letter A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.UW_RI_FIRST"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.UW_RI_LAST","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Last regional indicator, U+1F1FF (letter Z).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.UW_RI_LAST"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.VS15","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Variation Selector-15: forces text (narrow) presentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.VS15"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.VS16","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Variation Selector-16: forces emoji (wide) presentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.VS16"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.ZWJ","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"Zero Width Joiner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ZWJ"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI._uw_break_word!-Tuple{Vector{String}, AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"_uw_break_word!(\n    out::Vector{String},\n    word::AbstractString,\n    w::Int64\n) -> SubString{String}\n\n\nEmit whole lines for a word wider than w and return the trailing remainder, whose text_width is <= w.\n\nA single cluster wider than the entire budget is given a line of its own: halving it would corrupt the grid, and dropping it would lose content and spin this loop forever.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._uw_break_word!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI._uw_string_backed-Tuple{String}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"_uw_string_backed(s::String) -> String\n\n\nReinterpret any string as a String-backed one, without copying when it already is. Keeps truncate_width's return type SubString{String} type-stable for String and SubString{String} inputs alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._uw_string_backed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI._uw_wrap_line!-Tuple{Vector{String}, AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"_uw_wrap_line!(\n    out::Vector{String},\n    line::AbstractString,\n    w::Int64\n)\n\n\nGreedily pack the words of one hard line into out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._uw_wrap_line!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.char_width-Tuple{AbstractChar}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"char_width(c::AbstractChar) -> Int64\n\n\nDisplay cells for a single CODEPOINT: 0, 1 or 2. Pure.\n\nPer-codepoint width comes straight from the Unicode width tables (Base.textwidth on a Char, which is a table lookup, NOT the codepoint-summing string method this file exists to avoid). The result is clamped into 0:2, and an invalid codepoint measures 0 so this function is total and never throws on the render hot path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.char_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.grapheme_cells-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"grapheme_cells(\n    s::AbstractString\n) -> Vector{Tuple{String, Int64}}\n\n\nSplit s into (cluster, width) pairs – the unit a Buffer stores. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.grapheme_cells"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.grapheme_width-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"grapheme_width(g::AbstractString) -> Int64\n\n\nDisplay cells for ONE grapheme cluster: 0, 1 or 2. Pure.\n\nNORMATIVE rule, applied in order. Peek codepoints via iterate; MUST NOT collect (that allocates a Vector{Char} per cell per frame).\n\nempty cluster                                    -> 0\nfirst two codepoints are both regional indicators -> 2\ncluster contains VS16 (U+FE0F)                    -> 2\ncluster ends with VS15 (U+FE0E)                   -> 1\nbase (first) codepoint is a control               -> 0\notherwise clamp(char_width(base), 0, 2) – every trailing codepoint (ZWJ, skin tone modifier, combining mark) contributes 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.grapheme_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.is_combining-Tuple{AbstractChar}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"is_combining(c::AbstractChar) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when c occupies no cell of its own. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_combining"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.is_regional_indicator-Tuple{AbstractChar}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"is_regional_indicator(c::AbstractChar) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue for U+1F1E6..U+1F1FF, the flag building blocks. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_regional_indicator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.is_wide-Tuple{AbstractChar}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"is_wide(c::AbstractChar) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue when c occupies two cells. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.is_wide"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.text_width-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"text_width(s::AbstractString) -> Int64\n\n\nSum of grapheme_width over Unicode.graphemes(s). Pure.\n\nNEVER uses textwidth(s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.text_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.truncate_width-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"truncate_width(\n    s::AbstractString,\n    w::Int64\n) -> SubString{String}\n\n\nLongest prefix of s whose text_width is <= w. A width-2 cluster that would straddle w is DROPPED, not halved. Pure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.truncate_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.wrap_width-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"wrap_width(s::AbstractString, w::Int64) -> Vector{String}\n\n\nGreedy word wrap on text_width. Breaks mid-word only when a single word exceeds w. Never splits a grapheme cluster. Pure.\n\nWords are whitespace-separated; an explicit newline is a hard break, so a blank input line survives as an empty output line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.wrap_width"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Event","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"abstract type Event\n\nSupertype of every input/system event.\n\nFields\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Event"},{"category":"type","location":"api/geometry/#ManyUI.Widget","page":"Geometry and Unicode","text":"abstract type Widget\n\nBase type of every UI component. Implementors provide exactly one required method: node(w::MyWidget)::WidgetNode.\n\nFields\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.Widget"},{"category":"section","location":"api/text/#Text-entry-widgets","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"","title":"Text entry widgets"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._TI_CARET","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"The caret's own style: REVERSE video, MERGED onto whatever cell the caret rests on so the glyph under it survives. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._TI_CARET"},{"category":"type","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.TextInput","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"mutable struct TextInput{F} <: Widget\n\nSingle-line text entry.\n\ntext and cursor are Dirty.PAINT-reactive, and that is a design commitment rather than an optimisation: measure returns Size(avail.width, 1), so a TextInput NEVER resizes to its content – that is what the horizontal scroll is for. A keystroke provably cannot move a single box, so a keystroke costs ZERO layout.\n\nReactive's default is the conservative Dirty.LAYOUT precisely because choosing PAINT for state that can change size is a correctness bug. The text-independent measure above is what licenses PAINT here; do not copy the choice to a widget without that property.\n\nThe horizontal scroll lives in node(w).scroll.x. The core never applies it (a TextInput has no children) – render! reads it and slices. Storing it there anyway is what lets a Scrollbar observe a TextInput with no new code.\n\nParametric on the submit handler, the Button{F} pattern.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\ntext::Reactive{String}: The content. PAINT-reactive: the box cannot move.\ncursor::Reactive{Int64}: Caret, a 0-based GRAPHEME index in 0:n. PAINT-reactive.\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused; drives the caret cell. PAINT-reactive.\nplaceholder::String\non_submit::Any: Called as on_submit(input) on ENTER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TextInput"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.TextInput-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{AbstractString}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractString, F}} where F","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"An input holding text, calling on_submit(input) on ENTER.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring.\n\nThe caret starts at the END of text, where a user who is handed a pre-filled field expects to carry on typing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TextInput"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._byte_after-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_byte_after(s::AbstractString, k::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nCode units in the first k graphemes of s; 0 for k <= 0 and ncodeunits(s) past the end. O(k). Pure.\n\nThe sum of whole clusters' ncodeunits, so the result is always a whole number of CLUSTERS and can never split one. This is THE one place byte and cluster indices meet. Internal.\n\nIt is a code-unit COUNT and NOT a character index, so SubString(s, 1, b) is WRONG: that form wants the INDEX of the last character and throws a StringIndexError the moment the prefix ends inside a multi-byte cluster – which is every CJK and every emoji. Split with _ti_head and _ti_tail, which take the same thisind precaution truncate_width already takes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._byte_after"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._cluster_width_at-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_cluster_width_at(s::AbstractString, b::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nCells of the cluster starting just after byte b; 1 at the end of s, so the caret always has a cell to sit in. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._cluster_width_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._col_at_cell-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_col_at_cell(s::AbstractString, cell::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe largest grapheme index of s whose clusters END at or before cell column cell. The inverse of \"cells before the caret\", and the reason an UP/DOWN move can never land INSIDE a wide cluster. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._col_at_cell"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._gindex_at-Tuple{AbstractString, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_gindex_at(s::AbstractString, b::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nClusters of s that END at or before byte b – the caret index for a caret sitting at byte b. Rounds DOWN inside a cluster. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._gindex_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ngraphemes-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ngraphemes(s::AbstractString) -> Int64\n\n\nNumber of grapheme clusters in s. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ngraphemes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_caret-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_caret(w::TextInput) -> Int64\n\n\nThe caret, clamped into 0:n. Internal.\n\ntext and cursor are two independent Reactives, so an application that assigns w.text[] behind the editing ops' back can leave the caret past the end. Clamping on every READ makes every op below total, instead of scattering the same guard through each of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_caret"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_caret_cells-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_caret_cells(w::TextInput) -> Tuple{Int64, Int64}\n\n\n(cells before the caret, cells of the cluster AT the caret). Pure with respect to the tree. Internal.\n\nThe second element is at least 1 even over a zero-width cluster, so the caret always has a cell of its own to reverse.\n\nONE pass over the graphemes, and this is the frame path: the obvious spelling – _byte_after, then text_width of the head, then _cluster_width_at – walks the clusters THREE times and each Unicode.graphemes costs an iterator per call. Walking once and stopping AT the caret subsumes all three, and it clamps cursor into 0:n on the way for free: a want below the range returns on the first cluster and one past it falls out of the loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_caret_cells"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_edits-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_edits(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target – everywhere except the capture phase.\n\nA TextInput edits on the way UP, exactly as a Button activates: capture belongs to ancestors that want to intercept, and an input is never an interceptor. AT_TARGET counts because a childless input IS the target, and the bubble phase excludes the target, so bubble alone would never visit it. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_edits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_head-Tuple{String, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_head(s::String, b::Int64) -> SubString{String}\n\n\ns's first b CODE UNITS, where b came from _byte_after. Pure. Internal.\n\nthisind is the whole point and is not defensive programming: SubString(s, 1, b) reads b as the INDEX of the last CHARACTER, so it throws a StringIndexError for any prefix ending inside a multi-byte cluster – SubString(\"a世\", 1, 4) throws, verified. thisind maps the count onto the index of the character it lands on, which for a cluster boundary is that character's start, so the split is exact and never loses a byte. truncate_width takes the same precaution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_head"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_noop-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_noop(_::Widget)\n\n\nThe default on_submit: do nothing. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_noop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_strip_controls-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_strip_controls(t::AbstractString) -> String\n\n\nt with every control character removed. Pure. Internal.\n\nPer CODEPOINT rather than per cluster, which is safe precisely because a control is never part of a printable cluster: ZWJ, the variation selectors and the skin-tone modifiers are format and symbol codepoints, not Cc, so no cluster this strips through is one it can break. CRLF is a single cluster of two controls and goes whole.\n\nReturns t itself when there is nothing to strip, so the ordinary paste costs one scan and no copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_strip_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_sync_scroll!-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_sync_scroll!(w::TextInput)\n\n\nStore the window visible_scroll computes, so the caret is visible on the next frame. Internal.\n\nA no-op before the first layout: with no content box there is no window to be inside, and render! recomputes the offset from the width it is actually given in any case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_sync_scroll!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_tail-Tuple{String, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_tail(s::String, b::Int64) -> SubString{String}\n\n\ns from byte b + 1 on, where b came from _byte_after. Pure. Internal.\n\nLegal at BOTH ends without a special case: b is a cluster boundary, so b + 1 starts a character, and one past the last byte yields the empty string rather than a BoundsError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_tail"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ti_window-Tuple{TextInput, Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ti_window(\n    w::TextInput,\n    width::Int64,\n    lo::Int64,\n    cw::Int64\n) -> Int64\n\n\nThe window for a caret whose cluster spans cells lo:(lo + cw - 1), 0-based, in a width-cell content box. Pure. Internal.\n\nSplit out of visible_scroll so render! can pay for the caret's column ONCE and reuse it, rather than walking the prefix twice per frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ti_window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.backspace!-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"backspace!(w::TextInput) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete the cluster BEFORE the caret. False at the start of the text.\n\nThe WHOLE cluster goes: one backspace over a ZWJ family takes all 25 of its bytes, not the last codepoint of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.backspace!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"content_extent(w::TextInput) -> Size\n\n\nThe content extent for the scrollable seam: Size(text_width(text) + 1, 1).\n\nThe + 1 is load-bearing: the caret must be able to rest ONE cell past the last glyph, and without it End scrolls one cell short.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.delete_forward!-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"delete_forward!(w::TextInput) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete the cluster AT the caret. False at the end of the text.\n\nThe caret does not move: it is the same count of clusters from the start as it was, which is why it is recomputed from the same byte offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.delete_forward!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.insert_text!-Tuple{TextInput, AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"insert_text!(w::TextInput, t::AbstractString)\n\n\nInsert t at the caret. The caret is RECOMPUTED from the new prefix, never advanced by the inserted cluster count.\n\nRecomputing is the only rule that is right for every input: a combining mark MERGES into the preceding cluster, so the cluster count may not increase at all, and an advance-by-n caret would then sit past the end of a string that never grew.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_text!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{TextInput, Size}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"measure(w::TextInput, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\nSize(avail.width, 1). A TextInput takes the width it is offered and scrolls the rest; it never sizes to its content. Give it width: 20 for a narrow box. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.move_by!-Tuple{TextInput, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"move_by!(w::TextInput, n::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nMove the caret by n CLUSTERS, clamped to 0:n. Returns the new caret.\n\nn is a count of CLUSTERS and never of codepoints or cells, so LEFT over a ZWJ family is one call with n = -1 and lands before all seven of its codepoints.\n\nSaturates instead of overflowing: the caret is non-negative, so only the rightward sum can leave Int, and a request that far right is an End by any reading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.move_by!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.move_to!-Tuple{TextInput, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"move_to!(w::TextInput, i::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nMove the caret to cluster i, clamped to 0:n: 0 is Home and typemax(Int) is End. Returns the new caret.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.move_to!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::TextInput)\n\n\nHide the caret.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TextInput, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TextInput, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nCHAR/SPACE insert; BACKSPACE/DELETE/LEFT/RIGHT/HOME/END edit and move; ENTER calls on_submit. Each consumes.\n\nUnmodified keys only. ctrl+a and friends belong to an application binding and consuming them here would silently shadow it. TAB and ESCAPE FALL THROUGH UNCONSUMED, which is what keeps the tab order alive.\n\nHOME and END are move_to! with the extremes of Int and let the clamp decide – \"go as far as you can\" IS the implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TextInput, Dispatch{PasteEvent}}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TextInput, d::Dispatch{PasteEvent})\n\n\nInsert a paste at the caret with newlines and other controls stripped: a single-line input has nowhere to put a line break, and silently accepting one would make text unrenderable.\n\nConsumes either way. A paste that strips to nothing was still a paste into this input, and letting the husk bubble to an ancestor would be a second delivery of the same event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{TextInput}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::TextInput)\n\n\nShow the caret, and scroll every ancestor pane until this input is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.visible_scroll-Tuple{TextInput, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"visible_scroll(w::TextInput, width::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nThe first visible CELL column, 0-based, for a width-cell content box.\n\nTHE single definition of \"scrolled so the caret is visible\", called by render! (which MUST NOT mutate) AND by every editing op (which stores the result). One function, so the painted scroll and the stored one cannot drift. Pure.\n\nMINIMAL MOVEMENT falls out of scroll_into_view: content already inside the window does not move, and the clamp at content_extent is what lets End rest on the caret's own cell instead of one short of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.visible_scroll"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._TA_CARET","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"The caret's cell: REVERSE video, merged onto whatever is under it.\n\nA caret is not a hardware cursor – the tree paints into a Buffer and the Driver seam has no cursor-placement method. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._TA_CARET"},{"category":"type","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.TextArea","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"mutable struct TextArea{F} <: Widget\n\nMulti-line text entry.\n\nScrolls by INDEXING lines, never by shifting an origin: render! touches scroll.y + 1 : scroll.y + height and nothing else, so paint is O(viewport) and a 100k-line buffer costs the same frame as a 10-line one.\n\nlines is a PLAIN field, deliberately NOT a Reactive: Reactive's == guard would be an O(n) elementwise compare of a Vector{String} on every keystroke, AND an in-place edit would never trip it. version is the reactive cell instead – an Int compare is O(1) for the O(1) edit that actually happened. THE PRICE, stated so nobody discovers it: lines mutated behind version's back will not repaint, so every edit goes through the ops below.\n\nThe cursor is (line, col): line 1-based into lines, col a 0-based GRAPHEME index within lines[line]. Same unit discipline as TextInput.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nlines::Vector{String}\nversion::Reactive{Int64}: Bumped by every edit. THE reactive cell. LAYOUT-reactive: a new line genuinely changes the extent.\n\nline::Int64: Cursor line, 1-based.\ncol::Int64: Cursor column, a 0-based GRAPHEME index within lines[line].\ngoal::Int64: The CELL column UP/DOWN aim for. See _ta_set_goal!.\nwidest::Int64: Monotone high-water mark of the widest line, in cells.\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused.\non_change::Any: Called as on_change(area) after every edit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TextArea"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.TextArea-Union{Tuple{}, Tuple{AbstractString}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{AbstractString, F}} where F","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"An area holding text, calling on_change(area) after every edit.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.TextArea"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_acts-Tuple{Dispatch}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_acts(d::Dispatch) -> Bool\n\n\nTrue while d is live and at or past its target – everywhere except the capture phase, which belongs to ancestors that want to intercept. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_acts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_cells_before-Tuple{String, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_cells_before(s::String, k::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nCells of the first k clusters of s – the CELL column of a caret at grapheme k, 0-based. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_cells_before"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_edited!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_edited!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nBump version, re-pin goal, fire on_change. THE single exit of every edit, so none of the three can be forgotten at a call site. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_edited!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_follow_caret!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_follow_caret!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nScroll the MINIMUM needed to bring the caret inside the content box, on both axes, via scroll_into_view. Internal.\n\nset_scroll! and NOT scroll_to!: content_extent is widest cells wide, and the caret must be able to rest ONE cell PAST the last glyph of the widest line. scroll_to! would clamp that last cell away and hide the caret at the right edge – the over-scroll is one blank column, and the contract licenses it explicitly (\"a UX bug, never corruption\").\n\nA no-op before the first layout, when the content box is still empty: there is no window to move yet, and set_text!/render! are correct at offset zero regardless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_follow_caret!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_move_col!-Tuple{TextArea, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_move_col!(w::TextArea, col::Int64)\n\n\nMove the caret to cluster col of the CURRENT line, clamped: 0 is Home and typemax(Int) is End. A horizontal move, so it re-pins goal. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_move_col!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_moved!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_moved!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nRe-pin goal, mark PAINT and follow the caret. THE single exit of every HORIZONTAL move, so none of the three can be forgotten. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_moved!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_noop-Tuple{Widget}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_noop(_::Widget)\n\n\nThe default on_change: do nothing. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_noop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_page-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_page(w::TextArea) -> Int64\n\n\nLines one PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN travels: one viewport LESS ONE ROW of overlap, so the reader keeps a landmark. At least one, so an unlaid-out area still moves. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_page"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_set_goal!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_set_goal!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nPin goal to the caret's current CELL column. Called by every HORIZONTAL move and every edit – and by nothing else.\n\ngoal is in CELLS, not graphemes: cells are the unit the user can see, and a grapheme goal lands UP/DOWN in a visually different column across wide clusters. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_set_goal!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_split-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_split(s::AbstractString) -> Vector{String}\n\n\ns split on newlines into a document. NEVER empty: an empty string is [\"\"], which is exactly what makes lines[line] total. Pure. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_split"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_split_at-Tuple{String, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_split_at(\n    s::String,\n    k::Int64\n) -> Tuple{SubString{String}, SubString{String}}\n\n\ns cut at grapheme k: the prefix of the first k clusters, and the rest. Neither half can split a codepoint, let alone a cluster. Pure. Internal.\n\n_byte_after returns a COUNT of code units, and a code-unit count is NOT a Julia string index: SubString(\"世界\", 1, 3) THROWS, because 3 is a continuation byte, while SubString(\"世界\", 1, 1) is the whole first cluster. thisind maps the count back onto the character it lands in, which is what makes the prefix legal; the SUFFIX takes the count + 1 directly, because that always IS a character start. This is the one place in the file where a byte and a cluster index meet, and it meets them through the shared helpers alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_split_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_step_left!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_step_left!(w::TextArea) -> Bool\n\n\nOne cluster left, crossing into the previous line at column 0. False at the start of the DOCUMENT. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_step_left!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_step_right!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_step_right!(w::TextArea) -> Bool\n\n\nOne cluster right, crossing into the next line at the end of this one. False at the end of the DOCUMENT. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_step_right!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI._ta_widen!-Tuple{TextArea, AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"_ta_widen!(w::TextArea, s::AbstractString)\n\n\nRaise the high-water mark to fit s. O(text_width(s)), never O(lines). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._ta_widen!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.backspace!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"backspace!(w::TextArea) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete the cluster BEFORE the caret; at column 0 of line n > 1 this JOINS with the previous line. False at (1, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.backspace!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"content_extent(w::TextArea) -> Size\n\n\nSize(widest, length(lines)). OVERRIDES the container default: a TextArea's content is data, not children, so the bounding box of its (nonexistent) children is not its extent. This override IS the whole integration with Scrollbar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.delete_forward!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"delete_forward!(w::TextArea) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete the cluster AT the caret; at the end of line n < end this JOINS the next line. False at the end of the document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.delete_forward!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.insert_newline!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"insert_newline!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nSplit the current line at the caret.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_newline!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.insert_text!-Tuple{TextArea, AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"insert_text!(w::TextArea, t::AbstractString)\n\n\nInsert t at the caret; a '\\n' splits the line.\n\nThe caret is RECOMPUTED from the new prefix, never advanced by the inserted cluster count: a combining mark MERGES into the preceding cluster and the count may not increase at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_text!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{TextArea, Size}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"measure(w::TextArea, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\navail. A TextArea takes the space it is offered and scrolls its content: an auto-HEIGHT TextArea would be as tall as its text and would never scroll at all. Give it height: 10 or a grow: 1 parent. Pure with respect to the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.move_by!-Tuple{TextArea, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"move_by!(w::TextArea, n::Int64)\n\n\nMove the caret by n CLUSTERS, wrapping across lines. Resets goal.\n\nClamped at both ends of the document: move_by!(w, typemax(Int) ÷ 2) is End-of-document and cannot run off it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.move_by!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.move_line!-Tuple{TextArea, Int64}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"move_line!(w::TextArea, n::Int64) -> Int64\n\n\nMove the caret by n LINES, landing on the cluster whose prefix width is the largest NOT EXCEEDING goal. Does NOT reset goal. Returns the new line.\n\n_col_at_cell is what makes the landing safe: an UP/DOWN move can never come to rest INSIDE a wide cluster, and a run down through a SHORT line and back up returns to the column the user started in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.move_line!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_blur!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nHide the caret.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TextArea, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TextArea, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nCHAR/SPACE insert; ENTER splits; BACKSPACE/DELETE edit; arrows, PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN and HOME/END move. Each consumes.\n\nUnmodified keys only: ctrl+a and friends belong to an application binding and consuming them here would silently shadow it. TAB and ESCAPE FALL THROUGH UNCONSUMED, which is what keeps the tab order alive.\n\nHOME and END are per LINE, not per document – that is what a caret in a text editor means, and Scrollpane's document-wide HOME/END still reach an ancestor pane whenever this area is not focused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{TextArea, Dispatch{PasteEvent}}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_event!(w::TextArea, d::Dispatch{PasteEvent})\n\n\nInsert a paste at the caret, splitting it on newlines into real lines.\n\nUnlike TextInput, which has nowhere to put a line break and strips them, a TextArea is exactly the widget a multi-line paste belongs in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"on_focus!(w::TextArea)\n\n\nShow the caret, and scroll every ancestor pane until this area is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.refresh_extent!-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"refresh_extent!(w::TextArea) -> Size\n\n\nRecompute widest from scratch. O(lines).\n\nwidest is a MONOTONE HIGH-WATER MARK: every edit raises it to max(widest, text_width(changed_line)) in O(1) and nothing lowers it. THE TRADEOFF, stated so nobody discovers it: after the longest line is deleted the horizontal range stays too wide – the thumb is slightly too small and there is slack to the right – until this is called. set_text! calls it. The alternative, an O(lines) rescan per keystroke, costs the editor to save a scrollbar thumb one cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_extent!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.set_text!-Tuple{TextArea, AbstractString}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"set_text!(w::TextArea, s::AbstractString)\n\n\nReplace the document with s, split on newlines. Resets the caret, the scroll and the extent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_text!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/text/#ManyUI.text-Tuple{TextArea}","page":"Text entry widgets","text":"text(w::TextArea) -> String\n\n\nThe document as one string, lines joined by \\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.text"},{"category":"section","location":"#ManyUI.jl","page":"Home","text":"ManyUI is a unified, declarative User Interface framework for Julia. \n\nIts philosophy is simple: write your domain model and UI presentation once, and render it anywhere.  Instead of tightly coupling your code to a single platform, ManyUI uses a hierarchical widget tree and an event-driven architecture that can be projected onto multiple backends seamlessly.\n\nThe ecosystem is extended by companion packages that provide different rendering projections:\n\nManyUI.jl (Core) The pure domain model and reactive widget tree. Completely platform-agnostic, with zero rendering dependencies.\nManyUITUI.jl (Terminal Backend) Render your application directly in the terminal with full interactivity, utilizing an optimized diffing renderer that emits minimal ANSI escape sequences.\nManyUIWeb.jl\nWebTerminal: Serve your TUI application over the web. The terminal is emulated in the browser via WebSockets.\nWebNative: Translate the exact same widget tree into native HTML and DOM elements for a true semantic web experience.\nManyUICLI.jl Automatically generate a Command-Line Interface from your declarative UI model using Comonicon.jl.\nManyUIDemos.jl A centralized demonstration hub showcasing how to write a domain model once and launch it across all ManyUI projections.","title":"ManyUI.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.develop(path = \"path/to/ManyUI\")\n\nTo also serve your application in a browser, add ManyUIWeb. It depends on ManyUI, so a pure terminal application never pays for HTTP.jl or WebSocket handling.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quickstart","page":"Home","text":"A counter: a label, a button, and a click that updates what the label says.\n\nusing ManyUI\n\nclicks = Ref(0)\nreadout = Label(\"Count: 0\"; id = :count)\n\nui = Container(\n    readout,\n    Button(\"Click me\", _ -> begin\n               clicks[] += 1\n               readout.text[] = \"Count: $(clicks[])\"\n               nothing\n           end; id = :go),\n)\n\n# `HeadlessDriver` renders into memory instead of a terminal, which is\n# what makes the examples on this page runnable. Swap it for\n# `TerminalDriver()` to draw on a real tty, or hand the same tree to\n# `ManyUIWeb.serve` to draw it in a browser.\ndriver = HeadlessDriver(Size(40, 8))\napp = App(ui, driver)\n\nManyUI.start!(driver, Size(40, 8))\nhandle!(app, ResizeEvent(Size(40, 8)))\nframe!(app)\n\nnothing # hide\n\nAssigning to readout.text is the whole update. Label.text is reactive, so writing it marks the label dirty and the next frame repaints it — nothing calls a render function by hand.\n\nWidgets are addressable by their CSS id, and a click routed through hit testing reaches the right one:\n\nbutton = query_one(ui, \"#go\")\nr = region(button)\nclear_output!(driver)   # forget the first frame; watch just this click\n\ndispatch_event!(ui, MouseEvent(MouseAction.PRESS, MouseButton.LEFT,\n                               r.x, r.y, MOD_NONE))\nframe!(app)\n\n(clicks = clicks[], text = readout.text[])\n\nNow look at what that click actually cost. Count: 0 became Count: 1, so exactly one cell changed, and the diff sends exactly one cell:\n\nString(take_bytes!(driver))\n\n\\e[1;8H1 is the entire counter update: move the cursor to row 1, column 8, and write 1. The button is redrawn too because clicking focused it, which genuinely changed how it looks. Nothing else on the screen is touched.","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"#Running-on-a-real-terminal","page":"Home","text":"TerminalDriver puts the host terminal into raw mode and the alternate screen buffer, so an application never overwrites the user's shell history, and restores both even if the application throws:\n\nusing ManyUI\n\napp = App(my_ui(), TerminalDriver())\nrun!(app)   # blocks until quit!(app)","title":"Running on a real terminal"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Concepts — the render pipeline and the driver seam, the idea the rest of the framework hangs off.\nLayout — the box model and flex sizing.\nStyling — the CSS-like syntax, selectors and colors.\nEvents — capture, bubble and consumption.\nWidgets — the built-in widget library.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"module","location":"#ManyUI.ManyUI","page":"Home","text":"Terminal-first UI framework: a widget tree, a CSS-like box model, a diffing renderer and an ANSI encoder, behind a nine-method Driver seam.\n\nThis package has NO web, HTTP or socket dependency and never will; ManyUIWeb plugs into the Driver seam from the outside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.ManyUI"},{"category":"section","location":"api/drivers/#Drivers","page":"Drivers","text":"","title":"Drivers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/list/#List","page":"List","text":"","title":"List"},{"category":"type","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.List","page":"List","text":"mutable struct List{T, F, A} <: RowsWidget\n\nA scrollable, focusable list of items.\n\nScrolls by INDEXING items, never by shifting an origin: render! touches scroll.y + 1 : scroll.y + height and nothing else, so paint is O(viewport) and a 100 000-item list costs the same frame as a 10-item one.\n\nA ROW IS NOT A WIDGET. TextArea's argument (textarea.jl:6), one type up: a Scrollpane inside would mean one widget per ROW, so 100 000 items become 100 000 WidgetNodes, a full layout pass per push_item! and 100 000 render! dispatches per frame – and, the part that is easy to miss, 100 000 hit-test nodes, so hit_test would be O(n) on every POINTER MOVE. 100 000 rows are 100 000 elements of a Vector and ZERO WidgetNodes.\n\nPARAMETRIC ON BOTH the element type and the formatter, the Button{F}/TextInput{F}/TextArea{F} pattern the codebase already uses three times: items[i] is a TYPED load and w.format(item) is a STATIC dispatch. List{String,typeof(_tc_show)} formats for ZERO allocation per row, because _tc_show(::String) returns its argument (string(s::String) === s).\n\nTHIS is why a List is not a one-column Table, and it is not cosmetic: see Table for why a table's cell callback is on the widget. Compare Label vs Static (label.jl:98) – two types because one of them must not pay the other's cost.\n\nitems is a PLAIN field, deliberately NOT a Reactive: Reactive's == guard would be an O(n) elementwise compare on every change AND an in-place edit would never trip it. version is the reactive cell instead – an Int compare is O(1) for the O(1) edit that happened. THE PRICE, stated so nobody discovers it: items mutated behind version's back will not repaint – call refresh_rows!. It will NOT CORRUPT, because render! re-syncs the selection's row count every frame (_tc_sync!, O(1) when unchanged).\n\nitems is ALIASED, never copied: copying 100 000 rows is exactly the O(n) this widget exists to avoid.\n\nversion is Dirty.PAINT-reactive, and this DIVERGES from TextArea.version (LAYOUT) ON PURPOSE. TextInput states the criterion verbatim (textinput.jl:158): \"Reactive's default is the conservative Dirty.LAYOUT precisely because choosing PAINT for state that can change size is a correctness bug. The text-independent measure above is what licenses PAINT here; do not copy the choice to a widget without that property.\" measure(::List, avail) = avail HAS that property, and compute_layout NEVER calls content_extent. A data change therefore PROVABLY cannot move a box, so push_item! on a 100 000-item list costs ZERO layout and never fires escalate_auto!. TextArea's LAYOUT is conservative, not normative.\n\nformat(item) MUST return an AbstractString and MUST NOT contain a newline – a list row is ONE row. Same contract as TextArea.lines, and policed the same way: not at all.\n\nFields\n\nnode::WidgetNode: Per-widget state.\nitems::Vector\nformat::Any\nversion::Reactive{Int64}: Bumped by every data OR selection change. THE reactive cell. Dirty.PAINT – see above.\n\nsel::Selection\nwidest::Int64: MONOTONE high-water mark of the widest row, in cells. NEVER an UNDER-estimate once scanned – see _lst_scan! for why that direction is the whole ballgame.\n\nscanned::Bool: True IFF widest is known not to UNDERSTATE any row. The memo bit of _lst_scan!; false seeds the one lazy O(items) scan.\n\nfocused::Reactive{Bool}: True while focused. PAINT-reactive.\non_activate::Any: Called as on_activate(list) on ENTER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.List"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.List-Tuple{AbstractVector, Vararg{Any}}","page":"List","text":"A list over any AbstractVector, collected ONCE into a Vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.List"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.List-Union{Tuple{Vector{T}}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, A}} where {T, F, A}","page":"List","text":"A list over items, calling on_activate(list) on ENTER.\n\non_activate is the SECOND POSITIONAL and it is NOT on_change: List(files, f -> open(f)) with an on_change there would fire on every arrow key, which is a trap the shape of the call invites. There is ONE callback and it means what it says.\n\nFocusable by construction, so it appears in focusable_widgets and is reachable by TAB with no further wiring.\n\nitems is ALIASED, not copied. List(1:100_000) collects, once, and the caller knows what they paid.\n\nwidest starts at ZERO and the formatter is called NOT ONCE here: the mark is DEFERRED, not skipped. scanned starts false and _lst_scan! pays the O(items) measurement at the first query – which is the first time anyone ASKS how wide the content is, and therefore the first time the answer can matter. A List(1:100_000) that nobody scrolls pays for nothing, and the constructor stays O(1) over an aliased Vector.\n\nisempty(items) is scanned BY CONSTRUCTION and is not a special case: 0 IS the exact maximum over no rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.List"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI._lst_reclamp!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"_lst_reclamp!(w::List)\n\n\nRe-clamp the stored offset to the CURRENT extent. Called by the two functions that can SHRINK it – refresh_extent! and refresh_rows! – and by nothing else, because scroll_to! reads content_extent and that is a cost the frame path must not pay for a no-op. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._lst_reclamp!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI._lst_scan!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"_lst_scan!(w::List) -> Int64\n\n\nMeasure every item and RAISE widest to fit, once. Returns the mark. O(items) and one formatter call per item on the MISS; O(1), zero allocation, one Bool test on the HIT.\n\nmax, not assignment: this only ever RAISES, so it cannot strand a stored offset past the end and MUST NOT re-clamp – which is what makes it safe to call from inside scroll_to!'s own content_extent read, where a _lst_reclamp! would recurse. refresh_extent! is the call that can LOWER the mark, and it is the one that re-clamps. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._lst_scan!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI._lst_widen!-Tuple{List, Any}","page":"List","text":"_lst_widen!(w::List, x)\n\n\nRaise the high-water mark to fit x, formatted. O(textwidth(x)), never O(items). `ta_widen!` (textarea.jl:151) is the precedent.\n\nMAINTAINS scanned rather than clearing it, and that is what keeps push_item! O(1) instead of O(items): max over items ∪ {x} IS max(mark, width(x)), so a mark that did not understate items does not understate items ∪ {x} either. Building a list by push_item! therefore stays O(n) overall, never O(n^2). Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._lst_widen!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI._tc_extent_width-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"_tc_extent_width(w::List) -> Int64\n\n\nw.widest, MEMOIZED on scanned: O(1) and ZERO allocation on a hit, one O(items) scan on the miss. A List has no columns, so there is no grid to resolve and _tc_extent_width's default would not find one – this is the List half of that per-widget branch.\n\nTHE MARK IS MEASURED FROM THE DATA AND NEVER FROM THE PAINT, and that is not a refinement – it is the difference between a working axis and a dead one. A mark raised only by render! from what it PAINTS cannot exceed scroll.x + width, because _tc_paint_slice! reports a CUT row as exactly that; and _tc_scroll_x! moves through scroll_to!, clamped to max_scroll.x == widest - width. The two together are a FIXPOINT:\n\nwidest <= scroll.x + width   AND   scroll.x <= widest - width\n=>  scroll.x <= scroll.x\n\n– the mark pins at the viewport width, max_scroll.x pins at ZERO, and every cell past the first screenful is unreachable FOREVER. The mark cannot bootstrap, because scrolling is the only thing that would raise it and the mark is the only thing that permits scrolling. This is the anti-pattern the architect's contract names for the GRID at _tc_resolve! – \"a widths field that only render! writes, so max_scroll(t).x == 0 – 'cannot scroll' when it can – until the first frame\" – and it is fatal here rather than merely late, because a List's extent has no columns to resolve it into shape.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THAT MATTERS, and the reason TextArea never had this bug: an OVER-estimate is self-correcting (you can always scroll, and refresh_extent! shrinks it), an UNDER-estimate is SELF-LOCKING. This is why scanned means \"not an under-estimate\" rather than \"exact\": the monotone over-report delete_item! leaves is safe and stays documented.\n\nKEYED ON THE DATA, NOT ON version – deliberately, and this is where it departs from _tc_resolve!'s (version, avail): version bumps on every ARROW KEY (_tc_touch!), so keying on it would rescan 100 000 rows per keystroke. _lst_widen! MAINTAINS the mark across push_item! and insert_item! instead – max over items ∪ {x} IS max(mark, width(x)) – so only the two BULK ops invalidate. Internal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI._tc_extent_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.content_extent-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"content_extent(w::List) -> Size\n\n\nSize(widest, length(items)). OVERRIDES the container default (scroll.jl:84): a List's content is DATA, not children, so the bounding box of its (nonexistent) children is not its extent. THIS OVERRIDE IS THE WHOLE INTEGRATION WITH Scrollbar – Scrollbar{List{T,F,A}} works with ZERO new code in scroll.jl, because the scrollable seam is three functions and not a type.\n\n_tc_header_rows(::List) == 0, so the + hh of _tc_extent degenerates and this is Size(widest, n) exactly.\n\nO(1) AND ZERO ALLOCATION ON EVERY CALL BUT THE FIRST, which is the bar that matters: THIS FUNCTION IS ON THE FRAME PATH – _sb_metrics, max_scroll, scroll_to! and Scrollpane.render! all reach it, several times per frame (scroll.jl:100,115,413,626). length on a Vector is O(1) and widest is a MEMOIZED mark. Writing maximum(text_width, items) here – on every call, unmemoized – would make every frame O(rows) and IS FORBIDDEN.\n\nTHE FIRST call after a bulk data change pays ONE O(items) scan (_lst_scan!), stated rather than hidden. There is no cheaper honest answer: the widest of n rows is not knowable without looking at n rows, and the alternative – inferring the mark from what render! painted – is the fixpoint _tc_extent_width documents, which does not cost O(rows) only because it never works at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.content_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.delete_item!-Tuple{List, Int64}","page":"List","text":"delete_item!(w::List, i::Int64) -> Bool\n\n\nDelete row i. False when out of range. REINDEXES via reindex_delete!: i leaves the selection and the cursor lands on the row that took its place, or on the new last row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.delete_item!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.insert_item!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{List{T}, Int64, T}} where T","page":"List","text":"insert_item!(w::List{T}, i::Int64, x)\n\n\nInsert x at i, clamped. REINDEXES the selection via reindex_insert!: every selected row at or above i moves up one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.insert_item!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.measure-Tuple{List, Size}","page":"List","text":"measure(w::List, avail::Size) -> Size\n\n\navail. TextArea's argument verbatim (textarea.jl:164): a List takes the space it is OFFERED and scrolls its content, because an auto-HEIGHT List would be as tall as its data and would never scroll at all. Give it height: 10 or a grow: 1 parent. This is also what licenses version's PAINT reactivity. Pure w.r.t. the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.on_blur!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"on_blur!(w::List)\n\n\nHide the cursor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_blur!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{List, Dispatch{KeyEvent}}","page":"List","text":"on_event!(w::List, d::Dispatch{KeyEvent})\n\n\nKeys: see _tc_key!. Consumes only when something actually moved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.on_event!-Tuple{List, Dispatch{MouseEvent}}","page":"List","text":"on_event!(w::List, d::Dispatch{MouseEvent})\n\n\nMouse: see _tc_mouse!. Consumes only when something actually changed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_event!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.on_focus!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"on_focus!(w::List)\n\n\nShow the cursor, and scroll every ancestor pane until this list is visible. reveal! is called EXPLICITLY because overriding on_focus! REPLACES the default that would have called it (widget.jl:666).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.on_focus!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.push_item!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{List{T}, T}} where T","page":"List","text":"push_item!(w::List{T}, x)\n\n\nAppend x. O(1) plus an O(text_width(x)) mark raise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.push_item!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.refresh_extent!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"refresh_extent!(w::List) -> Size\n\n\nRecompute widest from scratch and return the new content_extent. O(items), and it CALLS THE FORMATTER ONCE PER ITEM.\n\nwidest is a MONOTONE HIGH-WATER MARK: render! raises it from the width _tc_paint_slice! returns, in O(1) per painted row, and nothing lowers it. THE SAME NAME, THE SAME CONTRACT and the same return type as refresh_extent!(::TextArea) (textarea.jl:183) – one name, one meaning, four widgets, and no new export.\n\nTHE TRADEOFF, stated so nobody discovers it, and it is now the SAME as TextArea's rather than its mirror image: both start EXACT and drift TOO WIDE as you delete, and this is the call that makes them exact again. A List that started TOO NARROW would not be an optimistic thumb, it would be a DEAD AXIS – see _tc_extent_width for the fixpoint. The VERTICAL axis – the one a list is actually about – is EXACT and free either way, because it is length(items).\n\nTHIS IS NO LONGER LOAD-BEARING FOR CORRECTNESS, and that is the point: _lst_scan! reaches the same mark automatically, memoized, at the first query. What is left here is the one thing a monotone mark cannot do alone – LOWER itself after a delete – which is a thumb that reads a little small, never a cell you cannot reach.\n\nRe-clamps the stored offset afterwards, because this is the only call that can SHRINK the extent and strand an offset past the end.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_extent!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.refresh_rows!-Tuple{List}","page":"List","text":"refresh_rows!(w::List)\n\n\nBump version, re-sync the selection, re-clamp the scroll, follow the cursor. THE single exit of every data change, so none of the four can be forgotten at a call site – _ta_edited!'s pattern (textarea.jl:461). ALSO the public escape hatch for \"I mutated items myself\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.refresh_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/list/#ManyUI.set_items!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{List{T}, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"List","text":"set_items!(w::List{T}, xs::AbstractVector)\n\n\nReplace the contents. CLEARS the selection and cursor and rewinds the scroll: every index the selection held names a row that may no longer exist, and silently keeping them would select the WRONG ROWS. set_text!(::TextArea) makes the same choice with the caret.\n\nThe mark is INVALIDATED for the same reason and by the same precedent: set_text! calls refresh_extent!, because a mark measured over data that no longer exists is not a high-water mark, it is a stale number. Dropping it to ZERO and clearing scanned is the O(1) half of that – _lst_scan! pays the rescan at the next query, and only if there IS one – and it leaves EXACTLY the state the constructor leaves, which is what \"replace the contents\" should mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ManyUI.set_items!"}]
}
